"arcade_virtua-fighter-2","Virtua Fighter 2","arcade","Arcade","Arcade","Ýmsir (Namco, Sega, Capcom, Konami, Taito, SNK, Atari, o.fl.)","Arcade (Öll tímabil)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","SEGA","SEGA","Fighting / 3D","Fighting","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Virtua Fighter 2 is a fighting game developed by Sega. It is the sequel to Virtua Fighter and the second game in the Virtua Fighter series. It was created by the Sega's Yu Suzuki-headed AM2 and was released in the arcade in 1994. VF2 was known for breakthrough graphics at the time. It used Sega's Model 2 arcade hardware to run the game at 60 frames per second at a high resolution with no slowdown.

The arena size could be adjusted up to a very small platform or all the way to 82 meters, which in the genre is considered very large; this is the only game in the seriesâ€”other than Virtua Fighter Remixâ€”that could have such size adjustments. The physical energy meter could also be adjusted to infinity as well, giving you the advantage when beating opponents in the game or practicing moves against the computer player. Incidentally, players discovered that adjusting the arena to a smaller size and giving the characters infinite health could lead to mock sumo matches, wherein victory is achieved by knocking the other player out of the ring.","vf2.zip","ZIP","19.02","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/arcade/vf2.png","miximages/arcade/vf2.png","screenshots/arcade/vf2.png","titlescreens/arcade/vf2.png","3dboxes/arcade/vf2.png","backcovers/arcade/vf2.png","marquees/arcade/vf2.png"
