"nes_dandy-zeuon-no-fukkatsu","Dandy - Zeuon No Fukkatsu","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-10-21","1988","1980s","Atelier Double","Pony Canyon","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","A conversion port of the Atari 8-Bit computer game Dandy. Though it has been changed quite a bit for it's Japanese audience and doesn't resemble the original game very much at all.The view is overhead and much like Zelda you walk around and explore while using your sword to defeat various enemies. There are various dungeons and villages you can visit and the game even has a day/night cycle.","Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","miximages/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","screenshots/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","titlescreens/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","3dboxes/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","backcovers/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png","marquees/nes/Dandy Zeuon No Fukkatsu (English Translated, Rev 1.2, by Super Roboprotector).png"
"nes_dead-zone","Dead Zone","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-11-20","1986","1980s","Sunsoft","Sunsoft","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","In the space calendar 0385, the Earth suffers from overpopulation and humans started to build space colonies. Kirk, the protagonist of Dead Zone, works for the Earth federation's space development office. He is a brilliant engineer in space physics and he is working for Sirius third planet colony. He also got engaged with his beloved girlfriend Marry, but they had to postpone their wedding. Five months later, Kirk, accompanied with his robot Carry, decides to meet Marry at last, and prepare the ceremony. But when he arrives at the space station, everything is quiet. Then a beam of light hits him. He wakes up sometimes later, inside a sort of underground graveyard, pieces of dismantled robots surrounding him.

The player proceeds through the game by choosing from a selection of text menu commands. Like any traditional digital comic/text adventure games, a large view shows the surroundings. The bottom of the screen proposes simple action commands, like see, take or push; however, the game is entirely in Japanese (although it uses katakana exclusively). One of the original features of the game is to use real sampled sound in some places, which is very unusual for the time.","Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","miximages/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","screenshots/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","titlescreens/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","3dboxes/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","backcovers/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png","marquees/nes/Dead Zone (English Translated by Stardust Crusaders).png"
"nes_madou-senki-deep-dungeon","Madou Senki - Deep Dungeon","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-12-19","1986","1980s","Hummingbird Soft","Disk Original Group (DOG)","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","","","The town of Dorl has an infestation of demons who currently reside in the castles dungeon after a portal to the underworld was opened. The emperor of the underworld, Emperor Ruu, commanded his demons to raid the town to steal its treasure and during this ransacking the demons also managed to take Princess Etna's soul away too, leaving her in a comatose state. Many brave adventurers have entered the underground dungeon to try and reclaim her soul from this demon king, but none have returned successfully yet. So now it's up to the player to succeed where others have failed and put an end to the Emperor Ruu and also recover Princess Etna's soul.","Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","miximages/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","screenshots/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","titlescreens/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","3dboxes/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","backcovers/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","marquees/nes/Deep Dungeon - Madou Senki (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png"
"nes_deep-dungeon-2-yuushi-no-monshou","Deep Dungeon 2 - Yuushi No Monshou","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-05-29","1987","1980s","Hummingbird Soft","Disk Original Group (DOG)","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","","","In the time the last game, strange things have happened at the town of Dorl. A proclamation was made that Ruu has been resurrected. The people gathered in the temple to pray, until the swordsman Ral had been summoned by the heavens to enter the castle and fight Ruu.

Like it's prequel the game takes place in a 3D dungeon crawling environment only this time there is a tower to conquer as well as a dungeon. Battles also happen in real-time so if you idle too long you will quickly be attacked. However once you reach certain levels enemies will no longer randomly attack you.","Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","miximages/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","screenshots/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","titlescreens/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","3dboxes/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","backcovers/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","marquees/nes/Deep Dungeon - Yuushi no Monshou (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png"
"nes_dig-dug","Dig Dug","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1985-06-04","1985","1980s","Namco","Namcot","Action / Labyrinth","Action","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Dig Dug is a 1-2 player arcade game in which you have to use your shovel to dig your way through the earth. Stopping you from doing this are two monsters, called Pooka and Fygar, who will continually chase you around. The only weapon that you carry is an air pump, which you can use to inflate the monsters to the point where they explode. (if you start to inflate them but stop doing so, the monsters will get turned back to their normal selves.) Furthermore, rocks are scattered throughout the earth, and you can use these rocks to squash them. If the monsters do not find you for several seconds, they will eventually get turned into ghosts, which are able to walk through the earth. They are invincible and cannot be killed. From time to time, vegetables will appear in the center, and you can get these for points.","Dig Dug (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","miximages/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png","marquees/nes/Dig Dug (JP).png"
"nes_dig-dug-ii-trouble-in-paradise","Dig Dug II : Trouble in Paradise","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1989-12-01","1989","1980s","Namco","Bandai","Action / Labyrinth","Action","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","It is Dig Dug's job to protect 72 different tropical islands from his arch rivals, Pooka and the fire breathing Fygar. Played from an overhead point of view, Dig Dug now has two weapons at his disposal; the first is an air pump and the second is a drill. The pump can be used to inflate and destroy the enemies the same as in the original Dig Dug. At various locations on the islands are stakes, and when the drill is used near a stake a crack will form. If enough cracks become connected that section of the island will fall into the ocean, hopefully taking an enemy (and not Dig Dug!) with it. When all of the Pookas and Fygars have been destroyed, the game moves on to the next, more difficult level.","Dig Dug II (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","miximages/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png","marquees/nes/Dig Dug II (JP).png"
"nes_dirty-pair-project-eden","Dirty Pair - Project Eden","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-03-28","1987","1980s","Daiei Seisakusho","Bandai","Action / Adventure","Adventure","1-2","1","2","","","Kei and Yuri, two cute space investigators, are the heroines of this side-scrolling action game by Bandai and based on the popular animation series Dirty Pair. This was released along with the movie Project Eden though it doesn't follow the plot of the actual movie all that well. Also the game is known for it's incredible difficulty.","Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","miximages/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","screenshots/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","titlescreens/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","3dboxes/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","backcovers/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","marquees/nes/Dirty Pair - Project Eden (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png"
"nes_donkey-kong","Donkey Kong","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-10-15","1986","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","60","3","Can you save Mario's girlfriend from Donkey Kong's clutches? You must help Mario free Pauline before it's too late. You'll have to dodge booby-trapped barrels, fireballs and anything else Donkey Kong throws at you to try and stop you. So if you're looking for action, stop the antics!","Donkey Kong (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","miximages/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png","marquees/nes/Donkey Kong (JP).png"
"nes_donkey-kong-jr","Donkey Kong Jr.","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-06-15","1987","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Mario as a villain, who would have thought it? But this is the unlikely scenario of Donkey Kong Jr. on NES. Yes, the mustachioed villain has locked Donkey Kong in a cage and it's up to his offspring to get him out of the clutches of this devious plumber. Our friendly primate will have to climb along lianas to reach the top of the board, while avoiding the various objects that Mario sees fit to throw at his face.","Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","miximages/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png","marquees/nes/Donkey Kong Jr. (JP).png"
"nes_doremikko","Doremikko","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-12-04","1987","1980s","Konami","Konami","Music and Dancing","Action","1","1","1","","","Doremikko  is a music game developed by Konami for the Famicom Disk System and published in late 1987. Like a few other music games of its kind, it comes complete with a digital keyboard which connects to the Famicom for the purpose of composing and playing music. The inclusion of a large keyboard with the game makes Doremikko the most expensive Famicom Disk System title at the time of its original sale.The title of the game is often mistranslated as ""Dreamiko"", but it is intended to be a play on the names for the first three notes of a scale; Do, Re, Mi. Having been developed by Konami, the game actually features a medly of Gradius music, and contains a cameo of the Vic Viper on the menu screen. It was only release in Japan, and never translated into any other language.","Doremikko (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","miximages/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Doremikko (JP).png","marquees/nes/Doremikko (JP).png"
"nes_dr-chaos-jigoku-no-tobira","Dr. Chaos - Jigoku No Tobira","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-06-18","1987","1980s","Marionette","Pony Canyon","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","Dr. Chaos is a side-scrolling action adventure developped by Marionette and published by Pony Canyon. The genius scientist Dr Ginn Chaos has gone missing. Before his disappearance, he was actively working in his large mansion on a device allowing access to alternate dimensions. Armed only with a knife, his brother decides to look for him and ultimately unveil the mystery behind the incident. Something obviously went terribly wrong with Dr. Chaos's experiment, and the goal of the game is to locate all the eleven dimensional warp-zones hidden within the mansion, as well as assembling a powerful laser gun, the only weapon capable of taking down the last boss.","Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","miximages/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png","marquees/nes/Dr. Chaos - Jigoku no Tobira (JP).png"
"nes_druid-kyoufu-no-tobira","Druid - Kyoufu No Tobira","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-03-03","1988","1980s","Jaleco","Jaleco","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","Druid is an action game inspired by the arcade hit Gauntlet. As the eponymous hero, your job is to destroy four magic skulls hidden over eight levels.The game is presented in a top-down view. The druid walks around the terrain seeking out the staircase that leads to the next level. On the way, he is harassed by hordes of monsters that come out of every direction and attack him, decreasing his health. To defend himself, the druid can use the spells of water, fire or electricity to toss projectiles. However, these magical weapons have limited ammo.","Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","miximages/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","screenshots/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","titlescreens/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","3dboxes/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","backcovers/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","marquees/nes/Druid - Kyoufu no Tobira (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png"
"nes_eggerland","Eggerland","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-01-29","1987","1980s","HAL Laboratory","HAL Laboratory","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","The Eggerland series is better known outside of Japan as The Adventure of Lolo series.The game is often referred to as ""Syodai"", or the ""first one"". The game is a remake of ""Meikyuu Shinwa"" (Eggerland 2; lit. ""Labyrinth Myth"") for the MSX2 Home Computer System with updated graphics and different music. Furthermore, in this remake the location of some special rooms was swapped, and a final boss battle was introduced.In this Eggerland, Lolo must navigate his way through a non-linear series of rooms, the majority of which occupy a 10x10 arrangement along the main floor of a castle. Many rooms have more than one exit for Lolo to choose from, and a map can be collected along the way to provide the player with information about where he or she is, and what rooms have already been visited. Secret entrances to hidden chambers must be found so that keys can be collected, enabling the player to explore deeper into the castle.","Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","miximages/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","screenshots/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","titlescreens/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","3dboxes/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","backcovers/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png","marquees/nes/Eggerland (English Translated by Necrosaro).png"
"nes_eggerland-souzou-heno-tabidachi","Eggerland - Souzou Heno Tabidachi","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-10-18","1988","1980s","HAL Laboratory","HAL Laboratory","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Souzou he no Tabidachi translates to ""Departure to Creation."" Like with ""Revival of the Labyrinth"" before, this is a fitting description of the game itself. It references the fact that it brings the Creation Mode from the original back to the series, after it was abandoned in the previous two installments. It further resembles the first title in that it's also one of the most basic entries in the franchise, and among the easiest of the Japanese releases. It's also tied for the shortest game in the entire series, with just fifty puzzles in all. Given that the predecessors all had a hundred or more, this might seems like a problem, but the caliber of the puzzles is as high as ever. So if you found the prequels to be a bit overwhelming, then this might be the best of the early titles to start with. Interestingly, this seems to have been one of the FDS titles that were available solely from Disk Writer machines, as it doesn't appear to have ever been released at retail.","Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","miximages/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png","marquees/nes/Eggerland - Souzou e no Tabidachi (JP).png"
"nes_electrician","Electrician","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-12-26","1986","1980s","Synapse Software","Kemco","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","The player character is a young electrician called Richard Light. He must install electrical wiring in apartment buildings in various American cities. In each building, Richard navigates service corridors to link the electrical wires between each room, As he does so, animals threaten to chew through the wires. When an electrical connection is complete, the live wires illuminate the connected rooms and electrocute the ""enemy"" animals on contact. If Richard touches any of the rats, bats, or giant insects in the corridor, he loses a life. In the buildings, the player earns points by illuminating rooms and electrocuting any rats, bats, and insects. Occasionally, a burglar appears; intercepting him rewards the player with bonus points and a bell, which is worth a 1-up.","Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","miximages/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","screenshots/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","titlescreens/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","3dboxes/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","backcovers/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png","marquees/nes/Electrician (English Translated by KingMike's Translations).png"
"nes_emi-chan-no-moero-yakyuuken","Emi-chan No Moero Yakyuuken!","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-10-01","1987","1980s","Super Pig","New System House Oh!","Adults","Action","1","1","1","","","It's a strip janken game. Janken's just a fancy Japanese word for rock-paper-scissors I think, but janken's easier to type. The whole ?strip? part of the game is, for every match you lose, you take off an article of clothing. Woo! For every match you win, Emi (the girl you play against) takes off an article of clothing. WOO! For every match you tie, Emi puts back on an article of clothing. WHAT?! Yeah, it's a pirate game, no one said it had to be fair.

What's even more unfair is, once you get Emi down to like, four articles of clothing, you have to win three times against various barnyard animals. Win three times against a barnyard animal, win against Emi, she loses another article of clothing. Win three MORE times against another barnyard animal, win against Emi, she loses another article of clothing. So yeah, the match is pretty easily stacked in her favor, wouldn't you say? Too bad you don't have any mammals lying around to protect YOU in your strip janken game.

So yeah, just use up and down to cycle through the different hands, and hit A to choose what you want. Yeah, really complicated.","Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","miximages/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","screenshots/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","titlescreens/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","3dboxes/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","backcovers/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png","marquees/nes/Emi-chan no Moero Yakyuuken! (English Translated, Rev 0.99, by Spinner 8 and Friends).png"
