"nes_famimaga-disk-vol-4-clox","Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1991-04-19","1991","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Clox, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 Clox, is a falling blocks puzzle game that is superficially similar to Tetris or Columns. Single clock faces depicting one of four angles fall from the top and the player needs to manipulate and place them so they can form larger objects, such as simple formations like squares, diamonds and hourglass shapes to more object-intensive shapes like octagons and hexagons.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png","miximages/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png","","marquees/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (JP).png"
"nes_famimaga-disk-vol-5-puyo-puyo","Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1991-10-25","1991","1990s","Compile","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle / Fall","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","","","Puyo Puyo is the first entry of Compile's Puyo Puyo series. It was released on the MSX and Famicom Disk System on October 25, 1991; the FDS version was published by Tokuma Shoten as part of their ""Famimaga Disk"" line of games. The FDS version would later be released on a Famicom cart in 1993. Puyo Puyo would be succeeded by a much more popular arcade game of the same name.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png","miximages/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png","","marquees/nes/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (JP).png"
"nes_final-commando-akai-yousai","Final Commando - Akai Yousai","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-05-02","1988","1980s","Konami","Konami","Shooter","Shooter","1-2","1","2","","","Your brothers-in-arms are hostages behind enemy lines, and you're their only hope for freedom. But the firepower you'll face to rescue them is awesome. Cannons, tanks, submarines and snipers will blast you with horrific crossfire, while jet fighters zero in from above. To defend yourself, you control the army's advanced all-terrain attack jeep, with its arsenal of guided missiles and incendiary grenades. Of course these are merely tools, and to save your countrymen you'll need more than a handful of gunpowder. You'll need a pocketful of miracles, and the ferocity of a wild Jackal!","Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","miximages/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png","marquees/nes/Final Command - Akai Yousai (JP).png"
"nes_fire-bam","Fire Bam","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-02-01","1988","1980s","HAL Laboratory","HAL Laboratory","Action RPG","RPG","1","1","1","","","Bam is a fifteen years old courageous boy. Like every boy his age, Bam must pass the village's test and bring back an insect's shell from the forest. But as he returns, and to his surprise, most of the village has been destroyed and his parents turned into demons. Armed with his sword, Bam sets forth in search of what happened to the village and his parents.","Fire Bam (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","miximages/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png","marquees/nes/Fire Bam (JP).png"
"nes_fire-rock","Fire Rock","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-01-01","1988","1980s","System Sacom","Use Corporation","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","Fire Rock is an action game developed for the Famicom Disk System by System Sacom, and published in Japan by Use in 1988. The design of the game is credited to Mark Flint. In the game, the player controls what appears to be a cave man, whose goal is to navigate a series of item filled caverns in an effort to locate the boss of the level, defeat it, and then locate the door which leads to the next level. The game is commonly sited as having very poor controls, often requiring the player to grab hold of a wall, and very slowly climb it by holding up and tapping jump repeatedly. Other issues include a very low jump that places more platforms and items out of reach, and a difficult time bringing the character back under control if he starts to slip. This game was not released outside of Japan.","Fire Rock (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","miximages/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png","marquees/nes/Fire Rock (JP).png"
"nes_fuuun-shourin-ken","Fuuun Shourin Ken","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-04-17","1987","1980s","Jaleco","Jaleco","Fighting","Fighting","1","1","1","","","In Fuuun Shourin Ken, you control a rising martial artist who strives to defeat other great fighters. To do so, you must utilize a wide array of attacks to defeat your opponent before they knock you out first. You will compete in six different scenes against 13 different opponents. The game follows in the vein of Yie Ar Kung-Fu, and predates the original Street Fighter by only a couple of months. It features similar concepts such as a distinction between punch and kick attacks, relative directions, and a health bar for each fighter, depicted as a series of paper fans.","Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","miximages/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png","marquees/nes/Fuuun Shourin Ken (JP).png"
"nes_galaga","Galaga","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-01-01","1988","1980s","Namco","Bandai","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1-2","1","2","80","4","Galaga is a vertical arcade shooter from Namco.  You control a space fighter at the bottom of the screen, scrolling left and right in order to avoid oncoming bombs and kamikaze attacks from alien invaders--and to fire your own missiles to fight back!

There are an infinite number of stages to progress through.  Every third level is a ""challenge stage"" in which you have a free chance to shoot as many aliens as possible and rack up bonus points.

Certain aliens have tractor beams they can use to capture one of your ships.  If that wasn't your last ""life,"" you can try to shoot that alien down and recapture your ship, giving you two ships at once and doubling your firepower.

A summary screen appears when the game is over, giving you both your final score and you final ""hit ratio""--the number of missiles fired, enemies destroyed, and the percentage of targets hit.","Galaga (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Galaga (JP).png","miximages/nes/Galaga (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Galaga (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Galaga (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Galaga (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Galaga (JP).png","marquees/nes/Galaga (JP).png"
"nes_galaxian","Galaxian","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1984-09-07","1984","1980s","Namco","Namcot","Shooter / Space Invaders Like","Shooter","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Galaxian is a shoot 'em up in which the player is at the bottom of the screen, with an arrangement of aliens at the top. The player moves left and right to aim at an alien, then shoots a bullet up the screen, and the alien it hits is killed. The aliens are classed as Drones, Emissaries, Hornets and Commanders, with those higher up the screen scoring more points when destroyed. At set intervals an enemy will move down the screen towards you, escorting a bomber, which is a moment of high danger. Clear a wave and another is generated.","Galaxian (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","miximages/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Galaxian (JP).png","marquees/nes/Galaxian (JP).png"
"nes_gall-force-eternal-story","Gall Force - Eternal Story","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-11-19","1986","1980s","HAL Laboratory","HAL Laboratory","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Based upon the anime of the same name, Gall Force is a group of science fiction anime OAV series by the studio Artmic, with production by Youmex and AIC. The original character designs were by Kenichi Sonoda. In the original story line, an all-out, prehistoric galactic war rages between the all-female Solnoids and the amorphous Paranoids for too long. In the midst of battle, a plan which was secretly hatched by the leaders of both sides comes into fruition, and an unsuspecting crew of seven Solnoid soldiers aboard the Kulanis-class Cruiser named the Star Leaf is caught up in the middle.

The Famicom Disk System game is a top down shooter and follows a different story than that of the OAVs. It features Rabby flying across Terra, and catapulting into six different regions of space in order to rescue each of her crew members one at a time. With each crew member that you rescue, you gain access to a new configuration for your ship.","Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","miximages/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","screenshots/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","titlescreens/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","3dboxes/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","backcovers/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png","marquees/nes/Gall Force - Eternal Story (English Translated by Gil Galad).png"
"nes_ginga-denshou-galaxy-odyssey","Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-11-06","1986","1980s","Imagineer","Imagineer","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey is a game developed for the Family Computer Disk System by Wave Jack, and published by Imagineer in 1986. Unlike most games for the FDS, Galaxy Odyssey did not come with just a disk and an instruction booklet. Among the contents found in the large box, were an audio cassette tape and a book recounting the game's story.The game itself is quite unusual as well. It features a blend of vertical shooting gameplay mixed with more Zelda-like exploration sequences. As the pilot of a ship, you could dock with certain dungeon entrances, and take a shuttle down to the ground to explore and search for items. Searching around isn't easy at first, since you run out of oxygen fairly quickly, and that starts to drain your life, of which you start out with three bars.To win the game, you must find the medicine needed to rescue the people who are suffering from a disease in your home area. To do so, you must collect warp codes, and translate alien codes, and blast off from one dungeon location to travel to another. The game's text is in Japanese, and has never been translated to another language.","Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","miximages/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","screenshots/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","titlescreens/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","3dboxes/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","backcovers/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png","marquees/nes/Ginga Denshou - Galaxy Odyssey (English Translated by Chris M. Covell).png"
"nes_golf","Golf","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-11-15","1986","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Sports / Golf","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Golf features two challenging 18 hole courses where players are able to play a round of golf, and allows players to choose a club, adjust stance and control the swing. The game features three alternating views; an overhead long-range view of the entire hole, an overhead medium-range view from the ball's current location, and an overhead close-range view of the green. Gameplay modes include 1 player, or 2 player play with a link cable.","Golf (JP, Rev 2).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","miximages/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","screenshots/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","titlescreens/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","3dboxes/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","backcovers/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png","marquees/nes/Golf (JP, Rev 2).png"
"nes_golf-japan-course","Golf - Japan Course","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-02-21","1987","1980s","HAL Laboratory","Nintendo","Sports / Golf","Sports","1-2","1","2","","","An update of sorts to Golf. Players get to take control of Nintendo's mascots Mario and Luigi (in the 2 player game) and play over 18 holes of golf on courses modeled after real courses in Japan.

Players can choose to either try their skills in match play or stroke play. Match play involves two golfers trying to win the most holes. They win holes by getting the better score than their competitor. Stroke play differs as it just involves the golfer trying to get the best score possible by completing each hole in as few strokes as possible.

When on the golf course, players can choose the club they want to use, adjust the speed and strength (from a 1 to 3 scale) of their swing, and aim in the direction they want to hit the ball. Hitting the ball involves the use of a meter with an icon that goes back and forth, which gauges the distance the ball is hit. Players can take a step back from the tee and take practice swings so they can get into a rhythm using this meter.

This game also contained a now defunct feature where players could record their personal information and their best scores and then bring the game disk to a Disk Fax machine. Inserting the disk into this machine would fax over the player's information to Nintendo's headquarters. This information was used for a contest. Being selected a contest winner would award the player with a golden colored disk version of this game. This golden disk version of the game contained an exclusive course that was not present on the original version of this game.","Golf - Japan Course (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","miximages/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png","marquees/nes/Golf - Japan Course (JP).png"
