"nes_goonies-the","Goonies, The","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-02-21","1986","1980s","Konami","Konami","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","The Goonies is based on a movie of the same name from 1985. A group of youngsters from a neighborhood called the ""Goon Docks"" are trying to find the lost treasure of One-Eyed Willie to prevent the foreclosure of their houses. Unfortunately, the treasure is buried under the hide-out of the infamous Fratelli gang, a family of ruthless criminals who are on the run from the law. You play as the asthmatic Mikey, who must find the treasure and save the other Goonies before time runs out.

The game consists of six levels. In each level, you must collect three keys to advance to the next area. These can be found by blowing open doors with bombs that are dropped by enemies. Also hidden behind doors are power-ups and other Goonies to rescue. Trying to stop you are enemies such as rats, skeletons, and even members of the Fratelli Gang. 

Unlike the sequel, the Famicom version was never released outside of Japan.","Goonies (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Goonies (JP).png","miximages/nes/Goonies (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Goonies (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Goonies (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Goonies (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Goonies (JP).png","marquees/nes/Goonies (JP).png"
"nes_green-beret","Green Beret","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-04-10","1987","1980s","Konami","Konami","Platform / Shooter Scrolling","Shooter","1-2","1","2","","","Based on the arcade game of the same name, the player's objective in the FDS version was changed from rescuing prisoners to destroying a secret weapon being developed in the enemy's headquarters. Additionally, a 2-players co-operative mode was introduced as well, allowing two players to play simultaneously (with Player 1 in blue and Player 2 in red). The play mechanics are essentially identical to the arcade version, however the Flamethrower is removed (only the Rocket Launcher and Grenades remained) and two new power-up items are introduced: a Star mark which grants invincibility and a pistol with unlimited ammo, both which are only usable for a limited period. The FDS version also features two additional stages that were not in the arcade game: an airport set between the Missile Base and the Harbor, where the player faces a group of rocket soldiers at the end; and a new final stage set inside the enemy's base in which the player must disarm a nuclear missile at the end that is about to be launched; by using rocket launchers dropped by enemy soldiers. The flamethrower corps at the end of the Warehouse stage was replaced by a paratrooper unit.","Green Beret (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","miximages/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Green Beret (JP).png","marquees/nes/Green Beret (JP).png"
"nes_gun-smoke","Gun.Smoke","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1989-02-23","1989","1980s","Capcom","Capcom","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1","1","1","70","3.5","Gun.Smoke is a top-down scrolling shooter that casts the gamer as a lone gunman, out to save the gold rush town of Hicksville from a fearsome group of bandits known as the Wingates. Each level has a particular target enemy, although his or her henchmen must also be taken out.

Although it largely takes place on foot, the gameplay resembles Commando, with its top-down action. The screen scrolls at a constant rate, and enemies come at you from the edges, to be shot or avoided. You can grab various power-ups along the way. Finding a horse will allow you to get hit twice before you die rather than just once. There are scenes in which you raft up a river and use the tracks of a railway line.

On the NES you can tap the A button to fire up and right, the B button to fire up and left, or A and B simultaneously to fire straight ahead. One-button control systems mimic all this.","Gun.Smoke (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","miximages/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png","marquees/nes/Gun.Smoke (JP).png"
"nes_gyruss","Gyruss","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1989-02-01","1989","1980s","Konami","Ultra Games","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","70","3.5","The entire solar system is being attacked by the vicious Gyrusians, and it's up to you to save the day! You will need to fly your spaceship to each of the nine planets and then clear three stages of enemies to rid that planet of the Gyrusians. You start the game with a single canon, and can gain a double canon after shooting a power up satelite. After clearing each planet, there is another bonus stage where additional weapons and bonus points can be collected. Gyruss is an arcade action shooter, however instead of piloting your spaceship horizontally or vertically, you move in a circle around the perimeter of the screen. The NES version of Gyruss adds to the original arcade version additional music, additional weapons, new enemies, and a large boss at the end of each level.","Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","miximages/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","screenshots/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","titlescreens/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","3dboxes/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","backcovers/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png","marquees/nes/Gyruss (English Translated, Rev 1.01, by Stardust Crusaders).png"
"nes_halley-wars","Halley Wars","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1989-01-14","1989","1980s","ITL","Taito","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","","","It’s the middle of the 21st century. The armies of Lord Halley are launching attacks on planet Earth. You are the last chance for Earth, piloting a craft specially developed to engage these hordes. You must fight your way through their defenses to the final goal, the alien base. The final battle is about to commence!

Halley Wars is a vertical scrolling shooter. The game has 6 stages set in locations in space such as, above planets or in asteroid fields. There are end of levels bosses at the end of each stage each with their own weaknesses. There are power-ups, which can be picked up along the way when you shoot particular asteroids or satellites. These upgrade your weapon strength, speed, add explosive pods and shields.

Where the game stood out was, there are comets that need to be destroyed on your journey. Each comet and ship that you don’t shoot makes it to earth and does some damage there. Comets do more damage than the enemy ships do. If Earths damage level reaches 100% the game is over. For every 2000 points you score, 1% of Earths damage is repaired.","Halley Wars (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","miximages/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png","marquees/nes/Halley Wars (JP).png"
"nes_hao-kun-no-fushigi-na-tabi","Hao-kun No Fushigi Na Tabi","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-05-11","1987","1980s","Carry Soft","Disk Original Group (DOG)","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","As my apprentice, you have been studying a long time to master the secrets of magic. Now the time has come to prove yourself. You must journey to the four Mystery Castles in search of four magic talismans. You will face many perils along the way: the castles are riddled with underground passages and intricate mazes, where you may become lost forever! And strange creatures prowl the castles, ready to pounce upon you when you least expect it! But you will find magic powers hidden along the way, to help you in this Mysterious Journey. The magic talismans you seek are the greatest gifts known to man: wealth, wisdom, happiness, and peace. Go now, complete your quest, and prove that you are as great a wizard as I!","Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","miximages/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png","marquees/nes/Hao-kun no Fushigi na Tabi (JP).png"
"nes_date-de-blackjack","Date De Blackjack","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1990-05-18","1990","1990s","Hacker International","Hacker International","Casino / Cards","Board / Casual","1","1","1","","","An adult poker game by Hacker International in which you will play a girl in poker. Upon winning enough you will have interactions with her in which she slowly undresses.","Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","miximages/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png","marquees/nes/Hayama Reiko no Date de Blackjack (JP).png"
"nes_hikari-shinwa-palutena-no-kagami","Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-12-19","1986","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","In a time where humans and gods coexisted in harmony, the kingdom of Angel Land was ruled by two goddesses, Palutena the Goddess of Light and Medusa the Goddess of Darkness. While Palutena administered the light and helped the mortals cultivate their crops, Medusa despised the mortals and used the darkness to destroy their crops and turn the mortals into stone. Enraged, Palutena transformed Medusa into a hideous monster and banished her into the dark Underworld.

But Medusa would not go quietly. She assembled an army of monsters and evil spirits of the underworld to conquer Palutena's home, the Palace in the Sky. War erupted and Medusa's minions overwhelmed Palutena's army, eventually imprisoning the Goddess of Light. Medusa then seized the Three Sacred Treasuresâ€”the Mirror Shield, the Arrow of Light, and the Wings of Pegasusâ€”and gave them to her most powerful minions.

Defeated and imprisoned, Palutena's only hope was to seek the help of Pit, a young angel trapped in the Underworld. Using the last of her strength, she sent Pit a magical bow. Thus, Pit set out on a quest to escape the Underworld, retrieve the Sacred Treasures that would help him defeat Medusa, and rescue Palutena and restore peace to Angel Land.

The original Famicom Disk System versions feature save slots, unlike the North American version which uses a password system (known in-game as ""Sacred Words""). As with Metroid, the FDS version of Kid Icarus also features higher-quality music and sound effects that take advantage of the system's additional sound channels.","Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","miximages/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","screenshots/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","titlescreens/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","3dboxes/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","backcovers/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png","marquees/nes/Hikari Shinwa - Palutena no Kagami (English Translated by BlackPaladin).png"
"nes_ice-climber","Ice Climber","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-09-01","1986","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","60","3","In Ice Climber one or two players need to climb their way to the top of 32 different mountains. Each mountain is broken up into eight levels of platforms along with a bonus stage at the top. Attempting to stop you from making upward progress are different creatures ranging from polar bears to condors as well as treacherous landscapes with falling icicles, moving platforms, and icy floors. To help out, your only weapon is a mallet which can be used to knock out enemies as well as bust away bricks in the platforms to give you some room to jump up to the next level. When you finally reach the top, it's time to move on to the next, more difficult level.","Ice Climber (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","miximages/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png","marquees/nes/Ice Climber (JP).png"
"nes_ice-hockey","Ice Hockey","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-04-15","1988","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Ice Hockey is an action game for one or two players. Start of by selecting a team and customizing your players. Each team member can be one of three player types: small and skinny (very fast, but not good at checking or shooting), very large (slow, but with a strong body check and outstanding shooting strength) or average (medium speed, with average checking and shooting strength). After the teams are set up, it's time to play some ice hockey! Gameplay features a scrolling screen to follow the action, and lets you choose which of the players you wish to control when on defense (on offense you always control the player with the puck). Fights can break out between players, and the loser may end up in the penalty box. There are five different speed settings for the game, and periods can be either 7, 10, or 15 minutes long.","Ice Hockey (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","miximages/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png","marquees/nes/Ice Hockey (JP).png"
"nes_idol-hotline-nakayama-miho-no-tokimeki-high-school","Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho No Tokimeki High School","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-12-01","1987","1980s","Square","Nintendo","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","The game's protagonist enters Tokimeki High School and runs into a girl wearing glasses who looks identical to Miho Nakayama. Though the game is a standard text command-style adventure game similar to the Famicom Tantei Club series, in important scenes, the player is required to select a facial expression in addition to a verbal response. The four expressions (straight face, laughter, sadness, anger) must match the content of the response being given, and any incorrect responses immediately lead to the ""game over"" screen. This increased the game's difficulty considerably in comparison to other text adventure games where there were fewer incorrect choices. Some scenes specifically require that the dialogue not match with the expression. For instance, choosing a dialogue expressing joy with a straight face may be the correct choice in a certain situation because it represents a deeper level of emotion and thought on the part of the protagonist. This system allowed the game to simulate a level of complexity resembling actual love relationships, leading to its classification as a dating simulation rather than an adventure game. The game has two different endings, depending on the choices made during the game, and the prizes receivable via the Disk Fax network differed for each ending.","Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","miximages/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","screenshots/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","titlescreens/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","3dboxes/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","backcovers/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png","marquees/nes/Idol Hotline - Nakayama Miho no Tokimeki High School (English Translated by FCandChill, Filler, TheMajinZenki, and Graphicus).png"
"nes_igo-kyuu-roban-taikyoku","Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-04-14","1987","1980s","Bullet Proof Software","Bullet Proof Software","Asiatic board game","Board / Casual","1-2","1","2","","","Igo: Kyuu Roban Taikyoku is the first real attempt to bring the Japanese board game Go to a home console, namely the Famicom. The game was designed by Henk Rogers, a Dutch programmer who would play games of Go against the CEO (at the time) of Nintendo, Hiroshi Yamauchi. It was during one such game that the prospect of developing such a title for the Famicom was discussed. It was later converted from disk format to cartridge and release in August 1987.

Prior to the release of this game, Nintendo had released a game which was played on the Go board in 1983, known as Gomoku Narabe Renju, but the play mechanics were more akin to ""Connect Four"" (or five, as it were). The real game of Go is traditionally played on a 19 Ã— 19 grid, but the permutations of possibilities would take too long for the Famicom's processor to consider, so the board size has been significantly reduced to a 9 Ã— 9 grid, which allows for a smoother single player experience. The game also features a lesson mode, in which ninjas appear to teach the player about the rules and strategy of the game.","Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","miximages/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png","marquees/nes/Igo - Kyuu Roban Taikyoku (JP).png"
"nes_ishido","Ishido","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1990-12-07","1990","1990s","Software Resources International","Hori Electric","Board game","Board / Casual","1","1","1","","","Ishido is a puzzle board game consisting of a set of 72 stones and a game board of 96 squares.

Every stone has two attributes: a color and a symbol. There are six colors and six symbols in each stone set, thus creating 36 unique stones. Since each stone comes in a pair, there are therefore 72 stones in each stone set.

The primary objective of Ishido is to place all 72 stones onto the board of 96 squares. The challenge arises because stones must be placed adjacent to others that they match, either by color or symbol. When the board begins to fill up, this objective is not so easily accomplished.

A valuable move is the 4-way, in which a stone is placed in the midst of four others, two of which are matched by color, and two which are matched by symbol.

Ishido comes with 6 differently themed stone sets, 5 different game boards, and a variety of Oriental chimes and sound effects.","Ishidou (English Translated by Django).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png","miximages/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png","screenshots/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png","titlescreens/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png","3dboxes/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png","","marquees/nes/Ishidou (English Translated by Django).png"
"nes_jikai-shounen-metmag","Jikai Shounen Metmag","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-07-03","1987","1980s","Thinking Rabbit","Disk Original Group (DOG)","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","The player must go around each stage and collect every sphere in order to progress. Each sphere can change the polarity of the character from red to blue and vice versa. Each stage is littered with blue and red arrows: Being the same color as these arrows will magnetize the player character, which has different effects depending on the direction the arrow is pointing: For instance, an arrow pointing down will push the player to the ground if they are the reverse color but a same-colored character can hover across as the blocks will hold them up. Conversely, an arrow pointing up will allow players of the opposite color to jump far higher from the arrow, but will almost entirely eliminate the jumping power of a same-colored character as it pulls them down.","Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","miximages/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png","marquees/nes/Jikai Shounen Met Mag (JP).png"
"nes_kaettekita-mario-bros","Kaettekita Mario Bros.","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-11-30","1988","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","","","Kaettekita Mario Bros. (""The Return of Mario Bros."") is an updated rerelease of the original Mario Bros. arcade game, which was one of the first games to be ported to the NES console. It adds a few new features to the format, including improved controls for Mario, cutscenes and the ability to save high scores with the Famicom Disk Reader's save function, but is otherwise identical.

The game was part of the Disk Writer system in Japan, where players could write new games over old Famicom disks at special machine vendors found in video game stores and other likely locales. This made the games cheaper to buy (as it minimized production costs) but also made games that were exclusive to this service, such as Kaettekita Mario Bros, harder to find for collectors.

Inexplicably, the game also has a sponsor: Nagatanien, a Japanese producer of seasonings for rice and noodle dishes. Mario had also previously appeared on Nagatanien Japanese TV commercials. This makes Kaettekita Mario Bros. an early example of an advergame.

The European version of Mario Bros., named Mario Bros. Classic, was based off this game rather than the NES original.","Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","miximages/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","screenshots/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","titlescreens/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","3dboxes/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","backcovers/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png","marquees/nes/Kaettekita Mario Bros. (English Translated by FCandChill).png"
