In this solo game, you play a game of Hold Up with the beautiful model Kobayashi Hitomi.","Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","miximages/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png","marquees/nes/Kobayashi Hitomi no Hold Up (JP).png"
"nes_konamic-ice-hockey","Konamic Ice Hockey","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-07-22","1988","1980s","Konami","Konami","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","","","Blades of Steel, later released in Japan as Konamic Ice Hockey is an ice hockey video game released by Konami for North American arcades in 1987, and ported to the Family Computer Disk System and Nintendo Entertainment System in 1988. All teams are fictional but based out of real Canadian and American cities. The game is known for its fast paced hockey action and especially for the fighting. It is a one or two player game. When playing against the computer, there are three difficulty levels to choose from: Junior, College, and Pro (with Pro being the most difficult and Junior being the easiest). Each team consists of three forwards, two defense men, and a goaltender.","Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","miximages/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png","marquees/nes/Konamic Ice Hockey (JP).png"
"nes_konamic-tennis","Konamic Tennis","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-08-19","1988","1980s","Konami","Konami","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","","","Konamic Tennis is part of a long line of Konami sports games made specifically for the Famicom and its Famicom Disk System peripheral, which had previously used the term ""Exciting"" to signify its Konami status: As is the case with games such as Exciting Baseball or Exciting Basketball (known in the west as Double Dribble). Instead, this and future Konami sports games for the system would have the ""Konamic"" appellation; a portmanteau of ""Konami"" and ""dynamic"". At least, this is how the same pun in its original Japanese is commonly transliterated.

Konamic Tennis follows a similar design philosophy as other tennis games at the time (and would be used for most of the ones to follow) by depicting the action from behind the serving player's side of the court. It follows the rules of the sport precisely, with a single game usually ending after four successful volleys (scored as 15, 30, 40 and Game) and the first to win six games deciding a set. Players can choose between a single set match or a longer 3-set match. They can also play in a tournament or a single one-on-one game with a CPU (or human) opponent of their choosing.

One feature Konamic Tennis introduces to the genre is that of the serving view, shown as an inset whenever a player serves the ball to start a game. The goal is to hit the ball at precisely the right moment to serve an excellent shot; too early or too late will cause the player to either hit the net or send the ball too far, or simply not hit the ball at all. In all these cases, it counts as a foul and the player has one more chance to correctly serve the ball, otherwise points are awarded to the opponent.","Konamic Tennis (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","miximages/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png","marquees/nes/Konamic Tennis (JP).png"
"nes_koneko-monogatari-the-adventures-of-chatran","Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures Of Chatran","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-09-19","1986","1980s","Marionette","Pony Canyon","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","Koneko Monogatari: The Adventures of Chatran is a game developed for the Family Computer Disk System in 1986 by Pony Canyon and the NES in 1987. It is actually a movie license title based on the cinema release of Koneko Monogatari, better known in the United States as The Adventures of Milo and The video game representation of this movie is quite different, and regretfully slow. It gives the player control over the kitten Milo, as he attempts to navigate through a side scrolling world of trees, hostile animals, and other obstacles. Milo plods along at a very slow pace, and can jump from tree branch to tree branch, popping fruit out from the leaves, which he must collect before they turn rotten and disappear. Every moving object from mice to beetles, is lethal to Milo and must be avoided. Fruit can be dropped on enemies for extra points. Eggs can be collected to use as weapons which must be dropped on enemies. Bottles of milk grant temporary invincibility. And if the player manages to collect four eggs that spell out the word HELP, Otis will appear to attack enemies that are headed Milo's way. However, touching Otis will also kill Milo..","Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","miximages/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","screenshots/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","titlescreens/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","3dboxes/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","backcovers/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png","marquees/nes/Koneko Monogatari - The Adventures of Chatran (English Translated With Corrected Ending by BlackPaladin).png"
"nes_link-no-bouken-the-legend-of-zelda-2","Link No Bouken - The Legend Of Zelda 2","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-01-14","1987","1980s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","","","Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 is the second installment in The Legend of Zelda series, published by Nintendo for the Nintendo Entertainment System. It was originally released in Japan on the Famicom Disk System less than a year after the release of The Legend of Zelda. 

The Adventure of Link is a direct sequel to the original The Legend of Zelda, and again involves the protagonist Link on a quest to save Princess Zelda -- who is not the same Princess from the first game, but another member of the royal family by the same name, who was placed under a sleeping spell a long time ago. The Adventure of Link's emphasis on side-scrolling and RPG-style elements, however, was a significant departure from its predecessor and has given it a reputation as the ""black sheep"" of the Zelda series. It is arguably the hardest Zelda game. Despite this reputation, the game was highly successful and introduced many elements that would become commonplace in future Zelda games.","Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","miximages/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","screenshots/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","titlescreens/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","3dboxes/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","backcovers/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png","marquees/nes/Link no Bouken - The Legend of Zelda 2 (English Translated, Rev 2.33, by Columbo).png"
