"nes_golf-bishoujo-classic-the","Golf - Bishoujo Classic, The","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1990-01-01","1990","1990s","Hacker International","Hacker International","Sports","Sports","1","1","1","","","An adult game in which you play golf, upon doing well enough you will see various girls undress.","The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","miximages/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","screenshots/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","backcovers/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png","marquees/nes/The Golf - Bishoujo Classic (JP).png"
"nes_tobidase-daisakusen","Tobidase Daisakusen","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-03-12","1987","1980s","Square","Disk Original Group (DOG)","Shooter / TPV","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Tobidase Daisakusen was developed by Square in 1987 and was published by DOG, a now-defunct label of Square. The North American release, which was the first Square game to reach American shores, was published by Acclaim Entertainment, Inc. for the NES under the name The 3-D Worldrunner.

The gameplay can best be described as an attempt to merge Super Mario Bros. game mechanics with Space Harrier's third person perspective. In the game, the player assumes the role of Jack, a wild ""space cowboy"" on a mission to save various planets overrun by serpent-like beasts. The game takes place in Solar System #517, which is being overrun by a race of aliens known as Serpentbeasts, who are led by the evil Grax. As WorldRunner, the player must battle through eight planets to destroy Grax. For its time, the game was technically advanced; the game's three dimensional scrolling effect is very similar to the linescroll effects used by Pole Position and many racing games of the day.

Part of the appeal and selling point of 3-D WorldRunner was its ""3-D mode,"" and it was the first of three games by Square to feature such an option (the other two being JJ, the sequel to 3-D WorldRunner, and Rad Racer). When the 3-D mode is selected, the game uses computer image processing techniques to combine images from two slightly different viewpoints into a single image, known as anaglyph images. The game was packaged with cardboard ""anaglyph glasses,"" which use red and cyan color filters to moderate the light reaching each eye to create the illusion of a three dimensional image.

Tobidase Daisakusen was designed by Hironobu Sakaguchi, Nobuo Uematsu, and Nasir Gebelli, all whom would later rise to fame as core members of the team behind the popular RPG Final Fantasy. The three also developed JJ - Tobidase Daisakusen Part II, the sequel to 3-D WorldRunner.","Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","miximages/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png","marquees/nes/Tobidase Daisakusen (JP).png"
"nes_topple-zip","Topple Zip","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-10-09","1987","1980s","Bothtec","Bothtec","Shooter","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Topple Zip is a vertically scrolling shooter that is also labeled as a race game. The player pilots an air craft and flies in competition against several computer controlled craft. In addition to firing upon your opponents, you may ram into them (and they may ram into you) and dislodge a power-up from them which you can then shoot and collect. There are eight distinct scenes, each of which contain a number of warps which link to other scenes. How the scenes connect is random on each play through. The goal is to locate the key which is necessary to access to warp which leads to the eight and final scene, collect it from whichever scene it is hidden inside of, and access the eighth scene before any other competitor.","Topple Zip (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","miximages/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png","marquees/nes/Topple Zip (JP).png"
"nes_transformers-the-headmasters","Transformers - The Headmasters","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-08-28","1987","1980s","Takara","Takara","Shooter","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Transformers: The Headmasters is a 1987 Family Computer Disk System game by Takara based on the popular cartoon series of the same name and released only in Japan. The game stars the Autobot Headmasters as the protagonists and playable characters. The game is infamous for its high level of difficulty and off-key background music.

You play as one of the Autobot Headmaster through four planets, fighting the Decepticons. Unlike it predecessor, Transformers: Convoy no Nazo, players cannot switch between robot and vehicle mode. Instead, each level contains an initial vehicle mode stage, followed by a robot mode stage. Players start out with only Chromedome. They can switch between Autobots at any time, but only if that Autobot has been collected. Players must rescue them throughout the game, as they will be flown in by helicopter in the vehicle mode stage, or found in one of the rooms of the robot mode stage. Since Headmasters was developed for the FDS, it contains a save game feature similar to those found in early The Legend of Zelda and Metroid games.","Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","miximages/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png","marquees/nes/Transformers - The Headmasters (JP).png"
"nes_twinbee","Twinbee","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-01-04","1986","1980s","Konami","Konami","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1-2","1","2","","","TwinBee is a vertical scrolling shooter. The players control cartoon-like spacecrafts with anthropomorphic arms, player one being TwinBee and player two being WinBee. The spacecrafts can be moved in all 8 directions, with one button for regular shooting flying enemies and one button for dropping ground bombs.

Power-ups in the form of bells can be collected by shooting clouds. The color of the bell will change if it is shot by the player, assuming one of the possible colors:

1. Yellow: grants bonus points;
2. White: upgrades player's guns to a twin cannon;
3. Blue: increases player's speed (up to five levels);
4. Green: creates image copies of the craft for more firepower;
5. Red: provides a shield to sustain damage.

The green and red bells cannot be combined. Alternate power ups can be collected from the ground, such as an alternate bell which upgrades the craft with a three way gun and a star which destroys all enemies on screen.

One shot in the middle section of the craft will destroy it. Shots which hit one of the craft's arms will destroy only the arm. If both arms are lost, the craft won't be able to shoot bombs anymore and will have to be repaired by an ambulance.

In the two-player mode, TwinBee (cyan craft) and WinBee (red craft) have the same speed and they can join ""hand-to-hand"" and fire a super blast. Also in two player mode, if one craft is hit by the other from behind, it will fire four multi-directional shots.","TwinBee (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","miximages/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","screenshots/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","backcovers/nes/TwinBee (JP).png","marquees/nes/TwinBee (JP).png"
"nes_ultraman-kaijuu-teikoku-no-gyakushuu","Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku No Gyakushuu","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-01-29","1987","1980s","Bandai","Bandai","Shooter / Run and Gun","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Ultraman: Kaiju Teikoku no Gyakushu, loosely translated as ""Ultraman: Counterattack of the Monster Empire"" is a side scrolling platformer developed for the Family Computer Disk System and published by Bandai at the start of 1987. This game is the first Famicom game dealing with Ultraman. Ultraman 2 was a sequel developed as a simulation game, and Ultraman Club: Chikyuu Dakkan Sakusen was developed as an RPG utilizing the ""chibi"" or super-deformed style of artwork for the characters.

The player controls Hayate, a member of the Science Special Search Party, which protects the earth from invasions of large hostile aliens. He must run across each stage, defeating enemies with his ray gun. He must also collect energy orbs to fill a meter on the screen. When it is full, he can transform himself into the powerful Ultraman. Energy balls can appear under a variety of circumstance, such as beating an enemy or shooting points in space. Just like in the show, you can only be Ultraman for 3 minutes, and you can chop or kick the boss monsters. When only one minute remains, Ultraman will gain access to two secret powers, the Specium Ray, and the Cutting Halo. Once the boss monster is defeated, the stage is cleared.

If all 11 stages are cleared and the player continues, the game will change the character to Dan Moroboshi who can transform into Ultra Seven. Although Ultra Seven's capabilities are the same as Ultraman's, the defensive power of the enemies will be higher. There are two versions of this game, one which was distributed on disk, and one which was distributed through disk writing stations (made available on March 26, 1987) and they feature slightly different background music tunes.","Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","miximages/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png","marquees/nes/Ultraman - Kaijuu Teikoku no Gyakushuu (JP).png"
"nes_volleyball","Volleyball","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1986-07-21","1986","1980s","Pax Softnica","Nintendo","Sports","Sports","1-2","1","2","","","The game enables the player to choose the amount of people on their team, their country and their gender. The countries include the United States, China, Japan, South Korea, Brazil, Cuba, Russia (USR), and Tunisia. 

In the game the player will control their players by using the d-pad, and will use the b button to perform the game's various actions. The a button is not used in Volleyball. On the court there are two rows, 1 and 2. When the ball nears one of the rows, the team members in that row will then be able to be controlled by the player. If you are unable to hit the ball over the net, then the opposing team receives one point. After hitting the ball to the other side, the other team may or may not hit it over. If they don't your team receives one point.

To serve, the player must press the b button, wait for the ball to come back down to them and then press the b button again. When the opposing team serves, you will have to quickly hit it back over. Occasionally when you simply hit the ball, it'll go straight up in the air. If this happens, you'll have to press the b button once more in order to hit it over.","Volleyball (JP).fds","FDS","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","miximages/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","screenshots/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","titlescreens/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","3dboxes/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","backcovers/nes/Volleyball (JP).png","marquees/nes/Volleyball (JP).png"
