"nes_family-circuit-91","Family Circuit '91","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1991-07-19","1991","1990s","GAME","Namcot","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","","","Family Circuit '91 is a follow-up to the racing game Family Circuit. Like the previous game, it offers plenty of customizable options for the player to take account of. Players have the options of setting the color of the car, can tune the engine to their liking, can adjust the car's transmission, suspension, brake, and steering settings, set the angle of the wing, and choose the softness or hardness of the tires. The game also features 24 different tracks from around the world to race on.","Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).zip","ZIP","0.26","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","miximages/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","screenshots/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","backcovers/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png","marquees/nes/Family Circuit '91 (Japan) (En).png"
"nes_family-circuit","Family Circuit","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-01-06","1988","1980s","GAME","Namcot","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","","","""Family Circuit"" is a racing game for the Family Computer that stands out from other arcade-oriented titles of its time due to its realistic features. Vehicles can be customized, including tires, wings, suspension, and detailed engine settings. There are four racing modes: Free Driving, Sprint Races in various classes, Long-Distance Races, and a Spectator Mode. Collisions with opponents are not possible, but damage from obstacles requires a pit stop to repair parts.","Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).zip","ZIP","0.07","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","miximages/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","screenshots/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","backcovers/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","marquees/nes/Family Circuit (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png"
"nes_family-feud","Family Feud","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1991-05-01","1991","1990s","Beam Software","GameTek","Quiz","Board / Casual","1-2","1","2","30","1.5","Based on the television game show, Family Feud. The player chooses a family of five to play against another family. Players are asked to find the most common answers to various questions asked by the host, so the family can earn points. The family with the most points at the end of the game wins the prize money.","Family Feud (USA).zip","ZIP","0.1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","miximages/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","screenshots/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","backcovers/nes/Family Feud (USA).png","marquees/nes/Family Feud (USA).png"
"nes_family-jockey","Family Jockey","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-04-24","1987","1980s","Namco","Namco","Horse racing","Racing","2","1","2","","","Family Jockey is a horse racing simulation developed and published by Namco for the Famicom in Japan in 1987. In the game, players take control of a horse, and help direct it to finish a track as quickly as possible by steering it, and wisely timing the expenditure of the horses energy in order to stay ahead of the pack without tiring too quickly. Progress is achieved by placing in the top four horses of each race. Prize money is earned by placing in the top three and by breaking course records.","Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).zip","ZIP","0.03","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","miximages/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","screenshots/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","backcovers/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png","marquees/nes/Family Jockey (Japan) (Namcot Collection).png"
"nes_family-mahjong","Family Mahjong","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-08-11","1987","1980s","Nichibutsu","Namco","Mahjong","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Family Mahjong is another in a long line of mahjong games for the Famicom. Uniquely, at least for the time, it does include a full tutorial mode that will teach the player how to actually play mahjong. It also includes rules for mahjong gambling, which is another feature included in the game. After the player has learned how to play, they can compete against a series of CPU opponents in one-on-one matches, most of whom are attractive blonde women for some reason.","Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).zip","ZIP","0.09","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","miximages/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","screenshots/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","backcovers/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png","marquees/nes/Family Mahjong (Japan) (Rev A).png"
"nes_family-mahjong-2-shanghai-e-no-michi","Family Mahjong 2 - Shanghai E No Michi","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-11-25","1988","1980s","Nichibutsu","Namco","Mahjong","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Family Mahjong II: Shanghai e no Michi is a Mahjong game and the direct sequel to Family Mahjong. In addition to the standard Mahjong mode, there is a tournament mode with a slight RPG aspect to it, in that the player can enhance certain stats after winning games in order to increase their odds in future rounds of the tournament.

The game is a one-on-one version of the game, less common in real-life Mahjong games but the standard for computer adaptations due to the reduced complexity of only having a single AI opponent. Nihon Bussan was responsible for Family Mahjong II's development and was at the time fairly well known for their Mahjong Arcade games.","Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","miximages/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","screenshots/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","backcovers/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png","marquees/nes/Family Mahjong II - Shanghai e no Michi (Japan).png"
"nes_family-noraebang","Family Noraebang","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","","","","Daou Infosys","Daewoo","Unknown","Unknown","1","1","1","","","Open Production provided the software for the Family Noraebang, a Famicom clone &amp; karaoke combo manufactured by Daou Infosys and distributed by Daewoo. (But the cartridge was also sold as a standalone for use with any other Famicom compatible.) With 56 songs the initial selection paled against its direct competitor by HiCom, but Daou offered various song packs which could be inserted into a special slot in the Karaoke cart. It is unknown how many of those packs were actually produced, though at least two could be spotted in an advertisement (40 songs each). The cart also included an FM sound chip and an additional audio jack to directly connect it to an amp.","Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).zip","ZIP","0.34","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","miximages/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","screenshots/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","backcovers/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png","marquees/nes/Family Noraebang (Korea) (Unl).png"
"nes_family-quiz-4-nin-wa-rival","Family Quiz - 4-nin Wa Rival","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1988-11-16","1988","1980s","Athena","Athena","Quiz / Japanese","Board / Casual","1-4","1","4","","","Family Quiz is a Japanese trivia game where the goal is to beat the other opponents in four mini-games, each requiring that the player answer some Japanese trivia questions in order to proceed. The four mini-games are based on: A typical game show set up, where answering questions correctly raises that player's podium towards a balloon; Othello, where the goal is to surround the other player's pieces; a board-game, where the player moves their piece around the board; a card-based memory game, where the goal is to flip over pairs of matching cards. In each game, the player gets a turn only after they've answered the trivia question correctly.","Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.07","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","miximages/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","screenshots/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","backcovers/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png","marquees/nes/Family Quiz - 4-nin wa Rival (Japan).png"
"nes_family-school","Family School","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","","","","Toppan Printing","Tokyo Shoseki","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","","","Family School is an action transport puzzle game, where your task is to water plants in some kind of garden so that fruit grows. Once the fruit has grown, you have to push the right fruit into the right basket at the edge of the playing field. Watch out for animals which will try to harm you. The game can be played with at least two players simultaneously. The game was apparently given as a free gift ""Dai-ichi Life"", an insurance company, to people who purchased policies from them.","Family School (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.11","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Family School (Japan).png","miximages/nes/Family School (Japan).png","screenshots/nes/Family School (Japan).png","titlescreens/nes/Family School (Japan).png","3dboxes/nes/Family School (Japan).png","backcovers/nes/Family School (Japan).png","marquees/nes/Family School (Japan).png"
"nes_family-tennis","Family Tennis","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1987-12-11","1987","1980s","Namco","Namcot","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","","","Family Tennis released December 11, 1987 exclusively in Japan for the Nintendo Entertainment System. The fourth of Namco's lengthy ""Family"" series of sports games and the first to concentrate on tennis, Family Tennis plays much like its Nintendo forebear and other tennis game contemporaries. It shares the same viewpoint from behind the court and requires that the player make their way to where each shot is heading and return the ball by tapping the swing button in order to win.

Family Tennis has a few notable quirks, however. First, is that the many fictional tennis players available all superficially resemble actual real-life stars of the sport. The second is ""Cosmos Mode"", in which the action is taken to outer space. Besides the starry background and the astronaut ballboys, there's little functional difference however. There was also four female characters, of the sixteen total, who all had a bigger racket then the men. Essentially making the women into an 'Easy Mode'","Family Tennis (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.05","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","miximages/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","screenshots/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","titlescreens/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","3dboxes/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","backcovers/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png","marquees/nes/Family Tennis (Japan).png"
