"nes_the-incredible-crash-dummies","The Incredible Crash Dummies","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1993-09-23","1993","1990s","Software Creations","LJN","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","Junkman breaks into the crash test center and kidnaps two of the Incredible Crash Dummies, Daryl and Spare Tire with the intentions of using them for their parts to build his junkbots. Now it's up to Slick and Spin to go and rescue the kidnapped crash dummies.

In The Incredible Crash Dummies the player alternates between Slick and Spin as they go through five levels with each level containing 2 stages (except for the 4th level which only consists of one). One of the Dummies' legs has been replaced with a wheel while the other Dummy has his legs intact. So the main difference in gameplay is that in some stages the player either rolls throughout it or walks throughout it. The Dummies' arsenal includes an air gun which stuns enemies at close range and an oil gun which shoots and kills enemies at a far range. There are also a few powerups scattered throughout the stages such as the arrow, balloon, and arm powerup.","Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).zip","ZIP","0.15","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","miximages/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","screenshots/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","titlescreens/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","3dboxes/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","backcovers/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png","marquees/nes/Incredible Crash Dummies, The (USA).png"
"nes_indiana-jones-and-the-last-crusade","Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade","nes","Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom","NES","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","NMS Software","Ubi Soft Entertainment Software","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","90","4.5","Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade differs from the two other games with the same name as it is a hybrid of various genres such as platform action, motorcycle racing, puzzle solving and even some adventure elements.

In Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade you play Indy's role in his search for the Holy Grail, but this time it is you who chooses which step he will take at each part of his quest. For example, in the very beginning you receive a diary about the Grail from Indy's father, who is in Venice. At the same time, you receive a telegram from Marcus saying that the Cross of Coronado is in Portugal on a ship called Coronado, and that he'll meet Indy there. So you have to choose what you'll do first: go to Venice and check about the Grail and your father, or go to Portugal to find Marcus and the Cross of Coronado.

In the entire game you'll be prompted with these choices, each one leading to different stages throughout the game. Each stage presents different game genres. For example, in Venice you find a scrambled picture of the Grail, and you have to try to put it back together, just like you'd do with the pieces of a puzzle. Besides, in the platform stages, Indy has plenty of movements, as he can punch, kick, walk, run, jump and use his whip.

The game presents bitmapped pictures of the real movie actors, such as Harrison Ford and Sean Connery.","Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).zip","ZIP","0.17","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","miximages/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","screenshots/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","titlescreens/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","3dboxes/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","backcovers/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png","marquees/nes/Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (USA) (Taito).png"
