"fds_family-composer","Family Composer","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1987-10-30","1987","1980s","Musical Plan","Tokyo Shoseki","Music and Dancing","Action","1","1","1","","","Family Composer  was developed by the same team that created Ikinari Musician for the Famicom earlier in the same year. Like Ikinari Musician, it is not really a game, but rather a music simulator. Unlike Ikinari Musician, Family Composer gives players more control over the music played, and even allows them to compose new songs, and record the compositions to disk. In many ways, it can be seen as a precursor to the music composing feature of Mario Paint.","Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.03","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","backcovers/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","marquees/fds/Family Composer (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-1-hong-kong","Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1990-03-23","1990","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Mahjong","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Hong Kong, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 Hong Kong, is a Shanghai variant in which players must remove specified tiles from a pattern. The player can select which pattern (from pyramids and hourglasses to more complex designs) from which to draw tiles as well as inputting a three letter code which offers several thousand possible combinations of the tiles within that pattern.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.02","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","backcovers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 1 - Hong Kong (Japan).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-2-panic-space","Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1990-10-19","1990","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Panic Space, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 Panic Space, is a puzzle game in which the goal is to reflect a laser to an exit panel in order to open the way to the next stage. The laser cannot hit any of the stage's surrounding wall: were it do so, it would cause the level to explode and the player to lose a life (from an initial allotment of five, though this can be increased while playing the game). Much of the time the various reflectors (which send the beam off at a 90 degree angle) and blocks (which simply stop the laser in its tracks) must be maneuvered into place before the laser should be tested on them.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.03","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","backcovers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 2 - Panic Space (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-3-all-1","Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1991-02-22","1991","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","All One, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 All One, is a puzzle game in which the player controls a ladybug pushing over dice. The goal of each stage is to push all the dice onto their side so that their ""one"" side is facing upwards. The player is given a smaller first-person view window from the ladybug's perspective to allow them to see the sides of each die and plan out their strategy.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.03","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 3 - All 1 (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-4-clox","Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1991-04-19","1991","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Clox, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 Clox, is a falling blocks puzzle game that is superficially similar to Tetris or Columns. Single clock faces depicting one of four angles fall from the top and the player needs to manipulate and place them so they can form larger objects, such as simple formations like squares, diamonds and hourglass shapes to more object-intensive shapes like octagons and hexagons.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.03","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 4 - Clox (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-5-puyo-puyo","Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1991-10-25","1991","1990s","Compile","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle / Fall","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","","","Puyo Puyo is the first entry of Compile's Puyo Puyo series. It was released on the MSX and Famicom Disk System on October 25, 1991; the FDS version was published by Tokuma Shoten as part of their ""Famimaga Disk"" line of games. The FDS version would later be released on a Famicom cart in 1993. Puyo Puyo would be succeeded by a much more popular arcade game of the same name.","Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.04","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 5 - Puyo Puyo (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_famimaga-disk-vol-6-janken-disk-jou","Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1992-12-22","1992","1990s","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Tokuma Shoten Intermedia","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","","","Janken Disk Jou, or Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 Janken Disk Jou, is a puzzle game in which the protagonist (who looks uncannily like Disk-kun, the mascot of the Famicom Disk System) must brave a labyrinth of puzzles. In each room there are three variants of hand-shaped blocks between the hero and the exit: each one either making the ""scissors"" gesture, the ""rock"" gesture or the ""paper"" gesture. Pushing a block adjacent to another of a different type causes whichever was the inferior (e.g. with paper and rock, rock is the inferior) to vanish. In addition, each block has a ""strength"" which is signified by its color: green is level 1, yellow is level 2 and red is level 3. If a green block is pushed next to an inferior red block, the red block will instead become yellow instead of vanishing (and then green with a subsequent superior block, and then will vanish after a third).","Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.06","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","false","covers/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png","","marquees/fds/Famimaga Disk Vol. 6 - Janken Disk Jou (Japan) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_final-commando-akai-yousai","Final Commando - Akai Yousai","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1988-05-02","1988","1980s","Konami","Konami","Shooter","Shooter","1-2","1","2","","","Your brothers-in-arms are hostages behind enemy lines, and you're their only hope for freedom. But the firepower you'll face to rescue them is awesome. Cannons, tanks, submarines and snipers will blast you with horrific crossfire, while jet fighters zero in from above. To defend yourself, you control the army's advanced all-terrain attack jeep, with its arsenal of guided missiles and incendiary grenades. Of course these are merely tools, and to save your countrymen you'll need more than a handful of gunpowder. You'll need a pocketful of miracles, and the ferocity of a wild Jackal!","Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.06","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","miximages/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","screenshots/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","titlescreens/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","3dboxes/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","backcovers/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png","marquees/fds/Final Command - Akai Yousai (Japan).png"
"fds_fire-bam","Fire Bam","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1988-02-01","1988","1980s","HAL Laboratory","HAL Laboratory","Action RPG","RPG","1","1","1","","","Bam is a fifteen years old courageous boy. Like every boy his age, Bam must pass the village's test and bring back an insect's shell from the forest. But as he returns, and to his surprise, most of the village has been destroyed and his parents turned into demons. Armed with his sword, Bam sets forth in search of what happened to the village and his parents.","Fire Bam (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.06","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","miximages/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","screenshots/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","titlescreens/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","3dboxes/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","backcovers/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png","marquees/fds/Fire Bam (Japan).png"
"fds_fire-rock","Fire Rock","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1988-01-01","1988","1980s","System Sacom","Use Corporation","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","Fire Rock is an action game developed for the Famicom Disk System by System Sacom, and published in Japan by Use in 1988. The design of the game is credited to Mark Flint. In the game, the player controls what appears to be a cave man, whose goal is to navigate a series of item filled caverns in an effort to locate the boss of the level, defeat it, and then locate the door which leads to the next level. The game is commonly sited as having very poor controls, often requiring the player to grab hold of a wall, and very slowly climb it by holding up and tapping jump repeatedly. Other issues include a very low jump that places more platforms and items out of reach, and a difficult time bringing the character back under control if he starts to slip. This game was not released outside of Japan.","Fire Rock (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.05","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","miximages/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","screenshots/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","titlescreens/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","3dboxes/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","backcovers/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png","marquees/fds/Fire Rock (Japan).png"
