"fds_omoikkiri-tanteidan-haado-gumi-matenrou-no-chousenjou","Omoikkiri Tanteidan Haado Gumi - Matenrou No Chousenjou","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1988-03-25","1988","1980s","Bandai","Bandai","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","Based upon a Japanese television series that aired in 1987. It features a group of five detective kids on a mission to retrieve various objects stolen by a mysterious thief who wears a golden mask. The group counts the leader Hiroshi, the cute and Aikido expert Yasuko who also happen to be the only girl of the group, Susumu, Satoru and Takeo. The mysterious thief uses many ingenious tricks to escape his pursuers, such as a huge and colorful air-balloon.
Before getting to the main portion of the game, the player must pass the organization's test. After entering personal information such as name, birth-date and blood type, the player must go through two challenges. The first one is a memory test. Eight character faces (from a list of four) appear at random on the screen and the player needs to remember the order in which they came in. The second test is action based and the player must catch roses thrown by the thief, himself hanging from an air-balloon. Once the tests are passed, the real game starts. Our hero can move around town and visit buildings to gather information. He can also use various items such as a slingshot, a metal detector, a radio, binoculars etc. Baddies also wander around the city and must be avoided. They can also be knocked down using any weapon our hero may carry (like the slingshot) or by punching them on the chin.","Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).zip","ZIP","0.06","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","miximages/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","screenshots/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","titlescreens/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","3dboxes/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","backcovers/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png","marquees/fds/Omoikkiri Tanteidan HaadoGumi - Matenrou no Chousenjou (Japan).png"
"fds_othello","Othello","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1986-10-13","1986","1980s","HAL Laboratory","Kawada","Othello","Action","1-2","1","2","","","Othello is a conversion of the board game. Each player tries to fill the game board with as many discs of his color as possible. On each turn the player places a disc on the board by selecting a location where the opponents discs will become trapped between two of his discs. The trapped discs are flipped over to the players color. The game ends when the board is filled or time runs out, and the player with the greatest number of his color discs on the board wins. Two players can play against each other, or one player against the computer.","Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).zip","ZIP","0.02","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","miximages/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","screenshots/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","titlescreens/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","3dboxes/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","backcovers/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png","marquees/fds/Othello (Japan) (DV 5) (Disk Writer).png"
"fds_otocky","Otocky","fds","Famicom Disk System","FDS","Nintendo","3. kynslóð (8-bit viðbót)","1987-03-27","1987","1980s","Scitron","ASCII","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","","","Otocky can be described as a musical side-scrolling shoot 'em up. The player's spaceship has a ball for a weapon, which can be fired in eight directions; each direction corresponds to a different musical note. The note plays when the player presses the fire button, and is also quantized in time so that it matches the beat playing in the background. By using the weapon selectively the player can improvise music while playing.

