The most technically-advanced of Dizzy's adventure games in many ways, it was the only one to feature actual scrolling rather than a flick-screen system, the only one not to be released for the 8 bit computers the character originated on, the only one to originate on a console, as well as featuring many mini-games, such as Bubble Dizzy and an Operation Wolf clone. 

Gameplay involves walking Dizzy through the levels, picking up objects and using them to solve simple puzzles. There are plenty of tests of arcade skill along the way as well, as gaps must be jumped and baddies avoided - starting with a limited number of lives, the player can earn more by completing Theodore's puzzle. Before rescuing Daisy the player must capture all stars that unlock the gates of the tower.","Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).7z","7Z","0.37","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","miximages/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","screenshots/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","backcovers/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png","marquees/megadrive/Fantastic Dizzy (USA, Europe) (En,Fr,De,Es,It).png"
