"megadrive_futbol-argentino-96","Futbol Argentino 96","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Miky","Miky","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-4","1","4","","","Futbol Argentino 96 is an unlicensed hack of J. League Pro Striker 2, which was distributed in Argentina during the late 1990s. Futbol Argentino 96 is almost the same game as Pro Striker 2, but contains teams from the Argentine Football Association (AFA), presumably without a license to do so. This made it (and similar SNES hacks) unusually popular in the country. Futbol Argentino 96, was intended for players from Argentina. That year, Argentina had the best footballers in the country, Maradona, Francescoli, Batistuta, Cannigia and others, included in this game. So it was very successful on 16-bit consoles. A trick that this game brings in the selection screen command, hold A + B + C and it leads to penalty shots. A successor to the classes exists as Futbol Argentino 98.","Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).7z","7Z","0.81","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png","miximages/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png","screenshots/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).jpg","titlescreens/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png","backcovers/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png","marquees/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 96 (Argentina) (Pirate).png"
"megadrive_fifa-soccer-95","FIFA Soccer 95","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-10","1994","1990s","Extended Play Productions","EA Sports","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-4","1","4","70","3.5","After the first game proved to be a worldwide success, EA developed this sequel exclusively for Sega's 16-bit console. The biggest change from FIFA International Soccer are obviously the dozens of club teams from England, France, Germany, Italy, The Netherlands, United States, Spain and Brazil, taking the team number tally to over 200. This also increased the number of competitions, with each country  having it's own League, Tournament and Playoff. Also new, a battery backup allowed to save up to four competitions in the cartridge, replacing passwords. For those who want to settle things the fast way, a Penalty Shootout training mode was added.

While graphically the game only received minor changes to the isometric sprite engine, gameplay feature new moves such as volley kicks and more detailed headers (now more effective), as well as general controls being more responsive facilitating passing play, and goalkeepers now are smarter when chasing balls. Set pieces also received a new format, and instead of just kicking the ball the player can choose to play a predefined set piece.","Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).7z","7Z","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","miximages/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","screenshots/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","backcovers/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png","marquees/megadrive/Futbol Argentino 98 - Pasion de Multitudes (Argentina) (Pirate).png"
