ISS Deluxe has realistic animations and player sprites. You can compete with 36 national teams with recognizable celebrity players (like Valderrama, Baggio, Ravanelli, Petkov...), but their names are fictitious. You can choose too from 16 formations and 8 strategies to win.

The competitions available are: International Cup, World Series (League), Scenario Mode and customizable mini-cups/leagues.","International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).7z","7Z","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","miximages/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","screenshots/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","titlescreens/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","3dboxes/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","backcovers/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png","marquees/megadrive/International Superstar Soccer Deluxe (Europe, Brazil) (En).png"
"megadrive_ishido-the-way-of-stones","Ishido : The Way of Stones","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1990-01-01","1990","1990s","Publishing International","Accolade","Board game","Board / Casual","1","1","1","40","2","Ishido is a solitaire board game played with tiles, similar to Shanghai. The objective is to place all of the tiles onto the board and obtain the highest possible score by following specific rules of play. There are ways to play that can dramatically increase the score, which makes the game very strategic in nature. Additionally, when desired, the player can get an oracle reading by asking a question, which is answered when the player makes a very elegant play (four-way match).","Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).7z","7Z","0.05","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","miximages/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","screenshots/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","backcovers/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png","marquees/megadrive/Ishido - The Way of Stones (USA) (Unl).png"
"megadrive_it-came-from-the-desert","It Came from the Desert","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1989-01-01","1989","1980s","Cinemaware","Electronic Arts","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","40","2","The player assumes the role of Dr. Greg Bradley who comes to remote Lizard Breath, Nevada on June 1, 1951. As a geologist, he wants to study a recent site of a meteor crash somewhere in the desert south-west of the small town. Early in the game, he learns that the radiation of the meteor has enlarged a local ant population to an enormous size. However, few take his observations seriously. Worried, that the ants will soon mate and spread, he must work against a ticking clock and devise a plan to stop the ants from terrorizing the world. In order to succeed, the player must visit many locations ranging from mines, farms, a pub, an airfield, a local radio station and many more to find evidence of the ants, then convince townsfolk and authorities of impending doom. At the same time the player must contain the ant infestation.","It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).7z","7Z","0.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","miximages/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","screenshots/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","titlescreens/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","3dboxes/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","backcovers/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png","marquees/megadrive/It Came from the Desert (USA) (Proto).png"
"megadrive_the-itchy-amp-scratchy-game","The Itchy &amp; Scratchy Game","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Bits Studios","Acclaim Entertainment","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","20","1","The Itchy &amp; Scratchy Game on Megadrive is a platform game from the Simpsons' universe. Itchy (the mouse) tries to kill Scratchy (the cat) with unlimited violence and imagination. In this video game, the player plays Itchy and must find various weapons through the 7 levels to once again demolish his old enemy.","Itchy &amp; Scratchy Game, The (USA) (Proto).7z","7Z","0","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","","","","","","",""
"megadrive_izzy-s-quest-for-the-olympic-rings","Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Alexandria","U.S. Gold","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings is the official game of the mascot of the 1996 Olympic Games in Atlanta. As the name suggests, Izzy must find the five Olympic rings, hidden by the guardians so that Izzy does not take them to his world.

The game plays a lot like Mario games; to eliminate an opponent Izzy must jump over his head. There are two jumps available, the ball jump suitable for height and the hurdle jump, suitable for distance. Both allow Izzy to clear an enemy, but the second harms  him.If Izzy is hit, then he must find three medals, or at the next hit he loses a life. There are some extra objects, like Olympic torches, and eggs that hatch either objects for bonus points, trampolines or  morph points to reach otherwise inaccessible areas or traps. Izzy can also morph into a glider, a baseball player, an archer or a rocket, among others, to overcome some sections of the levels.

Graphics and sound remain faithful to the cartoony origins of the mascot.","Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).7z","7Z","0.75","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","miximages/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","screenshots/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","backcovers/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png","marquees/megadrive/Izzy's Quest for the Olympic Rings (USA, Europe).png"
"megadrive_european-club-soccer","European Club Soccer","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-08-01","1992","1990s","Krisalis Software","Krisalis Software","Sports","Sports","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","European Club Soccer has dozens of teams from all across Europe (around 170), and each country is represented by at least two teams. In addition to correct club names, it also has recognizable imitations of team badges and kits; although more elaborate kits (such as those worn by Juventus, FC Porto or Celtic F.C.) are simplified (Juventus have a silver shirt, Porto blue and Celtic green). Player names, on the other hand, were made by mixing names found on real squads. Portuguese teams, for instance, had a large number of Yugoslavian players in the early 1990s, and that was reflected by players with names such as Valentim Ivic (of Valentim Loureiro and Tomislav Ivic, then Boavista FC chairman and S.L. Benfica manager, respectively). Names could not be changed, and since the game lacked a battery, it does not store changes done to kits and passwords were used to resume tournaments (example of a password sheet). Rotherham United are included amongst the English clubs despite never playing in the top tier of English football, this is due to the developer, Krisalis Software, being based in Rotherham. All other clubs have played in their own country's top tier.","J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).7z","7Z","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","miximages/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","screenshots/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","titlescreens/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","3dboxes/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","backcovers/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png","marquees/megadrive/J. League Champion Soccer (Japan).png"
"megadrive_j-league-pro-striker","J. League Pro Striker","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-18","1993","1990s","SEGA","SEGA","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-4","1","4","80","4","Pro Striker is the first title in Sega's early-'90s soccer franchise. It is an arcade-style soccer game similar in play to Sega's earlier World Championship Soccer. The game is viewed from a birds-eye perspective with a full field minimap on the side of the screen. The player can also make substitutions and choose from six different formations. It features an official J.Leauge license, including all ten teams and all players from the 1993 season. There is an exhibition mode, a playoff mode, and a full league mode, and up to four players can play simultaneously.","J. League Pro Striker (Japan).7z","7Z","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","miximages/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","screenshots/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","titlescreens/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","3dboxes/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","backcovers/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png","marquees/megadrive/J. League Pro Striker (Japan).png"
