"megadrive_sword-of-vermilion","Sword of Vermilion","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-01-28","1991","1990s","SEGA","SEGA","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","The cousin of Sega's RPG Phantasy Star II, Sword of Vermilion contains elements from action, adventure, and RPG games in a medieval-fantasy setting. A third-person, top-down perspective is used to move around towns. A first-person view is used for overland travel outside of towns. A third-person sideview perspective (a la Double Dragon or Final Fight) is used for the combat sequences. The player is cast in the role of an ordinary young man who discovers that he is really a prince, and the son of the deceased good King Erik. He is charged with traveling around the land of Excalabria to collect the eight magic Rings of Good, defeating a number of boss enemies on the way to confronting the evil King Tsarkon.","Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).7z","7Z","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","miximages/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","screenshots/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","backcovers/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png","marquees/megadrive/Xin Shi Zhong Jian (China) (Pirate).png"
"megadrive_nobunaga-no-yabou-haouden","Nobunaga No Yabou - Haouden","megadrive","Sega Mega Drive / Genesis","Mega Drive","Sega","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-02-25","1994","1990s","Koei","Koei","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1-8","1","8","","","Nobunaga no Yabou: Haouden (Nobunaga's Ambition: Tale of the Conquerer) is a strategy-sim from Koei and the fifth in their flagship Nobunaga's Ambition series of Sengoku-era feudal sims. Once again, the player assumes one of a number of daimyos, including Oda Nobunaga himself and tries to take over the rest of Japan.

Among the tweaks to the gameplay was the addition of castles, which are now the locations that players must conquer in order to take over new territory. Haouden was also the first game in the series to be given an expansion pack though this pack would not be available to all versions of the game.

The game was the first flagship Nobunaga's Ambition game to never receive a US localization. It sits between the fourth game, Nobunaga's Ambition: Lord of Darkness (Nobunaga no Yabou: Bushou Fuuunroku) from 1990 and the sixth game, Nobunaga no Yabou: Tenshouki from 1994.","Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).7z","7Z","0","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","miximages/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","screenshots/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","titlescreens/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","3dboxes/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","backcovers/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png","marquees/megadrive/Xin Zhang Yewang - Bawang Chuan (China) (Pirate).png"
