"snes_3x3-eyes-juuma-houkan","3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-22","1995","1990s","Banpresto","Banpresto","Adventure","Adventure","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Based on the manga series 3x3 Eyes, long time ago in ancient China a group of people discovered the secret of immortality. This game follows the story of volumes 8 to 11 of the manga. Lingling discovers a letter left by Fujii, where he reports about a strange tribe that can control beasts. Yakumo is ordered to travel to Nepal, but first he has to find more information about the mysterious tribe by contacting people in Hong-Kong. The game is a mixture of puzzle-solving adventure and platform action, with the emphasis on the first. Yakumo moves around the gameworld, picking objects and manipulating them in order to solve puzzles, and talking to characters. But he can also jump, climb on ladders, run, and sometimes fight enemies in platform-style.","3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","miximages/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","screenshots/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","titlescreens/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","3dboxes/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","backcovers/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png","marquees/snes/3x3 Eyes - Juuma Houkan (English Translated by Atomizer_Zero, Rev 1.01).png"
"snes_90-minutes-european-prime-goal","90 Minutes : European Prime Goal","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-01","1995","1990s","Ocean","Namco","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","90 Minutes: European Prime Goal is a soccer game with the 14 teams of the Japanese first division (J. League) to play with. In the European version they were replaced by national teams, but the some playing modes are still reminiscent of the origin. The available modes are exhibition matches, tournament, league, cup and all-star (the teams are divided into two leagues and the overall winner is decided with play-offs). With the option ""You're a Hero"", the player can create a new athlete, change his stats with a few training lessons and swap it with an existing one. The player either controls the athlete who has the ball or, when in defense, the one who is nearest to it. The field is shown from a slightly angled side-perspective. During most modes, all teams can be taken over by human players, but only two can play at the same time.","90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/90 Minutes - European Prime Goal (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_a-bug-s-life","A Bug's Life","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","2000-01-01","2000","2000s","DVS Electronic","DVS Electronic","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","The Super Nintendo version of A Bug's Life is an unofficial (bootleg) release derived from the Mega Drive version. It keeps the same levels, but changes the controls and character behavior: A is used to jump, B to run, and both X and Y to throw berries. The gameplay is less responsive, requiring gradual acceleration to reach top speed, and the berry throw follows a higher arc, making it harder to hit certain enemies. In Hang By a Thread, it is possible to move faster with Dim by holding A. In the final level, the boss Hopper has different flight patterns and, in his last phase, starts chasing the player. Visually, it preserves the graphics with minor touch-ups, but includes sprite inconsistencies (such as the berry changing style during its animation). The audio uses a sound driver reused from Bonkers, and the backgrounds do not scroll. The intro is also different, showing Flik repeatedly flying on a dandelion seed.","A Bug's Life (TW).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","true","covers/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png","miximages/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png","screenshots/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png","titlescreens/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png","3dboxes/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png","","marquees/snes/A Bug's Life (TW).png"
