"snes_an-american-tail-fievel-goes-west","An American Tail : Fievel Goes West","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Shimada Kikaku","Hudson Soft","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","Based on the movie by the same name, Fievel Goes West is a platform game featuring the young mouse Fievel Mousekewitz. After immigrating to overseas to America in the 1800s(?), the Mousekewitz family has moved out onto the open frontier of the wild west. With dreams of being a cowboy, Fievel soon makes friends and plays on the dusty streets imagining himself a great gunslinger. But all is not well as the villainous Cat R. Waul and his gang of cats (as well as other creatures) has plans to turn this place into an all-you-can-eat mouse buffet. It's up to Fievel to get in the way of Cat's plans with the help of the canine sheriff, Wylie Burp and his non-mouse eating cat pal, Tiger

Fievel Goes West is a platform game. Fievel can jump and fire a pop-gun as he ventures through each level, collecting coins. The pop-gun can be powered-up to fire more powerful projectiles.","An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","miximages/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","screenshots/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","backcovers/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png","marquees/snes/An American Tail - Fievel Goes West (NA).png"
"snes_ancient-magic-bazoo-mahou-sekai","Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-23","1993","1990s","Hot-B","Hot-B","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","60","3","Ancient Magic is a fairly generic little RPG for the SNES by the much-maligned Hot-B. See if you can spot their advert for another of their products in the game! Despite its rather pedestrian roots, the game takes several surprising twists towards the end, and is generally worth sticking it out even if the wrap-up (or lack thereof) is a bit of a let down. As a note, there?s more game past ?THE END.? Also as a note, do set your window speed to ?fast? in the in-game options menus - it?ll improve your experience dramatically.","Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Ancient Magic - Bazoo! Mahou Sekai (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_andre-agassi-tennis","Andre Agassi Tennis","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-13","1993","1990s","Radiance Software","Tecmagik","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A game endorsed by the American player who stunned the tennis world in the early 90s with his ""Image is everything"" looks, sporting long hair, earrings and colorful shirts, Andre Agassi Tennis includes eight players (male and female and as as expected, only Agassi is a real player) rated according to movement speed and both accuracy and strength on serve, backhand and forehand. Gameplay features smashes, passing shots, volleys and all kinds of backhand and forehand plays, but unlike other games, precise positioning and timing are crucial to avoid hitting the ball outside the court, swinging the racket into thin air or more embarrassing, let the ball hit you on the head. Three courts are available. Game modes change according to the version, with the 16-bit versions including a ""Skins"" game, where each point is worth a sum of money based on the number of times the ball was hit.","Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","miximages/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png","marquees/snes/Andre Agassi Tennis (NA).png"
"snes_animaniacs","Animaniacs","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-01","1994","1990s","Konami","Konami","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","90","4.5","Animaniacs is a side-scrolling action/puzzle game based on the animated TV series of the same name.  The Warner kids, Yakko, Wakko, and Dot have escaped the water tower again and are on a quest to retrieve movie memorabilia from the Warner Bros. studio lot.  

There are 4 stages, and in each stage the Warners must work together to get past obstacles and enemies. Yakko can push or pull boxes, Wakko can use his hammer to smash things, and Dot can blow kisses to charm simple minded foes.","Animaniacs (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","miximages/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png","marquees/snes/Animaniacs (NA).png"
"snes_aoki-densetsu-shoot","Aoki Densetsu Shoot!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-16","1994","1990s","KSS","Affect","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1","1","1","80","4","Aoki Densetsu Shoot is a Japanese anime produced by Toei Animation between November 9, 1990 and December 22, 1991 on the Fuji TV television network; there were fifty-eight episodes. The game has an exhibition mode, an anime-style story mode, and a variety of options, which is entirely in Japanese, so literacy in this language is strongly recommended for quality gameplay. Once the game begins, the action progresses in a side view, similar to an arcade soccer game.","Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","miximages/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png","marquees/snes/Aoki Densetsu Shoot! (JP).png"
