"snes_ballz-3d","Ballz 3D","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Cave Logic Studios","Accolade","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","20","1","What is Ballz? Ballz is a killer 3-D fighting game. It's an in-your-face, all out fight to the finish with 8 unique characters and 8 bosses that punch, kick, club, slam, humiliate and fart on their opponents. Ballz even has an instant replay that shows you the damage from almost any angle. And with three times more special moves than those flat 2-D games, you're going to be doing lots of damage. Our characters will even morph into other characters whenever you want. Ballz also has a bunch of weird sound effects and a wisecracking scoreboard. It's the coolest *#!#@*#! fighting game on the shelf!","Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ballz 3D - Fighting at Its Ballziest (NA).png"
"snes_barbie-super-model","Barbie : Super Model","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-01","1993","1990s","Software Creations","Hi Tech Expressions","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","","","Help Barbie become a great fashion model!  This is an arcade game with many different activities to participate in.

In each level, Barbie starts by going left to right with forced scrolling.  She has a default speed and can speed up as well.  She must avoid moving obstacles by moving up and down and controlling her speed.  If she hits one, she loses a chance and it's game over if she runs out of chances.  She must pick up items which grant point bonuses and extra chances.

There is also a camera item to pick up which brings you to a magazine cover shoot.  You are shown a cover with barbie wearing an outfit which is themed to the level.  You must then go to her dressing room and dress her in a matching outfit.  You are also given separate control of the colors of three different components and must match these.  You are awarded points for each correct match.

When you reach the right side of the level, you are given a runway practice.  This consists of four positions on the runway floor labeled to tell you which button you must press at each one.

Barbie then goes back through the level right-to-left.  On the return trip, there is a handbag to pick up.  This brings you to another magazine cover.  You then go to a makeup mirror where you must match Barbie's hairstyle, earrings, eyeshadow and lip and nail color to the cover.  Again, you are awarded points for each item which you get correct.

When you reach the left side of the level, you must then do Barbie's runway walk that you were given earlier.  You are awarded points for each station at which you perform the correct move.

Barbie then continues to the next level.  There are a total of four: Barbie driving down the street, skating on the beach, walking through a ski resort and walking through the park.","Barbie Super Model (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","miximages/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png","marquees/snes/Barbie Super Model (NA).png"
"snes_barbie-vacation-adventure","Barbie Vacation Adventure","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Software Creations","Hi Tech Expressions","Adventure","Adventure","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Barbie: Vacation Adventure invites you to go on vacation with Barbie to 4 different locations: Iowa, Wyoming, Texas, and Florida. For each destination, you will play a different minigame, including horseback riding, setting up a tent, and even volleyball.","Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/Barbie Vacation Adventure (Prototype).png"
"snes_barkley-shut-up-and-jam","Barkley : Shut Up and Jam!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-01","1994","1990s","Accolade","Accolade","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","50","2.5","In the game you play street basketball two-on-two with your friends on with computer. So there are no referees, no fouls, and no shot clock, but the teams called by the names of cities such as Phoenix or Chicago. You can choose your player from 16 available players, including Barkley himself.","Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","miximages/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png","marquees/snes/Barkley Shut Up and Jam! (NA).png"
"snes_bass-masters-classic","Bass Masters Classic","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-01","1995","1990s","Black Pearl Software","Malibu Games","Fishing","Action","1","1","1","60","3","This game consists of five 3-day tournaments at four lakes. The mission is to catch as much large bass as possible. Once the day of the fishing tournament is over, the five largest bass that were caught get weighed and are compared to the other fisherman's catches. The one with the heaviest catch is the winner. The player starts off choosing an angler. Then the player can either go to the bait shop, practice pond, or start in the tournament. In the bait shop, choices of baits, lines, rods, reels, fish finders, and boat engines are available for purchase. The practice pond mode is just where the player can cut his teeth on the game mechanics without consequence.","Bass Masters Classic (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","miximages/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png","marquees/snes/Bass Masters Classic (NA).png"
"snes_bass-masters-classic-pro-edition","Bass Masters Classic : Pro Edition","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-11-01","1996","1990s","Black Pearl Software","Malibu Games","Fishing","Action","1","1","1","60","3","This game is basically an update to Bass Masters Classic. Updates involve adding 6 pro fishermen, five different lakes instead of four, an expanded lineup of different fishing equipment, 2 difficulty level settings, a zoom function, larger lakes, and new music tracks.","Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","miximages/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png","marquees/snes/Bass Masters Classic - Pro Edition (NA).png"
"snes_bassin-s-black-bass","Bassin's Black Bass","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Starfish","Hot-B","Fishing","Action","1","1","1","70","3.5","Bassin's Black Bass is a fishing game endorsed by the professional fishing champion Hank Parker. It is another entry in Hot-B's long line of fishing games. Players aim to become a professional fisherman and can travel by themselves or along with Hank.

There are four different fishing areas players advance to as they win each round of the tournament. Players travel by boat on the water, select a fishing spot and then select lure and cast fish. Certain types of fish are only available at specific spots. There is also a fish radar that makes a sound when there is fish nearby. Players need to select the correct type of lure (there are about 20), keep track of weather conditions and the limited time available in tournaments. Some fish fight back hard and need to be reeled in with careful timing.

The four tournaments are Local Amateur Tournament, Amateur Bass Championship, Pro Bass Tournament and Bassin' World Championship. About eight types of fish can be caught.","Bassin's Black Bass (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","miximages/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png","marquees/snes/Bassin's Black Bass (NA).png"
"snes_bastard-ankoku-no-hakaishin","Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakaishin","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-28","1994","1990s","Cobra Team","Cobra Team","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","40","2","In this game, you're either in the foreground or the background, and your opponent is always on the opposite end of the screen. To fight whoever flies thar yonder in the distance, you send out projectiles, in our case fireballs. These behave differently depending on which of the four main buttons you press: if you press the A button, they fly in a curve towards the upper right part of the screen, if you push the Y button, they go left and down and so on and so forth. Sadly, they move so slowly your opponent has a good three seconds to get out of the way, and in so weird curves that aiming is impossible. Also, the screen constantly twists in all directions.","Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).smc","SMC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Bastard!! - Ankoku no Hakai-shin (English Translated by DeJap, Rev 1).png"
"snes_batman","Batman","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1999-01-01","1999","1990s","Software Creations","Software Creations","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1","1","1","","","This unnamed prototype of Batman, also known as ""Real Shitty Batman!"", was a game in production by Software Creations. As its nickname suggests, it is very lacklustre.","Batman (Prototype).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","true","covers/snes/Batman (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Batman (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Batman (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Batman (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Batman (Prototype).png","","marquees/snes/Batman (Prototype).png"
"snes_batman-revenge-of-the-joker","Batman : Revenge of the Joker","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-12-20","1991","1990s","Ringler","Sunsoft","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","30","1.5","Batman is armed with a utility belt that allows him to collect various types of icons throughout the stages in order to shoot different types of baterangs and projectiles. The game's password feature also helped the game from becoming too difficult.
The music was composed by Naoki Kodaka, Hara Nobuyuki and Shinichi Seya.It also features several remixes of the themes from the Megaman series, most notable the opening theme being a remix of the boss select theme from Megaman 2.","Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/Batman - Revenge of the Joker (Prototype).png"
"snes_batman-forever","Batman Forever","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-28","1995","1990s","Probe Software","Acclaim Entertainment","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Batman Forever is the officially licensed game based on the 1995 movie. The bad guys this time around are The Riddler and Two-Face.

This game is an usual hybrid of side-scrolling platformer and one-on-one fighter. You make your way through levels that include some platform jumping elements, but you typically take on opponents one at a time like in a fighting game. You arsenal includes a lot of nifty bat-gadgets, and a collection of fighting moves that are unleashed with special button combinations.

The graphics were rendered completely with digitized actors and objects, but none of the actors from the movie make an appearance in the game.","Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Batman Forever (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_batman-returns","Batman Returns","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-05-01","1993","1990s","Konami","Konami","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1","1","1","90","4.5","The successful movie sequel Batman Returns spawned many different game adaptations. The SNES version presents the story of Batman's encounter with the Penguin and Catwoman as a side-scrolling beat'em'up, with one Batmobile driving sequence thrown in.

There are two varieties of the beat'em'up levels. One plays like Final Fight and similar games: Batman can move in two dimensions, beating up his enemies with various punches, kicks, combos and special moves.
Special gadgets to be used include the famous Batarang to take out enemies from a distance and ""Test Tubes"" filled with chemical explosives which will damage all enemies on the screen.

In the second type of side-scrolling level, Batman can only move in one dimension, left or right. The Batarang is the standard weapon here, with punches only being used when the enemy gets close. Also in these levels, Batman can use a grappling hook to cross dangerous areas unharmed.

Enemies in the beat'em'up levels usually belong to the Penguin's Red Triangle Circus Gang - clowns in all varieties: thin clowns, fat clowns, clowns on bikes, clowns armed with bazookas, and more. At the end of each level there is a boss fight, including several encounters with Catwoman and the Penguin.

In the Batmobile driving level, the action is viewed from behind. Batman has to evade clowns on bikes and shoot them down when he gets the chance. At the end of the road, another boss fight awaits.

Digitized images from the film illustrate the storyline.","Batman Returns (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","miximages/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png","marquees/snes/Batman Returns (NA).png"
"snes_battle-blaze","Battle Blaze","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-01","1993","1990s","Aircom","Sammy Studios","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","40","2","In the land of Virg, it is custom to select the King at the Tournament of Champions. Aspiring to rule the human world, a zealous demon from the Netherworld sends out five phantoms to posses each contender, so that the last survivor will be able to murder the king. However, one of the contenders, Durill manages to defeat the phantom that tries to take control of him, but dies from exhaustion. His son Kerrel takes Durill's place in the Tournament to avenge his father and kill the demon.

Battle Blaze is a one on one fighting game seen from a side-scrolling perspective. A jump and an attack button is all that is needed to control the six different characters. Each character uses a different weapon and has a few unique special attacks.

There are two modes of play: In ""Hero""-mode, the player can only control of Kerrel and must follow the game's story. In ""Battle""-mode, all characters are available and two-player fights are possible.","Battle Blaze (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","miximages/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png","marquees/snes/Battle Blaze (NA).png"
"snes_battle-cars","Battle Cars","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-11-01","1993","1990s","Malibu","Namco","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Cars takes place in the 22nd century.  In the century beforehand, the Greenhouse Effect has taken a toll on the world, the polar ice caps have melted, causing countries to begin to go underwater, as a result, battles for territory become more fierce, causing confrontations to become nuclear, forever altering the landscape.

You are a rookie driver, in the new blood sport known as Battle Cars, held by the IBCA (International Battle Cars Association) and your goal is to qualify for each race then take out your rivals and eventually face the top driver of that circuit in a final fight for supremacy.

There are nine levels in all, each with their own unique boss and the ability to upgrade your car and weapons.  The game resembles F-Zero in a way of gameplay, but the cars are more modeled after Formula One cars.","Battle Cars (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","miximages/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png","marquees/snes/Battle Cars (NA).png"
"snes_battle-clash","Battle Clash","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-03-18","1993","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Lightgun Shooter","Shooter","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Clash is a game that uses the Super Scope light gun peripheral. In this game, you fight in a futuristic version of the world as a warrior named Michael Anderson, who takes part in a competition simply known as the ""Battle Game"". Contestants in the Battle Game fight using mobile Standing Tanks (or STs for short), that come with a variety of weapon systems and forms.

To defeat the enemies in each stage, you must shot at weak spots on their armor to cause damage to them. The game scrolls automatically along with the opponent, so you cannot manually dodge bullets. You will have to play defensively by shooting them down.","Battle Clash (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","miximages/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png","marquees/snes/Battle Clash (NA).png"
"snes_battle-cross","Battle Cross","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-09","1994","1990s","A-Max","Imagineer","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-5","1","5","80","4","Battle Cross is a competitive racing game with a single-screen top-down perspective similar to RC Pro Am. It was released exclusively in Japan for the Super Famicom in 1994. The game exhibits a comedic element, with racers often encountering unusual objects on the course and comical cutscenes during the intro and post-race celebrations.Rather than motorcycles, the racers use a type of hoverbike that allows them to jump and turn around on the course more effectively. The game supports up to five players and uses a password system for progress.","Battle Cross (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Cross (JP).png"
"snes_battle-dodge-ball-toukyuu-daigekitotsu","Battle Dodge Ball - Toukyuu Daigekitotsu!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-07-20","1991","1990s","Banpresto","Banpresto","Sports / Dodgeball","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A crossover combat dodgeball game featuring characters from Mobile Suit Gundam, Ultraman and Kamen Rider. Similarly to Technos's Super Dodge Ball, each combatant has a health meter which depletes whenever they are hit by the dodgeball. Stronger throws cause more damage and once a character's HP is depleted, they leave the arena.","Battle Dodge Ball (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Dodge Ball (JP).png"
"snes_battle-dodge-ball-2","Battle Dodge Ball 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-23","1993","1990s","Banpresto","Banpresto","Sports / Dodgeball","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Dodge Ball II is the second Battle Dodge Ball in the Compati Hero Series and the fifth game from that series for the Super Famicom overall. It is the sequel to Banpresto's earlier Battle Dodge Ball.The game features characters from Kamen Rider, Ultraman and Gundam. Each team is comprised of heroes or villains from each of the three universes, and have their own array of special moves that can be used to damage the opposing team. The goal is to throw a dodgeball back and forth to reduce the HP of opposing team members. Once a whole team has been eliminated from play, the other side wins.Battle Dodge Ball II was the last game in the Dodge Ball sub-series until 2012's Battle Dodge Ball III for the PSP.","Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Dodge Ball II (JP).png"
"snes_battle-grand-prix","Battle Grand Prix","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-04-01","1993","1990s","Naxat Soft","Hudson Soft","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","80","4","Battle Grand Prix is a Formula One racing game in which one or two players can pit themselves in 3 Grand Prix races. The players can pick from 40 fictional drivers and 24 teams to play with. The game is always shown in split-screen mode, even when the player is playing on his own, in this case the opponent directly behind or ahead of the player is shown in the other window. Car settings can be adjusted in the garage. Pit-stops can be used to repair the car during a race. Crashing the car can also force the player to retire from the race early.

In the game the player can choose from four game modes:

1. Survival - Finish at or lower than a certain position (determined by your difficulty) to continue to the next race
Champion
VS
Slot - In this game mode steering happens automatically. The only thing the players need to concern themselves with is the gas and break pedals.

The game features three difficulty levels: Beginner, Pro and Expert.","Battle Grand Prix (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","miximages/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png","marquees/snes/Battle Grand Prix (NA).png"
"snes_battle-master-kyuukyoku-no-senshi-tachi","Battle Master - Kyuukyoku No Senshi-tachi","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-11-19","1993","1990s","System Vision","Toshiba EMI","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Master: Kyuukyoku no Senshitachi (""Battle Master: Ultimate Warrior"") is a sci-fi themed one-on-one fighter game exclusively for the Super Famicom. It was developed by System Vision, which previously worked on the SNES fighter Deadly Moves.The player can select between seven characters and play through a single-player mode with each, meeting different opponents and fighting a boss character at the end. There's also a Versus mode that allows two human players to compete with fighters of their choice.FightersSyoh - The standard main character. A well-rounded martial artist from Japan. Unsurprisingly, he fights like Street Fighter II's Ryu.Body - A robot that works as a policeman on a space colony. His limbs are detachable, and can fly off and attack enemies.Altia - A female air force officer with strong kicks. Very similar to Guile, only gender-swapped.Chan - A Chinese martial artist who prioritizes speed over strength.Ranmaru - A female Japanese martial artist who resembles Fatal Fury's Mai Shiranui. Another fast fighter who leaves little room for her opponents to recover.Wolvan - A lycanthrope warrior from Transylvania. Strong and faster than he looks.Jian - A fierce pirate warrior with a metal jaw. Formidable but slow.Watts - A large wrestler with spiky hair and a distinctive yellow and white costume.Zero - The game's boss and normally unplayable. An alien warrior from beyond the stars, and resembles DBZ's Cell to some extent.","Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Master - Kyuukyoku no Senshi-tachi (JP).png"
"snes_battle-pinball","Battle Pinball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-24","1995","1990s","Banpresto","Banpresto","Pinball","Board / Casual","1","1","1","80","4","Battle Pinball is a multi-table pinball game exclusive for the Super Famicom and part of Banpresto's ""Compati Hero Series"" that brings together the licenses for Ultraman, Kamen Rider and Gundam. As with other games in the series, all the characters have been super-deformed to normalize the heights of the differently-sized heroes.

The game contains four tables, each themed to a different hero: Kamen Rider, who fights in dusty wastelands with a number of his recurring enemies; Ultraman, who fights in a city against various kaiju; Knight Gundam, who fights in space; and Banpresto's own original character Fighter Roar, whose stage is set under the ocean. Each table also has three screens, each with their own set of paddles, and the player attempts to earn highscores from the various features on each table.","Battle Pinball (JP).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Pinball (JP).png"
"snes_battle-racers","Battle Racers","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-17","1995","1990s","Minato Giken","Banpresto","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Racers is a Mode 7-enabled racing game and part of the Compati Hero Series, which combines characters from Gundam, Kamen Rider and Ultraman. The game takes after Super Mario Kart in its presentation and use of power-ups to get the edge in races. It also uses a similar zoomed-out view of the track for the second player's window for the game's single-player mode.Players select from four Grand Prix cups: each cup has one more track than the one before, starting with four. The final cup is locked until the others have been beaten.","Battle Racers (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Racers (JP).png"
"snes_battle-soccer-field-no-hasha","Battle Soccer - Field No Hasha","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-11","1992","1990s","Pandora Box","Banpresto","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","Battle Soccer: Field no Hasha (""Champions of the Field"") is a soccer game from Pandora Box and Banpresto that is part of the Compati Hero Series of crossover games. It features characters from the Gundam, Kamen Rider and Ultraman franchises, as with previous Compati Hero games, but also includes characters from Toho's Godzilla universe such as King Ghidorah, Rodan and Godzilla himself.The game uses a horizontal view of the pitch, with the two teams on either side. Depending on the character and their original series, each player has a special ability which increases their effectiveness on the pitch in some way. The game was followed up with Battle Soccer 2.","Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Battle Soccer - Field no Hasha (English Translated by Hurkey Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
"snes_battle-soccer-2","Battle Soccer 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-25","1994","1990s","Pandora Box","Banpresto","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Battle Soccer 2 is a Compati Hero Series soccer game for the Super Famicom and the sequel to 1992's Battle Soccer. It once again pits soccer teams of superheroes and villains from the universes of Gundam, Kamen Rider and Ultraman against each other in an SD format that standardizes everyone's heights.Matches are played with a horizontal perspective of the pitch and the various characters have specific strengths and weaknesses, as well as special abilities. The teams are sorted into three leagues: USER, A League and B League. There is also an all-stars team available in the versus mode as well as a special hidden team comprised of sheep.Like all games in the Compati Hero Series, the game was never localized into English.","Battle Soccer 2 (JP).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","miximages/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png","marquees/snes/Battle Soccer 2 (JP).png"
