"snes_chaos-seed-fuusui-kairoki","Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairoki","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-03-15","1996","1990s","Neverland","Taito","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","80","4","In this action-strategy-RPG hybrid you play the role of a young member of a cave-dwelling race of warriors. As the planet is dying, it is your task to build the necessary mystic rooms in your caves to channel the energy back to the planet, but the surface-dwellers think that you are to blame for the current situation and constantly send waves of explorers and adventurers trying to clear your ""dungeon"".","Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","miximages/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","screenshots/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","titlescreens/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","3dboxes/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","backcovers/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png","marquees/snes/Chaos Seed - Fuusui Kairouki (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.02).png"
"snes_chester-cheetah-too-cool-to-fool","Chester Cheetah : Too Cool to Fool","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-02","1992","1990s","Kaneko","Kaneko","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","Put yourself in his paws. You're a cool cat. So you would like to be treated like that. But no! You're stuck in a zoo with nothing to do. Mean Eugene, the Zoo Keepin' Fiend has planned an ugly scene. He wants to nail your tail without fail. So, he steals your wheels to cool your heels. Then he hides the parts in the zoo just to fool you. And that's just the beginning in Eugene's way of winning. But, stay cool and you can rule. Just find your bike and you can go where you like. The rule is cool. Remember a cool cat is just... TOO COOL TO FOOL!","Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","miximages/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png","marquees/snes/Chester Cheetah - Too Cool to Fool (NA).png"
"snes_chester-cheetah-wild-wild-quest","Chester Cheetah : Wild Wild Quest","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-02","1994","1990s","Kaneko","Kaneko","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","It's a groove to be on the move. But, if you're Chester Cheetah, ""chill is the pill."" So, when you are spotted in ""Not-So-Great-Falls, Montana,"" ""Unclearwater, Florida,"" and ""Little Shock, Arkansas"" you follow the ""law of the paw,"" and run for the fun. Pity, these cities can't spot a cat that knows where it's at. They want to bring you down in their town. Now, follow your feet and take to the street. It's cool to make the rules. ""Wild, Wild Quest"" features a full 8 Meg, 16 bit platform of adventure. Cool characters and wild-style graphics make this trip a ""Wild, Wild Quest"" of the best!","Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","miximages/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png","marquees/snes/Chester Cheetah - Wild Wild Quest (NA).png"
"snes_chibi-maruko-chan-mezase-minami-no-island","Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami No Island!!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-01","1995","1990s","Konami","Konami","Unknown","Unknown","1-4","1","4","80","4","Chibi Maruko-Chan: Mezase! Minami no Island!! (""Chibi Maruko-Chan: Take Aim! South Island!!"") is an anime licensed game based on Chibi Maruko-Chan, a slice-of-life manga about a grade-schooler, her friends and her family.

The game is a loose collection of different mini-games and utilities intended for a younger audience, a dodgeball game set on a Bomberman grid, a painting game that plays like competitive Snake, and a sumo-like game on a floating platform in a pool.  It also features a single-player story mode, in which interstitial text-heavy story cutscenes set the stage for the mini-games, as well as modes that let up to four players try each mini-game directly.","Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","miximages/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png","marquees/snes/Chibi Maruko-chan - Mezase! Minami no Island!! (JP).png"
"snes_chinhai","Chinhai","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-22","1995","1990s","Pixel","Banpresto","Asiatic board game","Board / Casual","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Chinhai is a puzzle game for the Super Famicom and a variant on Mahjong Solitaire, or Shanghai. Instead of pairing and removing any two tiles that match as long as one of their sides is clear, as is the case in Shanghai, the rules of Chinhai require that the two tiles can only be removed if it's possible to draw a line between the two that doesn't make a 90 degree turn more than twice. Two tiles on opposite sides of the board, for instance, can't be paired because it would take three 90 degree turns to draw a line between them. To account for this added difficulty, the game lacks the ""stacks"" of tiles that frequently appear in Shanghai, instead presenting a flat board of tiles.Chinhai is named for the municipality of Chinhai in China, maintaining the theme of naming mahjong solitaire variants after territories in China. Chinhai has long since been renamed Zhenhai, which is situated in the Ningpo Prefecture and now part of a much larger city. This mahjong solitaire variant is perhaps best known as Sichuan or Shisenshou, also named for a province in China.","Chinhai (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","miximages/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Chinhai (JP).png","marquees/snes/Chinhai (JP).png"
"snes_choplifter-iii","Choplifter III","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-08-01","1994","1990s","Beam Software","Ocean","Shoot'em Up / Horizontal","Shooter","1","1","1","60","3","Choplifter III for the SNES features entirely different levels from the Game Gear and GameBoy versions, and plays slightly different than other Choplifter games. While the controls and basic gameplay haven't changed significantly, and the main goal of each level is still rescuing a certain number of hostages, Choplifter III is faster and more action-oriented than earlier Choplifter games.

Enemies appear in larger variety and numbers now. They reappear more often and some levels feature fights against large bosses. To compensate for this, there are more power-ups (such as missiles, bombs, invisibility, etc.) available, and it is possible to have the helicopter repaired at certain points in a level.","Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","miximages/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png","marquees/snes/Choplifter III - Rescue-Survive (NA).png"
