"snes_daikaijuu-monogatari","Daikaijuu Monogatari","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-22","1994","1990s","Birthday","Hudson Soft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","The game is a traditional console-style RPG with random battles and turn-based combat, viewed from semi-third-person ""over-the-shoulder"" perspective. There are a lot of party members to recruit, the Shell heroes as well as optional, secret characters. The party members you find might have a unique skill which can be required to solve puzzles. At one point in the game, you'll also be able to build your own town.","Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","miximages/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","screenshots/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","titlescreens/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","3dboxes/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","backcovers/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png","marquees/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Hotfix 9 Patch).png"
"snes_daikaijuu-monogatari-2","Daikaijuu Monogatari 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-08-02","1996","1990s","Birthday","Hudson Soft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","Daikaijuu Monogatari series has been described as a Dragon Quest clone in the past due to its similarities. However, Daikaijuu Monogatari is notably more humourous than Dragon Quest, and has a bizarre story to match.","Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).sfc","SFC","6","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","miximages/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","screenshots/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","titlescreens/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","3dboxes/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","backcovers/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png","marquees/snes/Daikaijuu Monogatari II (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 0.9).png"
"snes_darius-twin","Darius Twin","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-04-22","1992","1990s","Taito","Taito","Shoot'em Up / Horizontal","Shooter","1-2","1","2","60","3","The evil alliance under the leadership of Belser is attacking your world; as pilot of a Silver Hawk fighter, your goal is to stop them! Darius Twin is a side scrolling shooter similar to other games in the Darius series. You'll fly through a series of different stages, each with numerous enemies and ending with a large boss. You begin the game with a limited amount of fire power, but after shooting certain enemies power ups are left behind to increase your capabilities. Unlike many of the Darius games, if your ship is destroyed you don't lose all of the weapons you've collected; likewise you also continue on at the same point rather than being sent back to a checkpoint. Similar to many of the Darius games there are different routes that can be taken towards the final stage. At the end of each stage, you are shown a map and can select the next stage you would like to complete.","Darius Twin (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","miximages/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png","marquees/snes/Darius Twin (NA).png"
"snes_dark-half","Dark Half","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-05-31","1996","1990s","Westone","Enix","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","80","4","The game is quite different from most other RPGs, because it lets you play as both the ""good"" hero and the villain. The game is divided into chapters, and each chapter is played by either Roda or Rukyu. You switch the two heroes chapter-after-chapter, and lead them to their ultimate battle from two opposite sides. The entire game, including the battles, is viewed from an isometric perspective. The battles are random and turn-based, and the battle field is divided into squares you can position and move your characters on. Both Roda and Rukyu can have party members join them, and both attack mostly with magic. Rukyu uses a special energy called ""Soul Power"", while Roda cast one-time spells from scrolls he receives after battles.","Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Dark Half (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_dark-law-the-meaning-of-death","Dark Law - The Meaning Of Death","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1997-03-28","1997","1990s","ASCII","ASCII","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Three-hundred years ago, there were two kingdoms on the earth - Reyfore, ruled by the benevolent and mighty king Reynard, and Amugnon, ruled by king Daruk. The two kingdoms could have lived in peace, but pride took over king Daruk, he didn't respect the Goddess any more, and as a result his mind became obsessed by the idea of destruction. Daruk attacked Reyfore, and for decades war was embracing the two kingdoms, setting the Earth in flames. After a prolonged battle, king Reynard defeated king Daruk, and a reign of prosperity began once again.

But can evil be truly defeated? The hero of the game, a young adventurer, falls down from a cliff while taking a stroll near the ancient Sophia Temple. He is saved by an old man who lives with his daughter Refia in a small country house. But one day, a demonic-looking knight appears, accompanied by monsters, kills the old man, and kidnaps Refia. The old man recognized the knight - it was king Daruk! Now the hero must bring Refia back and defeat Daruk once and for all.","Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Dark Law - Meaning of Death (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_date-kimiko-no-virtual-tennis","Date Kimiko No Virtual Tennis","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-05-13","1994","1990s","Jorudan","B-AI","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","Become one of the best female tennis players; like Date Kimiko. In the world tournament mode, there is an elaborate story line to participate in as the player tries to become the world's most premier tennis champ.

In addition to the world tournament mode, there is also an exhibition and a ""normal"" tournament mode. Passwords using Japanese characters are required to resume a player's progress within the course of the world tournament. Tournaments themselves are ranked in a series of letter grades to help determine the strength of the opponents.","Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","miximages/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png","marquees/snes/Date Kimiko no Virtual Tennis (JP).png"
"snes_david-crane-s-amazing-tennis","David Crane's Amazing Tennis","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-01","1992","1990s","Imagineering","Absolute Entertainment","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","A tennis game featuring both an exhibition and a tournament mode. In the head to head exhibition mode, the player can choose their opponent (with the higher ranked opponents being better than the lower ranked ones). The tournament mode allows for 16 different players to compete to become the champion. Both of these modes can be played on either grass, hard, or a clay court. The player can also choose whether to be either a left or right handed tennis player.

During the game, whoever is serving the ball will be closer to the screen. Players will be able to use topspin, execute drop shots, perform volleys, lobs, slices and overhead smashes, and decide on either a hard or soft serve to keep the opponent off balance.

Although the game features fictional players, it is endorsed by Footlocker.","David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","miximages/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","screenshots/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","backcovers/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png","marquees/snes/David Crane's Amazing Tennis (NA).png"
