"snes_demolition-man","Demolition Man","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-28","1995","1990s","Alexandria","Acclaim Entertainment","Platform / Shooter Scrolling","Shooter","1","1","1","50","2.5","Demolition Man is a fast-paced platform game, with some levels taking place in an overhead perspective (similar to Smash TV). As John Spartan, the player will spend most of his time shooting endless waves of ""cryocons"" released upon the antiseptic world of the future. The standard weapon can be upgraded with a shotgun and magnum, both with limited ammo. Additionally, various types of grenades can be collected, which are thrown independently of the firearm. Simon Phoenix appears as a boss character at the end of every level that is directly tied in with the movie.","Demolition Man (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","miximages/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png","marquees/snes/Demolition Man (NA).png"
"snes_demon-s-crest","Demon's Crest","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-01","1995","1990s","Capcom","Capcom","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","90","4.5","The Demon's Crest consists of six magical stones that when combined will give the holder power to rule all. The inhabitants of the Demon Realm had long fought for possession of this infinite power until one gargoyle emerged victorious: the red demon known as Firebrand. At the end of his final battle, Firebrand was ambushed by his nemesis, Phalanx, and the stones are stolen.

Now, Firebrand must recover the stones before humans and demons fall under Phalanx's sinister rule. Breathe fire, claw walls, and hover across seven intense levels of the Demon Realm. Gain new transformations and abilities as you fight your way through Phalanx's forces. With three different endings to experience, twenty-five hidden items to discover, and six magical stones of cosmic power to control, can you save the realms from total anarchy?","Demon's Crest (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","miximages/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png","marquees/snes/Demon's Crest (NA).png"
"snes_dennis","Dennis","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Ocean","Ocean","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","This game brings the US cartoon character Dennis the Menace's menacing treatment of poor old Mr. Wilson to the computer screen, around the time of the film also released in 1993. 

Dennis is a platform game across settings such as Sewers, the Park, and Mr. Wilson’s house, each of which has an end-of-level bad guy. The in-level opposition follows standard attack patterns and can usually be avoided. The enemies regenerate as you move on, although you generally only progress from bottom-left to top-right anyway. 

Some of the holes in the ground are deadly while others lead to secret rooms. Fortunately, you are given up to 9 lives at the start, and lots of extra lives are on offer throughout the game, so these are well worth checking out. A choice between music and sound effects is offered.","Dennis the Menace (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","miximages/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png","marquees/snes/Dennis the Menace (NA).png"
"snes_ogre-battle-the-march-of-the-black-queen","Ogre Battle : The March of the Black Queen","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-03","1995","1990s","Quest","Enix","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Lead the rebellion and lead your army to reclaim your land from the evil Zenobian Empire in this real-time strategy game. Free towns and collect items and tarot cards to increase your strength, as well as recruit allies to join you in your crusade. But when you finally win the day, will you be a benevolent leader, or an become an evil despot yourself? You must decide.","Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","miximages/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","screenshots/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","titlescreens/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","3dboxes/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","backcovers/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png","marquees/snes/Densetsu no Ogre Battle - The March of the Black Queen (English Translated by FinS, Rev 1.2 - Nintendo Power Version With Extra Features).png"
"snes_langrisser-der","Langrisser, Der","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-30","1995","1990s","Masaya Games","Nippon Computer Systems (NCS)","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","60","3","""Der Langrisser"" is a sequel to the game that was known outside of Japan as  Warsong, and is in fact a remake of the Genesis game  Langrisser II.

The Blue Dragon Knights of the Rayguard Empire are trying to steal the virtuous sorceress Riana. Their goal is to make her serve the evil Emperor Bozel, whose intention is to find the most powerful evil sword in the land, the Alhazard. Naturally, the main hero of the game, the knight Elwin, has not only to rescue Riana, but to retrieve the legendary holy sword Langrisser, the only artifact that is more powerful than Alhazard. At first, Elwin automatically follows the ""good"" path, but as the game progresses, he can choose whether to continue being good, to become an ally with Rayguard, or to side with the forces of Chaos whose goal is utter destruction.

""Der Langrisser"" features a complex character creation process, during which you must answer morally ambiguous questions,  Ultima-style, that will determine the class and the attributes of the main hero. The game is a strategy RPG in which you control your party only during battles. You just hire soldiers and unite them under commands of generals. When your units attack the enemy, an automatic battle sequence occurs. You can also use magic spells to heal your troops or to harm enemy soldiers.","Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","miximages/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","screenshots/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","backcovers/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png","marquees/snes/Der Langrisser (English Translated by Byuu and Derrick Sobodash, Rev 1.3.1).png"
"snes_desert-strike-return-to-the-gulf","Desert Strike : Return to the Gulf","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-05-27","1993","1990s","Visual Concepts","Electronic Arts","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","70","3.5","The first game in the Strike series. A year after the Gulf War, a self styled General named Kilbaba (Muababa in the GBA version) takes over an Arab Emirate and threatens to start WWIII against its enemy in the West, the U.S. The whole world holds their breath as the President chooses you to destroy Kilbaba and his terrorist army before he launches a nuclear attack on the world!

You must fly an specially designed AH-64A Apache on a series of missions from rescuing MIAs, destroying power plants, to blowing apart SCUD missles! Take out his defenses while trying to find out Kilbaba's plans. Take on the Madman and forever rid this threat....in the desert!","Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","miximages/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png","marquees/snes/Desert Strike - Return to the Gulf (NA).png"
"snes_dharma-doujou","Dharma Doujou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-10","1995","1990s","Metro Corporation","Den'Z","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","70","3.5","A puzzle game starring a monk  and a shrine maiden who must knock away all the blocks to clear the stage. The time limit, rather than being represented by a traditional clock, is a demon, located on the left of the screen, climbing up a rope and must be stopped before it reaches the ceiling and releases a large temple bell onto the player's blocks. The player must use his or her giant mallet to hit blocks and make them fall into a chamber beneath the playing field. If three identical blocks (or up to eight later in the game) are aligned correctly inside the compartment, they turn into a good spirit and perform a short strike attack against the demon, thus slowing it down. The game is over if you run out of time, or allow one of the block towers to reach the top of the screen.","Dharma Doujou (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","miximages/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png","marquees/snes/Dharma Doujou (JP).png"
"snes_dig-amp-spike-volleyball","Dig &amp; Spike Volleyball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-01","1993","1990s","TOSE","Hudson Soft","Sports / Volleyball","Sports","1","1","1","80","4","Dig &amp; Spike Volleyball is a volleyball game where the player can choose from two different venues, each with their own rules.  They can either choose an indoor event, where they can choose from International teams and compete in a 6 v. 6 person squad with rules and regulations, giving it an authentic feel.  There is also a 2 vs. 2 beach game where the gameplay is more relaxed which is ideal for beginners to the game, letting them learn the basic's such as serving, spiking and blocking.

There is also a practice mode that shows players the controls of the game and helps get them started. The game also contains two-player action that allows friends to compete head-to-head.","Dig &amp; Spike Volleyball (NA).sfc","SFC","0","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","","","","","","",""
"snes_digimon-adventure","Digimon Adventure","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1998-01-01","1998","1990s","Sintax","Unknown","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","","","Digimon Adventure uses the same engine as Pocket Monster, thus the gameplay mechanics are the same. The game spans six levels, which include two boss fights. The only playable character is Agumon. The player can attack enemies by pressing A to shoot a fireball. Each level has a strawberry item that, once collected, transforms that the player into Greymon, who's fireball attack is much larger and stronger. Once the player loses all their lives, they're given a chance to continue, which seems to have no limit.","Digimon Adventure (TW).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","false","true","true","covers/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png","miximages/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png","screenshots/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png","titlescreens/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png","3dboxes/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png","","marquees/snes/Digimon Adventure (TW).png"
"snes_dino-dini-s-soccer","Dino Dini's Soccer!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-29","1995","1990s","Eurocom","Virgin Interactive","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-8","1","8","80","4","Although this soccer game is based on Goal, it features a very different control method, with the ball on sticking to your foot to make running with the ball easier. The game is viewed from above and played up and down the pitch, without the left-to-right option of Goal. Matches can be between 2 or 20 minutes, and the scanner which shows player positions on the rest of the pitch can be moved, resized or disabled. The method of triggering aftertouch onto a pass can be chosen as well.

The game features a variety of tournaments, including the full World Cup and its qualifying rounds, and the European Championships, or individual matches, as well as being able to configure a league or cup tournament ot your own specification. Only international teams are included, although their names can be customised. Unlike most soccer games of the time, player ethnicities are accurately depicted.","Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Dino Dini's Soccer! (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_dinocity","Dinocity","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-01","1992","1990s","Smart Egg Pictures","Irem","Platform","Platformer","2","1","2","70","3.5","Timmy and his friend, Jaime go into Timmy's father's lab to watch a movie on the big screen. Little do they know that the big screen is actually a time machine. When Timmy touches the remote they are both sucked into prehistoric times. Now in order to return home they must reclaim a time machine fuse that was stolen by some Neanderthals who go by the name of ""The Rockies"". The Rockies are also looking to destroy DinoCity, so two dinosaurs named Rex and Tops decide to help out the two human children in their fight against the Neanderthals.

Gameplay involves one of the humans riding one of the dinosaurs throughout six stages, To defeat the enemies, the player can jump on top of them, punch them, or throw projectiles at them. Defeated enemies may drop eggs. It is also possible for the human to dismount the dinosaur. The human will now be able to jump and reach higher places. 

The player can only take three hits before losing a life. Energy can be refilled by finding hearts throughout a level. Time is of the essence as all stages are timed.","DinoCity (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","miximages/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","screenshots/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","backcovers/snes/DinoCity (NA).png","marquees/snes/DinoCity (NA).png"
"snes_dirt-racer","Dirt Racer","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-05-01","1995","1990s","Motivetime","Elite Systems","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","40","2","Players must drive their dirt track racing vehicle across a road rally. The car comes complete with a speedometer (in kilometres), a lap counter, and a lap time counter. There are a pre-determined number of chances to complete the game, like in Super Mario Kart and F-Zero. If the player is unable to defeat the game in those number of tries, then the player gets an automatic game over. A yellow smiley face shows up to track the performance of the driver. If it's smiling, then the player is winning. Otherwise, the player is losing the game.","Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Dirt Racer (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_dirt-trax-fx","Dirt Trax FX","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Sculptured Software","Acclaim Entertainment","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","60","3","Dirt Trax Fx is a Motocross racing game in which players can choose between 8 drivers, differing in top speed, acceleration, turning and braking, to compete on 22 different tracks. The tracks feature lots of mounds, ramps, pits and other similar obstacles. Parts of a track might also be covered in water mud or ice. To successfully navigate the motorbike through these obstacles, the shoulder buttons can be used to lean the bike, while pressing up or down changes the angle of the bike. Before the game begins, players can choose between four different bikes ranging from 50cc to 500cc (the last bike needs to be unlocked first). The choice of the bike greatly affects the game's speed as well as the overall difficulty.

Apart from the main race, there is also a ""Rad Tag"" mode. In this mode, a timer ticks down that can only be stopped by hitting the opponent. The driver whose timer reaches zero first loses.","Dirt Trax FX (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","miximages/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png","marquees/snes/Dirt Trax FX (NA).png"
"snes_dokapon-3-2-1-arashi-o-yobu-yuujou","Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi O Yobu Yuujou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-02","1994","1990s","Asmik","Asmik","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-5","1","5","90","4.5","Dokapon 3-2-1: Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (""Dokapon 3-2-1: Fellowship of the Summoners of the Storm"") is the second Dokapon game for the Super Famicom as well as the second overall. Like it predecessor, it follows a group of ragtag RPG heroes across a board game-like map as they attempt to outmaneuver each other and be the first to save the kingdom. Characters move around determinant on dice rolls and can acquire items and treasure depending on where they land. They can also get into fights with random monsters which raises their levels, though being defeated will cause them to lose a turn.

Dokapon Kingdom, the PS2 and Wii game released in 2008 for the US market, is an enhanced remake of this game in particular.","Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","miximages/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","screenshots/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","titlescreens/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","3dboxes/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","backcovers/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png","marquees/snes/Dokapon 3-2-1 - Arashi o Yobu Yuujou (English Translated by Krokodyl, Rev 1.4).png"
