"snes_super-swiv","Super SWIV","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-08-19","1993","1990s","SCi Games","Time Warner Interactive","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","A secret ""underground race"" on an island in the mid-Atlantic ocean has stolen top military vehicles and equipment from around the world and have rebuilt powerful war machines from them. They are even piloted by robots with one mission: to destroy. Military and intelligence officials of major countries have detected them, but they are afraid that they will be unable to prepare against an assault by this underground race. Their plan is to send you in to infiltrate their base and destroy them before they have a chance to unleash the war upon the rest of the world. You can choose to control either the Helicoptor or the Jeep, which have different advantages -- for instance, the Helicoptor is not obstructed by obstacles but the Jeep can fire in any direction. There are nine weapons (five permanent, four short-lasting specials) you can pick up along the way to help you destroy the enemy.","Firepower 2000 (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","miximages/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png","marquees/snes/Firepower 2000 (NA).png"
"snes_firestriker","FireStriker","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-10-01","1994","1990s","Axes Art Amuse","DTMC","Action / Breakout games","Action","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Long ago, on a distant world, there were four kingdoms governed by four constantly fighting tribes. One day the Arch-Mage Wylde proposed a solution to the constant battling. He offered to bestow the title of FireStriker and ruler of the four tribes, upon the one who could control the elemental orb of fire known as the Trialight.

However, the Arch-Mage Wylde had a hidden purpose. His true reason for bringing peace to the world was so he could destroy all four kingdoms at once.

Now, Slader, the last FireStriker, has offered to risk his life in the battle against Wylde and his monsters. The time has come to release the Trialight. The world's future hangs in the balance.","FireStriker (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","miximages/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","screenshots/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","backcovers/snes/FireStriker (NA).png","marquees/snes/FireStriker (NA).png"
"snes_fireteam-rogue","Fireteam Rogue","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Accolade","Accolade","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","FireTeam Rogue was a promising action-adventure game developed by Accolade, set for release sometime between 1994-1996. Issues with development led to its cancellation.
The Talis is under the control of the evil lords of Umbra, and FireTeam Rogue's the only band of heroes that's capable of rescuing it. The latest on this long-awaited cart is that it's still on the way. Guide a unique team of five characters through game play that includes action/adventure, hand-to-hand combat, aerial battle, puzzle solving, and maze exploration. The fantasy story line takes through five worlds in more than 100 hours of game play! More soon!","Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/Fireteam Rogue (Prototype).png"
"snes_first-samurai","First Samurai","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","Vivid Image","Kemco","Platform / Fighter Scrolling","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","""The Demon King descends from his mountain domain to you, the First Samurai and your master. Defenceless against the Demon's magic, you succumb to his powers, leaving the your Master to fight the Demon King alone. Your master falls in combat, dying. But, with his last breath, he summons the Wizard Mage. The Demon King fearing defeat, escapes to the future. Tormented, the young samurai seeks the teachings of the Wizard Mage in the ways of magic. Boiled with revenge and armed with a magical sword, the young samurai follows the Demon King into the future and the unknown...""","First Samurai (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","miximages/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","screenshots/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","backcovers/snes/First Samurai (NA).png","marquees/snes/First Samurai (NA).png"
"snes_flashback","Flashback","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-11-01","1993","1990s","Delphine Software International","U.S. Gold","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","90","4.5","The year is 2142. You play as Conrad B. Hart, a man who has lost his memory. After barely escaping from hostile aliens, Conrad's bike crashes on an unknown planet. Conrad finds himself in the jungle, and from now on his quest for survival and his lost identity begins.

""Flashback"" is a platform action adventure. You navigate Conrad over the platforms, performing various moves. You can simply jump or do a longer run-and-jump, run, climb, hang off ledges, and pick up objects lying on the ground. To defend yourself, you shoot enemies with your gun and also use various objects (such as stones) to harm or to distract them.","Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","miximages/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png","marquees/snes/Flashback - The Quest for Identity (NA).png"
"snes_flying-hero-bugyuru-no-daibouken","Flying Hero - Bugyuru No Daibouken","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-18","1992","1990s","Sting Entertainment","SOFEL","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1","1","1","70","3.5","Flying Hero: Bugyuru no Daibouken (""Bugyuru's Great Adventure"") is a vertically scrolling shoot 'em up with a bright, cartoony style, similar to games like Sega's Fantasy Zone or Konami's TwinBee series. The game features Bugyuru, a flying ball, whose girlfriend Pao gets kidnapped by a mysterious vampire named Biblio. Bugyuru takes off in close pursuit, flying across various worlds and taking down bizarre enemies like flying monkeys and giant rhino beetles.

The game was released exclusively in Japan for the Super Famicom and never officially localized, however a fan translation patch exists.","Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Flying Hero - Bugyuru no Daibouken (English Translated by KingMike's Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
"snes_football-fury","Football Fury","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-10-01","1993","1990s","Aicom Corporation","Sammy USA Corporation","Sports / Football (American)","Sports","1-2","1","2","40","2","An American Football game featuring fictional teams. Even though, the teams are fictional, they are based on the real NFL team cities and the division setup of that time period. 

Gameplay involves the usual football fare of choosing a play and attempting to execute it. On offense, when throwing the football, a meter appears which measures the strength of the passer's throw. On defense, the player controls a defensive player and tries to tackle the opposing ball carrier or attempt to break up a pass play.

There is an option to either play with the background music on or off.","Football Fury (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","miximages/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Football Fury (NA).png","marquees/snes/Football Fury (NA).png"
"snes_foreman-for-real","Foreman for Real","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-28","1995","1990s","Software Creations","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Boxing","Sports","1-2","1","2","40","2","Before marketing fat-reducing grills, boxing legend George Foreman endorsed this boxing game with a 3rd-person view similar to Punch Out. A full range of punches and jabs are available, with full round-by-round scoring from the judges. You can choose from 3-12 rounds, and there are 2 camera views as well as an automatic switch option.

You can play simple exhibition matches or tournaments, or launch into a career mode. In this you start at the bottom of the world rankings, and must reach the top by beating the game's players, who other than George are all fictitious boxers with varying strengths and weaknesses. The coach gives advice before each fight to help highlight weaknesses in your play. There are passwords for each round.","Foreman for Real (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","miximages/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png","marquees/snes/Foreman for Real (NA).png"
"snes_frank-thomas-big-hurt-baseball","Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Iguana Entertainment","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Baseball","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","Baseball player Frank Thomas licensed one of the first baseball games to use motion-captured 3D graphics and multiple camera angles. 28 stadia have been rendered in 3D, and each has its own visible and significant differences. 

There are 6 distinct game modes, allowing for single exhibition matches as well as full seasons on the playoffs. There is also a Home Run Derby mode, allowing the player to pelt the ball out of the ground for points. 

You can choose pitchers and batters, based on their statistics and form. Bowling options are set to define the delivery, with fastballs and curveballs as well as more precise setting of pace and height. Fielding is optional - it's best to leave this to the computer at first You can always alter the infield and outfield depth and shift.","Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","miximages/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png","marquees/snes/Frank Thomas Big Hurt Baseball (NA).png"
"snes_frantic-flea","Frantic Flea","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-01-01","1996","1990s","Haus Teknikka","GameTek","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","50","2.5","Frantic Flea has the player take control of a flea warrior out to protect his Flea World from an evil race of bugs called the Insideos who are enslaving his friends and family.

The gameplay is a side-scroller with six different levels, where the player explores each stage, freeing fellow captive fleas and utilizing weapons and devices to aid in their missions such as the Hoverpod which enables flight and the Stikko which can give the player the ability to climb walls.  The graphics are done up in a cartoon-style with over 100 animation frames to give sprites a more fluid life-like feel.","Frantic Flea (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","miximages/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png","marquees/snes/Frantic Flea (NA).png"
"snes_frogger","Frogger","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1998-01-01","1998","1990s","Morning Star Multimedia","Majesco","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Your task in this arcade conversion is to guide a frog across a treacherous road and river, and to safety at the top of the screen. Both these sections are fraught with a variety of hazards, each of which will kill the frog and cost you a life if contact is made.

The road is full of cars and trucks, at variable speeds. The river water itself is fatal, as are the snakes which hover within on later levels. Frogger must use the arrangement of logs, turtles (which are only there for a short time) and alligators (but stay away form their faces), and then jump into one of the open home-cells, ideally one containing a fly for extra points. Once all holes have been filled, you move onto the next, harder, level.","Frogger (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Frogger (NA).png","miximages/snes/Frogger (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Frogger (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Frogger (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Frogger (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Frogger (NA).png","marquees/snes/Frogger (NA).png"
"snes_front-mission","Front Mission","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-24","1995","1990s","G-Craft","Squaresoft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","80","4","Front Mission is a turn-based strategy RPG played from an isometric perspective. Players progress in the story through large strategic battles involving multiple units and cutscenes between them. At certain places, usually towns, the player can visit multiple locations including bars and shops, and talk to people. Shops are also found to purchase new weaponry, parts and make repairs. In combat, each Wanzer robot is a seperate unit and, has a large variety of weapons and attack styles depending on what's equipped. Units get experience for defeating enemies and also for using various techniques. The more an attack is used, the more powerful it becomes.","Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","miximages/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","screenshots/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","titlescreens/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","3dboxes/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","backcovers/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png","marquees/snes/Front Mission (English Translated by Frank Hughes, Rev 1b With Fixes by MTeam, Rev 1.02).png"
"snes_front-mission-gun-hazard","Front Mission - Gun Hazard","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-02-23","1996","1990s","Omiya Soft","Squaresoft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","60","3","Gun Hazard is a side-scrolling version of the Front Mission series. Gameplay is more than a little similar to Cybernator or Metal Warriors. The player moves between different stages on an area map. Once inside a level, the player controls Albert and his wanzer as it moves left, right across the screen. The wanzer is able to jump, and attack with a primary and a special weapon. At any time during a level, Albert can eject from his heavily armed wanzer to explore. Although vulnerable when outside, Albert is able to reach areas the wanzer cannot, in addition to being a smaller target and being able to jetpack indefinitely.","Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","miximages/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","screenshots/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","titlescreens/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","3dboxes/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","backcovers/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","marquees/snes/Front Mission Series - Gun Hazard (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png"
"snes_full-throttle-all-american-racing","Full Throttle : All-American Racing","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-16","1994","1990s","Gremlin Interactive","GameTek","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Full Throttle Racing takes in chaotic, violent and no-holds-barred racing across America. The racing takes place on both land and water, with motorbikes and boats featured.

There are 6 racers in the game, each with their own short profile, including a guy newly out of jail, a token woman and a token African-American. You can race one or both disciplines, racing either an individual weekend or a full championship.

As you play the game you can upgrade your vehicle, with faster engines, better tyres and more nitros. As in Road Rash, throwing your legs out at the other riders is potentially advantageous.","Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","miximages/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png","marquees/snes/Full Throttle - All-American Racing (NA).png"
"snes_fun-n-games","Fun 'n Games","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Leland Interactive Media","Tradewest","Casual Game","Action","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Fun 'N Games presents a mixture of games and ""applications"". The main menu is divided into four sections:

1. Paint: 

A paint program that offers different tools and brushes, a few pre-made backgrounds and objects as well as the option to import figures that were created in the sub-games 'Stylin' Stuff' and 'mix 'n match'.

Games: 

Mouse Maze: A Pac-Man-clone in which the player guides a mouse through a top-down labyrinth. To complete a level, all cheese symbol have to be collected while cats have to be avoided. Collecting a bone temporarily transforms the mouse into a dog that can kill the cats.

Space Lazer: A  shooter seen from the cockpit of a space-ship. The player controls a crosshair and has to shoot increasingly difficult enemies.

Whack a Clown: This game is exclusive to the Genesis Version and plays like 'Whac-a-Mole' only with clowns instead of moles.

Music: 

Songs can be composed by choosing from several instruments and putting notes onto a stave. It is also possible to add sound effects (animal noises and others) or edit provided songs.

Style: 

This section contains two mini-games:

Stylin' Stuff: Here, the player can edit the sprite of a woman by selecting different clothes, hats or haircuts.

mix 'n match: Sprites can be created by putting together different heads, torsi and legs.","Fun 'n Games (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","miximages/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png","marquees/snes/Fun 'n Games (NA).png"
"snes_funaki-masakatsu-hybrid-wrestler-tougi-denshou","Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-10-21","1994","1990s","Tecmo","Tecmo","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","The sport of wrestling mixed with real life simulation! Funaki Masakatsu of Pancrase has shaped the new, most unique wrestling game ever made, and its up to you to make wrestling history! Create your character, control his stats and take your place in the highest rank of wrestling!","Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","miximages/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","screenshots/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","titlescreens/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","3dboxes/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","backcovers/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png","marquees/snes/Funaki Masakatsu Hybrid Wrestler - Tougi Denshou (English Translated by Phil).png"
"snes_fushigi-no-dungeon-2-fuurai-no-shiren","Fushigi No Dungeon 2 - Fuurai No Shiren","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-01","1995","1990s","Chunsoft","Chunsoft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Furai no Shiren (The Wanderer Shiren) is a roguelike, hack-and-slash game with randomly generated dungeon areas, items, and monsters. Despite being named Fushigi no Dungeon 2 it has no relation to the storyline or characters of the first game in the series, it does however share the same style of gameplay. The movement is turn-based. You visit towns and villages where you can buy and sell supplies. The dungeons are full of various traps and monsters, and sometimes non-playable characters you can talk to, some of which can join you on your quest.","Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Fushigi no Dungeon 2 - Fuurai no Shiren (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_g-o-d-mezame-yo-to-yobu-koe-ga-kikoe","G.o.d - Mezame Yo To Yobu Koe Ga Kikoe","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-12-20","1996","1990s","Infinity","Imagineer","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","The year is 1999. A boy named Gen lives together with his mother in a small Japanese city. One day he goes to the nearby mountains and discovers an underground cave. In this cave, he encounters a strange bat-like creature guarding what appears to be a huge diamond. When Gen approaches the diamond, the floor crumbles, and all he can see is a group of aliens descending on the Earth...

Gen is awakened by a man in a military uniform. He quickly gets up, and notices a calender. The year is 2009! Ten years have passed, of which there is no recollection in Gen's memory. The world has changed a lot during those years. Aliens have invaded the Earth, many cities are destroyed, and he find himself amidst the members of an underground resistance movement. Now he has to travel all over the world, help the humans in their struggle, and discover the secret of the invasion.","G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","miximages/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","screenshots/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","titlescreens/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","3dboxes/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","backcovers/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","marquees/snes/G.O.D. - Mezameyo to Yobu Koe ga Kikoe (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png"
"snes_galaxy-robo","Galaxy Robo","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-11","1994","1990s","Copya System","Imagineer","Tactical RPG","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","The player directs a group of mech pilots, each riding distinctive bi-pedal suits and other vehicles. The game is structurally similar to Fire Emblem: the player moves all their units for their turn, either fighting enemy units or performing other tasks, and then control switches to the opponent. When two units meet in combat, a cutaway cutscene shows the aggressor landing (or missing) a hit, and the unit statistics underneath tell the player how much damage was caused.","Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","miximages/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","screenshots/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","backcovers/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","marquees/snes/Galaxy Robo (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png"
"snes_galaxy-wars","Galaxy Wars","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-13","1995","1990s","C-Lab","Imagineer","Unknown","Unknown","1-2","1","2","60","3","Players launch missiles from the bottom of the screen at enemy spacecraft located at the top. The missile must pass through a asteroid belt flying across the screen and avoid the shots being fired from the spacecraft. The player controls the missile. Once launched it can be moved left and right and can be sped up by using the boost to speed it's way out of trouble. Points scored depend on the location of the hit on the enemy spacecraft. The speed at which enemy spacecraft moves increases as their numbers decrease.","Galaxy Wars (JP).sfc","SFC","0.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","miximages/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png","marquees/snes/Galaxy Wars (JP).png"
"snes_game-no-tetsujin-the-shanghai","Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-10-13","1995","1990s","Sunsoft","Sunsoft","Mahjong","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","60","3","Game no Tetsujin: The Shanghai (Iron Man Game: The Shanghai) is a puzzle/board game based on Shanghai, or mahjong solitaire. It was released exclusively in Japan. The game features 3 different modes: Classic ""Shanghai"" Mahjong Solitaire, where the player can remove any two tiles as long as they match and both have a free horizontal side and aren't trapped with a piece on top; ""Chinhai"" or ""Sichuan"" Mahjong Solitaire, where the player can only remove paired tiles if they can be connected with an invisible line that bends at a 90 degree angle two times or fewer. This mode features stacked tiles, unusual for games with this variant, but has the benefit of instantly removing any paired tiles that are touching.","Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","miximages/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png","marquees/snes/Game no Tetsujin - The Shanghai (JP).png"
"snes_ganbare-goemon-kirakira-douchuu-boku-ga-dancer-ni-natta-wake","Ganbare Goemon Kirakira Douchuu - Boku Ga Dancer Ni Natta Wake","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-22","1995","1990s","Konami","Konami","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","The 4th title in the Ganbare Goemon series of platform/adventure games which cast you as the heroic Goemon and his gang of wacky adventurers as he fights evil all over Japan. The game takes the action to outer space as the gang attempts to rescue Goemon Impact (Goemon's giant mecha) from a strange planet. The game is based around a stage-select hub from which you select which character you want to play as and then attempt to clear the game's many side scrolling stages in standard action platformer fashion, navigating the obstacles and defeating all enemies you run into with your weapons.","Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","miximages/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","screenshots/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","titlescreens/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","3dboxes/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","backcovers/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","marquees/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Boku ga Dancer ni Natta Riyuu - Kirakira Douchuu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png"
"snes_the-legend-of-the-mystical-ninja","The Legend of the Mystical Ninja","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Konami","Konami","Platform / Run &amp; Jump Scrolling","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","Goemon (Kid Ying) and Ebisu-Maru (Dr. Yang) are enjoying themselves at home, when they hear about strange spirits sighted at the temple and taking control of the other villagers. Concerned, that a new evil has appeared, the two set out to confront it at the temple. This is just the beginning of a new adventure that will take them Goemon and his partner all across Japan; into moutain villages, amusement parks, castle fortresses and more!

Players control either Goemon or his partner Ebisu-Maru (or both, in 2 player mode). They sequentially travel through adventure-type villages and side-scrolling jump and run action stages. In the overhead-view villages, enemies must be defeated in order to earn money and other items (which include a weapon-enhancing lucky cat and mystical scrolls). The player is able to attack with their primary weapon and jump. Throwing weapons can also be used, which consume money for each one thrown. Attacking innocent villagers will cause the player to lose money. Money can also be gained (or lost) by visiting one of the numerous mini games, often in houses: which include mazes, whack-a-mole, the lottery, dice, a 3D labyrinth and a game trivia quiz show. Other businesses such as shops, resturants, inns and judo training can also be visited.

At the end of each village is an ""action area"" which changes the game in a complete side-scroller. One or both players can enter this place, filled with obstacles, traps, enemies and a boss creature. In addition to the regular moves used in town, players also gain the ability to unleash one of four special attacks for each character (if bought beforehand at the judo training center). These consume the scrolls collected in order to use. Additionally, in a 2 player game, characters can ride piggy-back on one another, to avoid getting stuck by a scrolling screen.","Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","miximages/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","screenshots/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","titlescreens/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","3dboxes/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","backcovers/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png","marquees/snes/Ganbare Goemon - Yuki Hime Kyuushutsu Emaki (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 2 With SRAM Hack by FlashPV, Rev 1.02).png"
"snes_ganbare-goemon-2-kiteretsu-shougun-magginesu","Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-22","1993","1990s","Konami","Konami","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","As you may have guessed from the number 2 in the title, this is the second SNES game in the popular blend of the adventure and jump and run genres which is the Goemon series. Again, you (and your partner, in 2 player mode) control Goemon, his friend Ebisu-Maru and, new to the bunch, robot Sasuke, through loads of action stages and villages. Another new feature comes with 3D boss fights, featuring the giant mech type robot called ""Goemon Impact"".","Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","miximages/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","screenshots/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","titlescreens/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","3dboxes/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","backcovers/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","marquees/snes/Ganbare Goemon 2 - Kiteretsu Shougun Magginesu (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 3 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png"
"snes_ganbare-goemon-3-shishi-juurokubee-no-karakuri-manjigatame","Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-16","1994","1990s","Konami","Konami","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","Third title in the Ganbare Goemon series of platform/adventure games which cast you as the heroic Goemon as he attempts to save feudal Japan from all sorts of wacky evildoers. This time around, a time-travelling machine threatens all of Japan and thus Goemon and co. must jump into the future to stop the machine.

The game alternates between a top-down world map view in which your characters explore the gameworld and side-scrolling stages in which the main action lies. As in most games of the series there are also loads of mini-games and unique gameplay sequences such as maneuvering Goemon's giant mecha.

The game continues the tradition of bizarre humor unique to the series, you can play as either Goemon or any of his 3 companions, Ebisumaru, Sasuke or Yae with each sporting unique weapons and abilities and also with their own dialogue options and cutscenes, which feature sitcom-like pre-recorded laughs for added wackiness.","Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","miximages/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","screenshots/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","titlescreens/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","3dboxes/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","backcovers/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png","marquees/snes/Ganbare Goemon 3 - Shishi Juurokubee no Karakuri Manjigatame (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 4 With Ganbare Title Screen Patch by Djungelurban).png"
