"snes_judge-dredd","Judge Dredd","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-08-24","1995","1990s","Probe Software","Acclaim Entertainment","Platform / Shooter Scrolling","Shooter","1","1","1","80","4","Judge Dredd is a platform action game based on the British comic book character of the same name and the 1995 movie that tarnished that name.  The game was a multi system release so it fits the standard console jump/shoot/duck formula although it does add some unique twists such as wounding enemies so that they surrender and can be arrested.

The initial levels follow the plot of the movie with futuristic lawman Judge Dredd framed and sent to the Aspen penal colony from which he must escape and prove his innocence.  Following the fight at the top of the Statue of Liberty that ended the film, the game continues on with levels and characters inspired by the comic book culminating with a battle against the Dark Judges on Deadworld.","Judge Dredd (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","miximages/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png","marquees/snes/Judge Dredd (NA).png"
"snes_jungle-no-ouja-tar-chan-sekaimanyuu-daikakutou-no-maki","Jungle No Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou No Maki","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-09-18","1994","1990s","Kuusou Kagaku","Bandai","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","Ta-chan is the king of jungle. He was abandoned in savanna, and has been raised by a chimpanzee, Etekichi. He has a dearest wife, Jane who used to be a top model in New York, but now she looks?Anyway, to protect animals from poachers, Ta-chan is fighting against them with his disciple, Pedro, and a master of Chinese martial arts, Ryo.","Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","miximages/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png","marquees/snes/Jungle no Ouja Tar-chan - Sekaimanyuu Daikakutou no Maki (JP).png"
"snes_jungle-strike","Jungle Strike","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Gremlin Interactive","Electronic Arts","Shoot'em Up / Diagonal","Shooter","1","1","1","70","3.5","Some time after Operation Desert Strike, Ibn Kilbaba, son of Kilbaba S.R, threatens to annihilate America. After his father was killed, the people who were under his control, sent his son running off, along with his father's money and nuclear weapons program. Kilbaba, more ruthless than his father, longs for revenge of his father's death and decides to shed the blood of those who killed him, the Americans. Already armed, Kilbaba hires Carlos Ortega to help him set up his Nuclear Weapons program, deep in South America. Carlos Ortega, the world's most notorious druglord, also yearns to seek revenge. With his own private army, armed with the most hi-tech weapons, he's ready to fight America at all costs.","Jungle Strike (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","miximages/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png","marquees/snes/Jungle Strike (NA).png"
"snes_jurassic-park","Jurassic Park","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-29","1993","1990s","Ocean","Ocean","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","80","4","In Jurassic Park, you control Alan Grant as he navigates the park, starting with a taser that can later be upgraded to weapons like bolas, grenades, or tranquilizer darts. Grant can only carry two weapons at a time. You hunt dinosaurs and explore the park, where frantic movements can make the dinosaurs more aggressive, while slower actions help you avoid attacks. As you traverse jungles, hills, and buildings, you collect useful items like batteries, ID cards, or night vision goggles to progress. Entering buildings switches the view from top-down to a 3D perspective. In the dark corridors and rooms, you search for generators, terminals, and exits while defending yourself against aggressive dinosaurs.","Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Jurassic Park (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_jurassic-park-part-2-the-chaos-continues","Jurassic Park Part 2 : The Chaos Continues","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-09","1994","1990s","Ocean","Ocean","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","One year after the events of the Jurassic Park, John Hammond wants to bring Jurassic Park back and he sends Dr. Alan Grant to aid a team of heavy armed men down to the former park to restore order.  Meanwhile, Biosyn Corp has sent men down the former park so the company can gain control of the park for themselves.

In this 2D shooter, Player 1 controls Dr. Alan Grant and Player 2 controls Tactical Sergeant Michael Wolfskin and alone or together you face off against the free roaming dinosaurs of the once known Jurassic Park along with the members of the Biosyn Corp.  Your player always has six weapons.  Your primary machine gun and cattle prod are unlimited and most take down both the humans and dinosaurs but some only work the dinosaurs such as the cattle prod.  Most of the levels are non linear so you must explore different pathways to complete the mission you have selected once you have once you selected either one or two player mode.","Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","miximages/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png","marquees/snes/Jurassic Park, Part 2 - The Chaos Continues (NA).png"
"snes_justice-league-task-force","Justice League Task Force","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-08-01","1995","1990s","Sunsoft","Acclaim Entertainment","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","The comic-book superhero team of the Justice League consists of Superman, Batman, Wonder Woman, The Flash, Aquaman, and Green Arrow. They must defeat the evil Darkseid and his henchmen, Despero and Cheetah. But before they can reach Darkseid, the members of the Justice League must battle each other. You can follow this storyline in Story Mode, or if you prefer to just hold exhibition fights you can choose Battle Mode which allows you to use the villian characters as well.","Justice League Task Force (NA).sfc","SFC","2.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","miximages/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png","marquees/snes/Justice League Task Force (NA).png"
"snes_jutei-senki","Jutei Senki","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-08-27","1993","1990s","TamTam","Enix","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Jutei Senki is a turn-based strategy game, never released outside of Japan. The game takes place in an alternate universe where centuries ago humanity and their tree soldiers called Junei fight against Letum, a race of robots powered by magic.

The player commands the human army. The mission in each battle is to defeat the enemy army or take their headquarters in a limited amount of turns. In the human army's main building, the player can recruit new soldiers, but their number is limited. Each unit has its own statistics, including range, speed and attack power. Battles are carried out automatically, with animations on a separate screen.

There are three mission modes which include Campaign, Tutorial, and Expert modes and there is also a two-player versus mode.","Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Jutei Senki (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_jwp-joshi-pro-wres-pure-wrestle-queens","JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-23","1994","1990s","Jaleco","Jaleco","Fighting","Fighting","1-5","1","5","70","3.5","JWP Joshi Pro Wrestling: Pure Wrestle Queens is a wrestling game exclusive for the Super Famicom. The player can assume the role of twelve wrestlers from the 1994 roster of the Joshi Pro Wrestling (JWP) circuit: an all-female wrestling promotion that began in 1992. The game features a competition mode, a versus mode and a five-woman Battle Royale.","JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","miximages/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","screenshots/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","backcovers/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png","marquees/snes/JWP Joshi Pro Wres - Pure Wrestle Queens (JP).png"
"snes_ka-blooey","Ka-blooey","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-08-01","1992","1990s","Mirrorsoft","Kemco","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","It's a mind-blower! Welcome to the mind-blowing, bomb-throwing, brain-twisting world of KA-BLOOEY--where any button you push could lead you right to oblivion. You're a weird little bug-eyed spud in a land of exploding tiles, and only your knowledge of demolitions can save you from certain disaster. Use your strategic skill to set off the bombs, before they get you. Watch your step on a tile that's cracked--it's liable to break your back. And be sure not to blow it too soon. There are 130 levels before you make it to total safety!","Ka-Blooey (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ka-Blooey (NA).png"
"snes_kaite-tsukutte-asoberu-dezaemon","Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu - Dezaemon","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-09-20","1994","1990s","Athena","Athena","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","80","4","Dezaemon, or Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu (""Draw, Make, Ready to Play"") Dezaemon is a shoot 'em up construction kit from Athena. It allows players to draw their own ships, including additional frames of animation for moving right and left, as well as creating enemies and bosses, customizing weapon types and other elements. The game comes with a built-in ""demo"" project: Daioh Gale, a pseudo-sequel to Athena's 1983 Arcade game Daioh.

Dezaemon is a rebuilt version of Athena's original 1991 Famicom game Game Designer Yusei Soft: Dezaemon. This version of Dezaemon would later see an enhanced PlayStation edition named Dezaemon Plus in 1996, which in 2010 became the first Dezaemon game available in the US when it was added to the US PSN store.","Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","miximages/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","screenshots/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","titlescreens/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","3dboxes/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","backcovers/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png","marquees/snes/Kaite Tsukutte Asoberu Dezaemon (Daioh Gale - English Translated by Aeon Genesis With Bugfixes by Mwksoul).png"
"snes_kaizou-choujin-shubibinman-zero","Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1997-01-01","1997","1990s","Masaya Games","Columbus Circle","Platform / Fighter Scrolling","Platformer","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A sequel to the Shubibinman Turbografx action platformers developed exclusively for the SNES.

A new gang of cyborg-enhanced goons threatens peace in the city, so ""Doc"" decides to test his newest creations: Raita and Azuki, by sending them to destroy all the baddies and restore peace.

The game plays as an 2D action platformer as previous entries in the series, in which you proceed through each stage pummeling enemies and collecting power-ups to enhance your powers. Now for the first time each character has unique abilities and attacks, furthermore each character has a charging attack that can be used to energize your partner thus encouraging teamplay in the two-player cooperative mode.","Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","miximages/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","screenshots/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","titlescreens/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","3dboxes/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","backcovers/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png","marquees/snes/Kaizou Choujin Shubibinman Zero (English Translated by Svambo).png"
"snes_kamen-rider","Kamen Rider","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-11-12","1993","1990s","Sun L","Bandai","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1-2","1","2","80","4","Kamen Rider is a 2D sidescrolling beat-em up that was released on the Super Famicom in 1993. Player 1 plays as Takeshi Hongo and Player 2 plays as Hayato Ichimonji. They start as their human forms but can change to Kamen Rider at any time, and only Kamen Rider can deliver the finishing blow to a boss monster.","Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Kamen Rider (JP, Rev 1).png"
"snes_kamen-rider-sd-shutsugeki-rider-machine","Kamen Rider Sd - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-09","1993","1990s","Yutaka","Yutaka","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Kamen Rider SD: Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine brings together all ten showa era Kamen Riders to do motorcycle combat with an army of Shocker soldiers. Each Episode starts with an introduction by ""Oyassan"" Tachibana, and stars a different Rider with his own unique special move. Episodes are broken up into six levels of combat against several Shockers riding their own motorcycles or cars or other vehicles, followed by a boss battle in the seventh level. After levels 2 and 5 the Kamen Rider will repair any damage and be able to choose from one of 4 limited powerups, such as invincibility, super speed, or a machine gun. Most of the time Kamen Rider will defeat enemies by riding alongside them and punching them, and enemies will try to do the same to ram or shoot him.","Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","miximages/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png","marquees/snes/Kamen Rider SD - Shutsugeki!! Rider Machine (JP).png"
"snes_kat-s-run-zen-nihon-k-car-senshuken","Kat's Run - Zen-nihon K-car Senshuken","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-14","1995","1990s","Atlus","Atlus","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","","","Kat's Run: Zennihon K Car Senshuken (""Kat's Run: All-Japan K Car Championship"") is a mode 7 racing game for the Super Famicom that features kei cars: a smaller weight/size category of vehicle in Japan that includes microvans and convertibles. The single-player mode has one of ten female drivers competing to win a street racing tournament. A small window allows players to see their driver's expression, which changes when turning or whenever she overtakes or gets overtaken by an opponent.

Similar to Super Mario Kart, the multiplayer mode includes items and unusual courses which can also include jumps and pitfalls.","Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).sfc","SFC","2.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","miximages/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png","marquees/snes/Kat's Run - Zen-Nihon K-Car Senshuken (JP).png"
"snes_kawasaki-caribbean-challenge","Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-01","1993","1990s","Park Place Productions","GameTek","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","50","2.5","Race a Kawasaki Ninja Motorcycle or Jet Ski over 3 different islands in the Caribbean. When you're ready, take the Challenge to see how good you really are! Look down at your vehicle from a bird's eye view and cross bridges, make sharp turns, knock other opponent's off the road (or ocean) and get first place, if you can! You get to choose from a selection of 3 Motorcycles and 3 Jet Ski's when you race. You must complete 5 laps on each level and try to get first place!","Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kawasaki Caribbean Challenge (NA).png"
"snes_kawasaki-superbike","Kawasaki Superbike","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Lankhor","Time Warner Interactive","Motorcycle race FPV","Racing","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Kawasaki Superbike Challenge is a motorcycle racing game that uses the same engine as F1. It includes 14 standard-length race tracks, plus the Suzuka 8 Hours endurance race, available in both training and Championship modes. The game is unlicensed (except by Kawasaki), so all riders and teams are fictional.

The game now has more polygonal roadside objects than F1, and there is still a Turbo mode, allowing for faster racing at the expense of some of the detail. You have the ability to turn on or off weather (during rainy days the graphics are darker and the bike has less traction). The number of laps on each course can be 5, 10 or 15, and there are 4 skill levels. Some changes have been made to how the vehicle handles, to emphasize the switch from cars to bikes.","Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kawasaki Superbike Challenge (NA).png"
"snes_keeper","Keeper","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-07-15","1994","1990s","Fupac","Datam Polystar","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Keeper is a cute action-puzzle game. A five-by-five grid fills up with colored blocks with different icons on them. Pikuru and Kintoto, the player characters, can move freely about the grid, climbing over the blocks, and pushing or pulling them in any direction where there is room. Line up three or more blocks of either the same color or the same symbol and they will disappear. If the entire grid fills up, the game ends.","Keeper (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Keeper (JP).png","miximages/snes/Keeper (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Keeper (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Keeper (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Keeper (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Keeper (JP).png","marquees/snes/Keeper (JP).png"
"snes_ken-griffey-jr-presents-major-league-baseball","Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-01","1994","1990s","Software Creations","Nintendo","Sports / Baseball","Sports","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Ken Griffey Jr Presents Major League Baseball is a baseball simulation featuring Ken Griffey Jr. of the ""Nintendo Owned"" Seattle Mariners. This licensed game allows you to play with all 28 Major League Teams in their own stadiums and over 700 player names (not likenesses). You control your pitcher or batter as well your fielders. The game allows you to play and save a 26, 78 or 162 game season.","Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Ken Griffey Jr. Presents Major League Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_ken-griffey-jr-s-winning-run","Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-06-02","1996","1990s","Rareware","Nintendo","Sports / Baseball","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run is a licensed arcade baseball  game featuring MLB's own Ken Griffey Jr. Released in 1996, it contains all 28 MLB teams and logos from that time, with rosters containing fictitious names for the players (Ken Griffey Jr being the only real player in the game).

Next to exhibition games, there are different game modes to compete in. In the MLB Challenge, players start with a weak team and go against every major MLB club to go up the rankings. There is also a complete World Series, All-Star games and the MLB League. In the league modes up to eight players can choose a team and play a season of 26, 52 or 162 games. Between matches players can change the line-up and rotation, and trade away players.

Gameplay is fairly tradition, with fielding, pitching, batting and baserunning. Next to fastball and curveballs, there are six different pitches such as Super Fast, Screwball, Super Curve, Slider, Change Up and Knuckleball.","Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ken Griffey Jr.'s Winning Run (NA).png"
"snes_kendo-rage","Kendo Rage","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-10-01","1993","1990s","Affect","SETA","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","“Kendo Rage” tells the story of Josephine, a teenager training under Honest Osaki to master kendo and supernatural powers. Equipped with the Zopikki Talisman, which transforms her into a warrior, and her bamboo sword, she faces ghosts, pandas, and other strange creatures. Players control Josephine, who can jump, crouch, and attack. Her psychic powers charge over time, enabling stronger moves such as projectile attacks and a flame aura during a dash attack. The game features seven levels, each culminating in a boss fight.","Kendo Rage (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kendo Rage (NA).png"
"snes_kessen-dokapon-oukoku-4-densetsu-no-yuusha-tachi","Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku 4 - Densetsu No Yuusha-tachi","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-10","1993","1990s","Asmik","Asmik","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-4","1","4","90","4.5","Kessen! Dokapon Okukoku IV: Densetsu no Yuusha Tachi (""Decisive Battle! Dokapon Kingdoms IV: Legend of Heroes"") is a mix of an RPG and a multiplayer board game. Players move their heroes around the board, gaining levels and finding equipment, and eventually saving a kingdom from encroaching monsters. The goal is to beat the target monsters before the other players.

Though the game includes a ""IV"" in its title, this actually refers to the number of players the game can support rather than being the fourth in the series. Kessen! Dokapon Okukoku IV is in actuality the very first Dokapon game. It was followed by Dokapon 3-2-1 and Dokapon Gaiden on the Super Famicom and BS Dokapon Gaiden: Hono no Audition for the Super Famicom's Satellaview.","Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","miximages/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","screenshots/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","titlescreens/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","3dboxes/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","backcovers/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png","marquees/snes/Kessen! Dokapon Oukoku IV - Densetsu no Yuusha-tachi (English Translated by Krokodyl).png"
"snes_kevin-keegan-s-player-manager","Kevin Keegan's Player Manager","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","Anco","Imagineer","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Kevin Keegan's Player Manager combines a football management sim with the Kick Off football game. The Kick Off game includes several skill levels, Challenge Cup and practice options. Manager offers three management skill levels, three division league and two Cup competitions. Options to play on the pitch or watch and get computer predicted results.","Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Kevin Keegan's Player Manager (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_kick-off","Kick Off","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-22","1993","1990s","Anco","Imagineer","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Kick Off is a soccer game for the Super NES. Unlike most of the titles of this genre, the matches are played with a top view. The other particularity is that the ball does not stick to the players' feet, so you have to practice the training mode to learn how to control the ball well. Each player has his own characteristics, divided into four categories: speed, endurance, precision and aggressiveness.","Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Kick Off (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_kick-off-3-european-challenge","Kick Off 3 : European Challenge","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Anco","Vic Tokai","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","The third entry in the Kick Off series is the first that offers a isometric view instead of the old top-down view. In the game there are four competitions (World Cup Finals, League, Knock Out Cup and the challenge). There is several options for game speed and ball control to suit both novice and skilled gamers. Each of the 32 teams in the game has a distinct style of play, ensuring that no two games are alike. Each team also has ""key players"", such as Liberos and Wingers, who play a pivotal role in how that team plays. Kick Off 3 offers two styles of play: Arcade and Simulation. The difference is that in the Arcade mode you can switch of rules to focus purely on reflexes.","Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Kick Off 3 - European Challenge (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_kid-klown-in-crazy-chase","Kid Klown in Crazy Chase","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-01","1995","1990s","Kotobuki System","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","You play as a clown, trying to finish the level while avoiding Blackjack's booby traps.

To successfully finish each level, you'll need to beat the fuse. What is the fuse? At the start of each level, there is a bomb fuse at the same location as you are, you must get to the end of the level faster than it does in order to prevent the big bomb from going off. In addition, you'll also need to find all 4 card symbols before you can finish. If you don't, you'll get to go to the beginning of the level and have another try.","Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kid Klown in Crazy Chase (NA).png"
"snes_kidou-butoden-g-gundam","Kidou Butoden G Gundam","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-27","1994","1990s","Pandora Box","Bandai","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","Kidou Butouden G Gundam is based on the 1994 anime TV series of the same name. The first Gundam production not set in the continuity established in the 1979 original series of the franchise, G Gundam presents a world in which global conflicts are settled in a yearly tournament fought between Gundam mecha, each representing its nation's qualities (and stereotypes). Naturally, this premise was adapted into a one-on-one fighting game.

The game includes 10 different Gundams (one of which can only be selected by players via a secret code), each with its own arena and background music.","Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Kidou Butouden G Gundam (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_kidou-senshi-gundam-cross-dimension-0079","Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-10","1995","1990s","Bandai","Bandai","Tactical RPG","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Cross Dimension 0079 uses an unorthodox system of tactical roleplaying. It sets the player with a number of mobile suits varying from one to three. In the early game the player controls only the Gundam. As the game progresses the player is allowed access to the Gundam, Guncannon and Guntank. In the late game (as the game follows the MS: Gundam film story) the player controls the Gundam and two Guncannons. Finally in the late game the player controls the RX-78XX Gundam Pixie along with two Guncannons.","Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam - Cross Dimension 0079 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_kidou-senshi-gundam-f91-formula-senki-0122","Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-07-06","1991","1990s","Bandai","Bandai","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","","","Based on the similarly-named Gundam movie released earlier that year, Kidou Senshi Gundam F91: Formula Senki 0122 puts the player in the role of the Earth Federation, commanding mobile suit armies against the remnants of Zeon. Like many contemporary strategy games, the player moves their units from an overhead view, while battles between units are portrayed through more dynamic cinematic sequences reminiscent of the fights in the shows.","Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","miximages/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","screenshots/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","titlescreens/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","3dboxes/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","backcovers/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png","marquees/snes/Kidou Senshi Gundam F91 - Formula Senki 0122 (English Translated by Aeon Genesis With FastROM Hack by ComboKyo).png"
"snes_kidou-senshi-v-gundam","Kidou Senshi V Gundam","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-11","1994","1990s","Bandai","Bandai","Fighting","Fighting","1","1","1","60","3","Kidou Senshi V Gundam recounts the events of the show, from Uso's first battle on Earth to the destruction of the Angel Halo. The story is told in occasionally animated cutscenes between combat. In battle Uso pilots the Victory Gundam and, later in the game, the V2 Gundam. The gundam is equipped with either three rifles, a beam saber, and a head mounted vulcan gun, or, in some space levels, with a chargeable beam rifle, a vulcan cannon, and the same 3 rifles. The goal of each encounter is to destroy any enemy MS that appear onscreen.","Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","miximages/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","screenshots/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","titlescreens/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","3dboxes/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","backcovers/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png","marquees/snes/Kidou Senshi V Gundam (English Translated by Twilight Translations).png"
"snes_kikou-keisatsu-metal-jack","Kikou Keisatsu Metal Jack","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-07-31","1992","1990s","Atlus","Atlus","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1","1","1","60","3","Kikou Keisatsu Metal Jack is a scrolling brawler based on the anime of the same name. At the start of the game the player can choose from Red Jack, Silver Jack, or Blue Jack. Red and Silver both have weapons they can find ammo for in levels (a gun and baton, respectively), and all three can do a multiple hit combo or a jumping attack. At the end of a level the Jack will be met by his vehicle which will then merge with his armor. In this form the Jack's attacks are strengthened and he can charge up an attack that hits every enemy onscreen.

There is no story told at any point in the game, so if you want to know what's going on you'll have to track down the anime.","Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","miximages/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","screenshots/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","titlescreens/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","3dboxes/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","backcovers/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png","marquees/snes/Kikou Keisatsu - Metal Jack (English Translated by MrRichard999, FlashPV, and Jink640, Rev 2).png"
"snes_killer-instinct","Killer Instinct","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-21","1995","1990s","Rareware","Nintendo","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","In the distant future, a monopolistic and technological corporation called Ultratech organizes a fighting tournament known as ""Killer Instinct"". Many fighters answer the challenge to participate, including several of Ultratech's experimental warriors.  To increase the tournament's challenge, Ultratech utilizes a technology that can create bridges between alternate dimensions, releasing a creature known as Eyedol.  Eyedol is a two-headed ancient warrior that was imprisoned along with his bitter rival, Gargos. Some warriors enter the tournament to seek glory or escape.  Other fighters are attempting to bring an end to Ultratech's corruption.","Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Killer Instinct (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_king-arthur-amp-the-knights-of-justice","King Arthur &amp; The Knights of Justice","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-01","1995","1990s","Manley &amp; Associates","Enix","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","60","3","King Arthur &amp; the Knights of Justice is a top-down action game. It might be mistaken for an RPG, however the abilities of characters do not change over the course of the game. Players control Arthur King and can recruit two additional party members from the knights. Each knight has his own statistics in various areas including: Life Force, Defense, Strength and Speed. These party members are computer controlled with their own energy bars. Arthur can move up, down, left and right, block with his shield and parry with his shield. Arthur can swing his sword at any time but the damage inflicted depends on how much strength bar he has. The strength bar replenishes itself naturally over time, but is depleted every time Arthur swings his sword. Each knight including Arthur has a health bar, when this bar runs out, the knight is defeated. It can be restored by returning to Camelot or using items such as healing herbs. Arthur can carry a variety of different items accessible from the menu.","King Arthur &amp; The Knights of Justice (NA).sfc","SFC","0","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","","","","","","",""
"snes_king-arthur-s-world","King Arthur's World","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-05-27","1993","1990s","Argonaut Software","Jaleco","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1","1","1","80","4","This is a 2D side scrolling strategy game.  You take the role of King Arthur and lead your army into battle against evil.  You'll travel to many worlds including fighting dark knights, underworld orcs, and cloud giants.  One of the few games that is Super NES Mouse capable.

You are given a certain amount of troops each level.  Passwords are used to save your progress between levels.  The levels have enemies you must thwart, traps you should avoid, and gold to be won.","King Arthur's World (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","miximages/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","screenshots/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","backcovers/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png","marquees/snes/King Arthur's World (NA).png"
"snes_king-of-the-monsters","King of the Monsters","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-01","1992","1990s","Genki","Takara","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","60","3","King of the Monsters is a two player (or one player vs. computer) fighting game, in which the players take control of two gargantuan monsters (like Godzillas) and battle it out in different city locations.

The city gets gradually destroyed as the two creatures fight it out. The players can also interact with the scenery by picking up boats and such, to use as weapons against the other player.","King of the Monsters (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","miximages/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","screenshots/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","backcovers/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png","marquees/snes/King of the Monsters (NA).png"
"snes_king-of-the-monsters-2","King of the Monsters 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-06-01","1994","1990s","Now Production","Takara","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","The object of King of the Monsters 2 is similar to its predecessor. Choose from three monsters which include a large lizard, a large robotic ape, and a huge mutant human. Then walk through each city, making sure to destroy everything in site, including planes, buildings, and other creatures. When smashed, some buildings reveal power-ups that make your character more powerful. At the end of each city, you go head-to-head with the boss creature. There are small stages in between where you fight a second monster for bonus points, or you fall from the sky and land underwater where you try to collect points and power-ups while falling.","King of the Monsters 2 (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","miximages/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png","marquees/snes/King of the Monsters 2 (NA).png"
"snes_kinnikuman-dirty-challenger","Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-08-21","1992","1990s","Yutaka","Yutaka","Sports / Wrestling","Sports","1-2","1","2","30","1.5","Kinnikuman: Dirty Challenger is a wrestling game based in the Kinnikuman (or Muscleman, as it's known in other countries) anime series. The game encompasses the ""Survivor Match for the Kinniku Throne"" saga, which started just after the ""Dream Chojin Tag Team Chapter"" ended.

In this game you will be able to choose among 6 relevant characters of the aforementioned saga, including the enemies of Kinnikuman. Combats will be 1 vs 1 and you will be able to execute a repertory of man to man acrobatic tactics, and of course, all combats will end either with a countdown or a surrender of one of the combatants.","Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","miximages/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png","marquees/snes/Kinnikuman - Dirty Challenger (JP).png"
"snes_kirby-no-kirakira-kids","Kirby No Kirakira Kids","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1998-02-01","1998","1990s","HAL Laboratory","Nintendo","Puzzle / Fall","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A Tetris-like puzzle game. There are varying modes of play, such as Time Trial, Challenge and Story Mode, but the basic idea of play is the same: you must sandwich Star Blocks between two like Friend Blocks, either horizontally or vertically. The Friend Blocks and any Star Blocks in-between are then removed from play. Similar to Tetris, sets of blocks fall into the bin that contain any two of the following types:","Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","miximages/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","screenshots/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","backcovers/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png","marquees/snes/Kirby no Kirakira Kizzu (English Translated by TheDanaAddams, Rev 1.1).png"
"snes_kirby-s-fun-pak","Kirby's Fun Pak","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1997-01-23","1997","1990s","HAL Laboratory","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","100","5","The game is actually a set of seven main games: Spring Breeze, Dynablade, Gourmet Race, The Great Cave Offensive are unlocked at first, and later Metaknight's Revenge, Milky Way Wishes and The Arena become available, as well as a couple of minor minigames.
These are all typical Kirby platformers, each one with its own set of levels and different gameplay. In most of them you can consume monsters and learn their special abilities, but in Milky Way Wishes abilities are instead collected in a manner similar to Metroid. Offensive Cave features levels with various obstacles, hidden treasures and some elementary lever-pulling kind of puzzle solving. Metaknight's Revenge is the only game on the cartridge with a (fairly strict) timer. The Arena is a ""boss rush"" mode, asking players to defeat every boss in the game with limited health refills and on one life.
Kirby Super Star is best known for its two-player cooperative play option, which has given it a reputation as a party game.","Kirby Super Star (NA).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kirby Super Star (NA).png"
"snes_kirby-s-ghost-trap","Kirby's Ghost Trap","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-01","1995","1990s","HAL Laboratory","Banpresto","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","The First Annual Dream Land's Avalanche Competition is about to get under way, and as Kirby and a host of characters from the Kirby series, you can join the tournament and become Avalanche king.

Avalanche is similar to Puyo Puyo, and has you stacking colored blobs similar in a way to Tetris. When a certain amount of one color is stacked, an explosion occurs and the screen clears of those blobs, which then appear in the other players screen (either the CPU or a second player). When your screen is cleared before the other player, you move onto the next round, and so on until the final boss. The winner of that match is then crowned the Avalanche king.","Kirby's Avalanche (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kirby's Avalanche (NA).png"
"snes_kirby-s-dream-course","Kirby's Dream Course","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-08-24","1995","1990s","HAL Laboratory","Nintendo","Sports / Golf","Sports","1-2","1","2","80","4","Kirby's Dream Course is an innovative SNES game featuring everyone's favourite pink blob, Kirby. After his adventures in Dream Land we find him in this mini golf game where Kirby himself is the ball, and you must guide him around each hole using the sort of bar system that you see in most golf games. Where this game differs, however, is in the fact that you need to eliminate all on-screen enemies before you can guide Kirby into the hole and complete it. As per usual in golf games, the more strokes it takes you to complete the whole, the lower your score - so make sure you aim Kirby just right!

You'll find eight courses and a total of sixty-four holes in the game, not including the final showdown with King Dedede, as well as an action-packed two-player mode.","Kirby's Dream Course (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","miximages/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png","marquees/snes/Kirby's Dream Course (NA).png"
