The tournament this time is an eating contest, and the game itself plays like a block-lining puzzle game in the grand tradition of Tetris and Puyo Puyo. You must line-up blocks  of the same type as they fall in the classic rectangular play area, clearing them before time runs up or they fill the entire screen. Features a 1P tournament mode, and a 2P competitive Vs mode in which you can chain blocks and send them to your opponent as ""attacks"" in hopes of causing him/her to lose.","Kunio no Oden (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","miximages/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png","marquees/snes/Kunio no Oden (JP).png"
"snes_kunio-kun-no-dodge-ball-da-yo-zenin-sh-go","Kunio-kun no Dodge Ball da yo - Zenin Shūgo","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-08-06","1993","1990s","Technos Japan","Technos Japan","Sports / Dodgeball","Sports","1-4","1","4","80","4","This sequel also adds more comprehensive tournament mechanics, for instance your performance in each match allows you to buy power-ups and special moves to enhance your players, you can also create a custom team and manage them throughout the tournament by adding or removing members as you see fit (you can even try to lure players from opposing teams). You can also choose among a series of formations and AI profiles in case you play alone, although you can use a multi-tap to play with up to 4 players.

