The story is about raising a weak hero to be an independent man in a role-playing game with a heart-warming atmosphere. The battle is done on the map like a simulation; players don't have to leave the map in order to kill enemies. All the graphics in the game are done using a manga-style fantasy theme.","Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","miximages/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","screenshots/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","titlescreens/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","3dboxes/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","backcovers/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png","marquees/snes/Light Fantasy (English Translated by Zarby89).png"
"snes_little-magic","Little Magic","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-24","1993","1990s","Altron","Altron","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","70","3.5","May is an apprentice mage at Magic School and must pass the final exam.

A puzzle game seen from a top-down perspective. The goal of each level is to open the exit by pushing a magic stone on a designated spot. May can also push objects one tile ahead without moving herself. This is often used to get the stone over water that she can't cross herself. May's other ability is to create magic bubbles. She can create bubbles in three different sizes. Bubbles will pop after a certain amount of time, depending on their size. If they pop while next to the stone or another bubble, the stone or bubble will be moved one space. Since May can create several bubbles simultaneously, it is possible, and often necessary, to create chain reactions to maneuver the stone into the desired position.

As the game progresses, levels become increasingly difficult and new hazards and enemies are introduced. Enemies follow a set movement pattern and can sometimes be stopped, but not destroyed by magic bubbles. Later levels are made more complicated by different types of tiles including: grass that slows down May, ice that May and the magic stone will slip over, spikes that destroy the stone if it is pushed into them, collapsing tiles that can only be walked over once, rune tiles on which May cannot create magic bubbles, teleporters and others.","Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","miximages/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","screenshots/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","titlescreens/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","3dboxes/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","backcovers/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png","marquees/snes/Little Magic (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1b).png"
"snes_little-master-nijiiro-no-maseki","Little Master - Nijiiro No Maseki","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-30","1995","1990s","Tokuma Shoten","Tokuma Shoten","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Little Master: Nijiiro no Maseki (""Little Master: Jewels of the Rainbow"") is a strategy RPG and the third game in the series. The first two games, Little Master Raikubaan no Densetsu and Little Master 2: Kaminari Hikari no Kishi, were exclusive to the Game Boy. Set two years after the events of Little Master 2, the heroes - swordsman Riim, minotaur brawler Mormor and healing mage Tamtam - rescue a goatman from a cave-in, which sets off another series of battles against old foes the Skull Bombers and other villains.

The game is structurally similar to Intelligent Systems's Fire Emblem or Camelot's Shining Force series: players move characters around a grid, fighting enemies in range via cutaways that shows the two characters exchanging blows on a 2D plane. Players need to be wary of counterattacks from enemies and getting surrounded by enemy units on the tactical map, as both might lead to an allied character getting eliminated from battle. The player can also investigate houses on the battle map to potentially earn items and recruit new characters.

The offers two difficulties: Normal and Advanced. The main difference is that experience gain is a lot slower on Advanced, forcing the player to consider how to spread around the finite amount of experience between their units. The game was not officially localized, but a few partial fan translations exist.","Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","miximages/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","screenshots/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","titlescreens/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","3dboxes/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","backcovers/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png","marquees/snes/Little Master - Nijiiro no Maseki (English Translated by DackR, Rev 2b).png"
"snes_live-a-live","Live A Live","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-09-02","1994","1990s","Squaresoft","Squaresoft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","In ""Live a Live"", you control seven main characters and play through seven different stories set in seven different time periods: prehistoric age, medieval Europe, feudal Japan, a futuristic world with robots and weird machines, etc. Those stories appear to have no common points, yet after completing all of them, you'll see they are in fact connected - the worlds of all those characters are threatened by the same ultimate evil force.

The combat features many innovations compared to traditional console RPGs: your position on the battle field is important, and certain techniques can be used only from certain positions, and enemy attacks also affect only specific parts of the field. Out of the seven chapters, some have more battle-oriented gameplay, while others are more devoted to puzzle-solving and information-gathering.

The game is one of the least known Squaresoft products, and was never released outside of Japan.","Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","2.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Live a Live (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2 - Deluxe Version With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
