"snes_mystery-circle","Mystery Circle","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-04","1992","1990s","Wave","K. Amusement Leasing","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","80","4","Mystery Circle is a Japan-exclusive puzzle game developed by Wave and published by K. Amusement Leasing Co. for the SNES platform. It plays like a combination of Tetris and Qix, with the player attempting to trace the outline of falling shapes before the screen fills up with them and causes the game to end. If every side of an object has a red outline, left by travelling around its circumference, the object vanishes and adds to the player's score, as well as dropping any objects sitting on top of it. The player has access to a single-use emergency satellite, which instantly eliminates everything within one third of the screen.

The game has a sci-fi theme, with a brief intro animation of the player's starship warping into a star system to begin the stage. The game was never released outside of Japan.","Mystery Circle (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","miximages/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png","marquees/snes/Mystery Circle (JP).png"
"snes_mystic-ark","Mystic Ark","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-14","1995","1990s","Produce","Enix","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Seven heroes from seven different Worlds. Seven heroes who were brought to a mysterious temple on a remote island and transformed into statues. Seven heroes who are chosen to bring harmony back to the universe: Miriene the witch (wizard), Lux the tetsujin (a race of iron people), Reeshina the grappler (monk), Tokio the ninja, Kamio the ogre, and Mesia the priestess. The seventh hero is you, the protagonist of the game - the knight Remeer or the female warrior Ferris. Your first task will be to release your companions from the statues they are locked in. You must travel to the Cat World and to retrieve an important artifact. But your main objective is to visit all the seven Worlds and to find seven mystic arks - the items that will re-unite those Worlds and bring peace to them.

""Mystic Ark"" is a RPG closely connected to  7th Saga. You travel on overworld map, visit towns, buy equipment, and descend into dungeons. The combat is turn-based and viewed from a third-person ""over-the-shoulder"" perspective. The battles are not random: the enemies can be seen as white dots on your radar, and if you don't want to fight, you can sometimes outrun or avoid them. The game also contains elements of pure adventure: often you must investigate objects closely and use items from your inventory on them.","Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","miximages/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","screenshots/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","titlescreens/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","3dboxes/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","backcovers/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png","marquees/snes/Mystic Ark (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 1.01).png"
"snes_nakajima-satoru-kanshuu-super-f-1-hero","Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-18","1992","1990s","Aprinet","Varie","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","70","3.5","Nakajima Satoru Super F-1 Hero is a Formula One racing game. The player can select between various competitions, each increasing in difficulty and course number, and compete against CPU opponents in races across the world. The player can also choose to do practice runs or time trials on the various courses in the game. There are various customization options for the player's car, though if they so wish they can simply choose a pre-selected ""Type"" that automatically customizes a car to maximize handling or top speed, for example.

The game was endorsed by Japanese F1 driver Satoru Nakajima, who was active throughout 1987 to 1991. Super F-1 Hero was not released outside of Japan, neither was its direct sequel Nakajima Satoru F-1 Hero '94. However, the original Nakajima Satoru F-1 Hero is better known in the US as Michael Andretti's World GP.","Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nakajima Satoru Kanshuu - Super F-1 Hero (JP).png"
"snes_nangoku-shounen-papuwa-kun","Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-25","1994","1990s","Daft","Enix","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","The game is a mix of brawling and platforming based on the manga of same title about a young boy and his unusual friends on a tropical island. The player character is Shintaro, a member of the Genma army, as he runs across the island to find out where a bunch of invading monsters came from. Shintaro has a quick punch move and a charged-up beam attack, and fights various sub-bosses as he makes his way through levels. He'll occasionally meet characters from the manga as well, including Tanno and Itou, who are a transvestite fish and an effeminate pink snail respectively.","Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","miximages/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","screenshots/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","titlescreens/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","3dboxes/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","backcovers/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png","marquees/snes/Nangoku Shounen Papuwa-kun (English Translated by FH, Rev 1b).png"
"snes_natsuki-crisis-battle","Natsuki Crisis Battle","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-04-21","1995","1990s","TOSE","Angel","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","isumi Natsuki is a member of the karate club at Goujyu High School. Strong and very skilled, only the captain of the group, Masaoki Yanagisawa, is a match for her. Her life starts to get really complicated when a student named Rina Takaoka is transferred to her school and becomes her classmate. Takaoka's former colleagues are set to punish her for having left Jotoh Academy, a school famous for it's martial arts.","Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).sfc","SFC","2.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","miximages/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png","marquees/snes/Natsuki Crisis Battle (JP).png"
"snes_natsume-championship-wrestling","Natsume Championship Wrestling","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-06-01","1994","1990s","Natsume","Natsume","Sports / Wrestling","Sports","1-4","1","4","50","2.5","Natsume Championship Wrestling is one of the many unlicensed wrestling games released by in the heyday. It contains 12 fictional wrestlers (Asteroid, The Viper, Spike, Python, M. Roach, The Phantom, Big Ape, K-Bruto, H. Snake, Fangz, J. Kraze and Conan). The game modes include 1P v. CPU, 1P v. 2P, a regular and a tag team tournament or a round robin tournament to compete in. Each wrestlers has a very extensive set of moves, where different moves can be performed based on the ring situation (grappling, holds, dashes, on the ground, from ropes...) but in the end every fighter basically has the same set of moves, punch, kick, bodyslam, with a few unique ones like piledrivers and powerbombs. Each one also has his own devastating finisher.","Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","miximages/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png","marquees/snes/Natsume Championship Wrestling (NA).png"
"snes_jaki-crush","Jaki Crush","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-18","1992","1990s","Compile","Naxat Soft","Pinball","Board / Casual","1-2","1","2","80","4","Jaki Crush is the third game in the Crush Pinball series, with a distinct demon and a horror theme. It is also the only title in the pinball series not released outside of Japan. It is based around the theme of a Jaki, a Japanese type of mythological demon and it contains a single table divided in three levels with six flippers. It can be played with two players (taking turns) and contains an option to alter the ball speed. There is a large amount of moving and stationary targets, and creatures that can be destroyed. There are six bonus stages that are accessed by shooting into a demon's mouth when it is open.","Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","miximages/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png","marquees/snes/Naxat Super Pinball - Jaki Crush (JP).png"
"snes_nba-all-star-challenge","NBA All-Star Challenge","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","LJN","Beam Software","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","NBA All-Star Challenge offers one-on-one basketball featuring NBA players from the 1991-1992 season. Five different playing modes are available: a single one-on-one match, a free throw competition, a 3 point shootout, a H.O.R.S.E. competition and a one-on-one tournament.
You can choose from 27 players (one from every NBA team), including Michael Jordan, Larry Bird, Patrick Ewing, Karl Malone and David Robinson.
Each mode can also be played by two players.","NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA All-Star Challenge (NA).png"
"snes_nba-give-n-go","NBA Give 'n Go","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-05","1995","1990s","Konami","Konami","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","70","3.5","NBA Give 'N Go is a port of the arcade version basketball game. The game is different from usual basketball games of the era because it is played in a vertical view. Most basketball games previously had a side scrolling or isometric view. Building upon this technique, the game features large player sprites and graphics. The game features the NBA roster from 1995 complete with player ratings. Gameplay consists of true 5 on 5 basketball but in a more arcade like style with alley-oops and high flying dunks. The audio includes action oriented commentary, in the style of NBA Jam.","NBA Give 'n Go (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Give 'n Go (NA).png"
"snes_nba-hang-time","NBA Hang Time","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-11-01","1996","1990s","Funcom","Midway","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","70","3.5","NBA Hang Time is a 2-on-2 arcade basketball action game following in the footsteps of NBA Jam. All NBA teams, each featuring 4 players to choose from, are included along with fast paced, over-the-top basketball, disregarding the normal rules of the game but including fouls and out of bounds. You can also create your own player, with a collection of body parts and clothing to choose from.","NBA Hang Time (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Hang Time (NA).png"
"snes_nba-jam","NBA Jam","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Midway","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","80","4","NBA Jam is a two-on-two basketball game You can play any NBA team, and you can play as players like Barkley, Starks, Pippen, and Grant. Each human player controls a single player, so choose your squad wisely to take team-mate skill into account. The game is action-oriented, and players can perform unrealistic slam-dunks. Full performance statistics are automatically recorded. The play clock and 3-point line apply, but other rules are largely overlooked.","NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/NBA Jam (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_nba-jam-tournament-edition","NBA Jam Tournament Edition","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-05-23","1995","1990s","Iguana Entertainment","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","90","4.5","""NBA Jam: Tournament Edition"" is a fast-paced two-on-two arcade basketball game featuring players from all NBA teams. There are hardly any rules, no fouls, and spectacular dunks. Players are rated across eight categories, including steals, blocks, and three-pointers. Up to four players can join, each controlling a single athlete. The Tournament Edition expands each team to three players and allows substitutions between quarters. Collisions now cause injuries that lower player stats, making strategic switches essential. New features like ""Hot Spots"" for bonus points, power-ups for arcade-style advantages, ""Juice Mode"" for faster gameplay, and ""Tournament Mode"" for challenging matches with unlockable players add extra excitement.","NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Jam Tournament Edition (NA).png"
"snes_nba-jam","NBA Jam","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Midway","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-4","1","4","80","4","NBA Jam is a two-on-two basketball game You can play any NBA team, and you can play as players like Barkley, Starks, Pippen, and Grant. Each human player controls a single player, so choose your squad wisely to take team-mate skill into account. The game is action-oriented, and players can perform unrealistic slam-dunks. Full performance statistics are automatically recorded. The play clock and 3-point line apply, but other rules are largely overlooked.","NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/NBA Jam XXX (Prototype).png"
"snes_nba-live-95","NBA Live 95","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-01","1994","1990s","Hitmen Productions","EA Sports","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-5","1","5","80","4","The first of the NBA Live titles on the PC, NBA Live 95 includes all of the basketball players from the '94 - '95 season as well as the All-Star teams from the East and West.

Manage over 300 players using the General Manager feature, new in this edition. Statistics for each player and team are saved. View players and teams side-by-side to compare stats and other information. You can trade any player in the league.

Multiple in-game settings let you choose the mode of play, the style, and even the length of a quarter or session. Full motion videos bring your plays to life onscreen. Listen to the crowd roar, the noises of the court and hoop, and voices of the players. There is also a soundtrack of modern music.

The DOS version includes support for 2 graphics resolutions: Normal mode (low res) 320x200 or Wide Angle (high res) 640x480. Gameplay can be controlled using the keyboard, mouse or joystick.","NBA Live '95 (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Live '95 (NA).png"
"snes_nba-live-96","NBA Live 96","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-11-23","1995","1990s","Hitmen Productions","EA Sports","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-5","1","5","80","4","As with all games in the NBA Live series you can draft, trade and edit players.  Play a full season, exhibition or just the playoffs.  96 includes 29 teams including the expansion Toronto and Vancouver teams.","NBA Live '96 (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Live '96 (NA).png"
"snes_nba-live-97","NBA Live 97","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-12-01","1996","1990s","EA Sports","Electronic Arts","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-5","1","5","50","2.5","NBA Live 97 is a basketball game with an emphasis on realism. You can choose from all of the NBA teams, create a team with players you create yourself, or choose a team formed by the All-Star players of the east or West coasts. When you create a player yourself you can customize the player to a high degree. There are over 60 offensive and 10 defensive set-ups.  Match options include an exhibition match, a whole season or the playoffs.","NBA Live '97 (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Live '97 (NA).png"
"snes_nba-live-98","NBA Live 98","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1997-11-02","1997","1990s","Tiertex","Electronic Arts","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-2","1","2","80","4","NBA Live 98 offers the player all teams, players and stadiums of the 1998 NBA season. More than 350 faces got scanned to improve realism. Also some new moves like crossover or fade-aways are implemented. You can play single games, whole seasons, the playoffs or new things like manager career or 3-point-shootout in the style of an all-star-game.","NBA Live '98 (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Live '98 (NA).png"
"snes_nba-showdown","NBA Showdown","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-10-01","1993","1990s","EA Sports","Electronic Arts","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","The final entry in Electronic Arts' basketball series based on the old NBA playoffs engine before the NBA Live series took over.

For the first time, the game features all 27 NBA teams from the 1993-1994 season. You can now play a full season with 82 games in addition to playoffs and exhibition games.

Back from the old versions are instant replays, signature moves (some of them new) and free throw T-meter. Game speed has been sped up.","NBA Showdown (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","miximages/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png","marquees/snes/NBA Showdown (NA).png"
"snes_world-league-basketball","World League Basketball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-22","1993","1990s","Sculptured Software","Nintendo","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","NCAA Basketball models a college basketball season, including March Madness (if your team qualifies!) and the Final Four (if you make it that far!). Teams represented are from the Big East, Big 8, ACC, SEC, and SWC conferences (some are missing because rights could not be secured). If season play is not for you, you can play exhibition games, but if you make it to the Final Four, you can save your game and have the option of using any team you like.","NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/NCAA Basketball (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_ncaa-final-four-basketball","NCAA Final Four Basketball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-02-01","1995","1990s","Bitmasters","Mindscape","Sports / Basketball","Sports","1-5","1","5","50","2.5","NCAA Final Four Basketball is a standard basketball-sim with a full NCAA license with 64 of the top Division 1-A teams including North Carolina, Florida State, Boston College, and Maryland to name a few.

There are three modes of play to choose from: two Practice modes, one which shows the player the basic in's and outs of the game and another for free throw shooting, Exhibition and Tournament.  Along with these modes are the ability to change around options to play a game to the player's liking including changing the length of the game. A battery-backup lets the player save their games, stats and rosters.","NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","miximages/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png","marquees/snes/NCAA Final Four Basketball (NA).png"
"snes_ncaa-football","NCAA Football","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-10-01","1994","1990s","The Software Toolworks","Mindscape","Sports / Football (American)","Sports","1-4","1","4","60","3","NCAA Football lets you play as one of sixteen college football teams in either an exhibition game or in tournament play. The tournament pits you against all the other teams which you must win in order to proceed; the game will automatically save after each game won so you can pick up the tournament where you left off. Team rosters are unique in that you can choose players from different decades from the 60's up to the 90's, and you can mix players from different decades if you want.","NCAA Football (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","miximages/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png","marquees/snes/NCAA Football (NA).png"
"snes_nekketsu-tairiku-burning-heroes","Nekketsu Tairiku: Burning Heroes","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-17","1995","1990s","J-Force","Enix","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","The young fighter Ryu was thrown out of his father's home, and this childhood trauma has been dominating his life ever since. His only wish is to surpass his father and to show him he has become a better fighter than him. Laila is a cheerful treasure hunter. She dreams only of valuable things, adventure, travels, and life in the capital city.  Gaou is experiencing a personal tragedy: he wants to discover the mystery of the disappearance of a woman he loved, Asuka, aided by his best friend Froz. Naaga is a girl who wears a heavy armor, which - as she thinks - will prevent her from losing any battle. At present, she has problems since she owes a million gold pieces to her guild. What do those heroes have in common? As their ways cross, they will find out they are in fact pursuing one and the same goal...

""Burning Heroes"" lets you start as either one of the four characters. Each one starts in a different place and is seemingly absorbed in his/her personal quest, but as the game progresses, the four heroes meet each other. You should also find and ""unlock""  four other playable characters as you advance in the game. The gameplay system of ""Burning Heroes"" is similar to other console-style RPGs: you fight random enemies and bosses in turn-based combat, viewed from third-person perspective, like in  Final Fantasy games. As you get hit in a battle, your ""passion"" (literally ""hot blood"", nekketsu in Japanese) level rises, and the more damage you take, the quicker you reach 100%. Once the passion bar is full, you can summon a powerful monster and to unleash a devastating attack on the entire enemy party.","Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).sfc","SFC","2.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","miximages/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","screenshots/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","titlescreens/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","3dboxes/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","backcovers/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png","marquees/snes/Nekketsu Tairiku - Burning Heroes (English Translated by Dynamic Designs).png"
"snes_network-q-rally","Network Q Rally","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-04-05","1995","1990s","JVC","Victor Entertainment","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","","","This car rally game was canceled in full development and never went out. The rom available on the net is however perfectly playable and seems to offer a game almost complete even if we feel that it lacks a lot of things that are usually found in this kind of softs (settings of the car, choice between automatic gearbox and manual ... etc ...). In the state, the game still allows us to play a championship on 4 different tracks.","Network Q Rally (Prototype).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/Network Q Rally (Prototype).png"
"snes_neugier-umi-to-kaze-no-kodo","Neugier - Umi To Kaze No Kodo","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-03-26","1993","1990s","Wolf Team","Telenet","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","You play a man named Duke, the son of Count Wein of Neugier. Long time ago, Duke was exiled from the country by his father. Traveling in faraway lands, Duke heard that his homeland was under attack. He decided to return to Neugier. On the way his ship was attacked by pirates, lead by a mysterious demon named Ord. After he barely escaped the ship, Duke found his way into his father's castle. He realized now that the pirates' attack and the monsters roaming the castle are connected. Although his father doesn't trust him, Duke is determined to save Neugier.

""Neugier"" is an action adventure game in which you fight enemies in real-time, slashing them with your melee weapon. You have a ""throw chain"" ability, which allows you to press objects to the walls, destroying them. You can also push enemies to the walls this way. Duke can also jump and equip armor you find sometimes in dungeons.","Neugier - The Journey Home (English Translated by Haeleth &amp; RPGOne, Rev 2).sfc","SFC","0","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","false","","","","","","",""
"snes_new-3d-golf-simulation-harukanaru-augusta","New 3d Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-04-05","1991","1990s","T&amp;E Soft","T&amp;E Soft","Sports / Golf","Sports","1","1","1","80","4","Harukanaru Augusta is the first title of the Harukanaru Augusta franchise and the first of many golf simulation games from T&amp;E Soft based on US courses to be released on the Super Nintendo, as well as on the Genesis and Japanese home computers. Both it and True Golf Classics: Waialae Country Club were released in the same year of 1991, with the Japanese release of Waialae and True Golf Classics: Pebble Beach Golf Links the following year.



Harukanaru Augusta is an in-depth golf simulator, rather than an Arcade-style golf game. It has a lot of customization options for the player's stance and clubs, and the player can consider the wind direction and topography before every swing. The golfers are depicted as digitized sprites of actors, both in the portraits and whenever the player's character takes a swing.","New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/New 3D Golf Simulation - Harukanaru Augusta (JP, Rev 1).png"
"snes_uncharted-waters-2-new-horizons","Uncharted Waters 2 : New Horizons","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-10-01","1994","1990s","Koei","Koei","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","70","3.5","In the first Uncharted Waters game, it was the 15th century and the New World was discovered. Now in New Horizons, it is the early 16th century and the age of exploration and sea trade is underway. Players choose from any one of six adventurers (scenarios), each with their own distinctive but intertwining plot, to embark on a quest of sailing, seamanship and exploration.","New Horizons (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","miximages/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","screenshots/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","backcovers/snes/New Horizons (NA).png","marquees/snes/New Horizons (NA).png"
"snes_new-yatterman-nandai-kandai-yajirobee","New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-03-22","1996","1990s","Tom Create","Yutaka","Unknown","Unknown","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","New Yatterman: Nandai Kandai Yajirobee is a multiplayer-focused game for the Super Famicom that is based on the 1970s Yatterman anime franchise.

Yatterman features a team of three heroes, all called Yatterman, who attempt to beat the supervillain team Doronbo (also a group of three) to a set of skull coins which will reveal the way to a giant treasure horde. The heroes often use toys and mechs to defeat their incompetent opponents, such as the male protagonist's kendamagic.

The game is similar to First Star's Spy vs. Spy series, in that both teams are attempting to reach the treasure first. They can trip up their opponents via a number of traps, some of which have conditional uses. By burying mines and setting off missiles, the player can whittle down the health of their opponents as well as keep them off their tracks. If the two teams meet, they fight each other in a three-on-three tag team battle with the losing side taking a large hit to their health. The game can be played single-player (as either the Yatterman team or the Doronbo team) or multiplayer.","New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","miximages/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","screenshots/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","backcovers/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png","marquees/snes/New Yatterman - Nandai Kandai Yajirobee (JP).png"
"snes_newman-haas-indycar-featuring-nigel-mansell","Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-08","1994","1990s","Acclaim Entertainment","Acclaim Entertainment","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","60","3","Newman Haas Indy Car is, as the name shows, a racing game endorsed by the CART racing team of the same name and their top driver at the time, Englishman Nigel Mansell. 

The player has the option to drive a single race (where the option to practice is hidden) or go for the crown against 12 other drivers in 15 tracks (with some variety, not only ovals) across North America, with the season opener in Queensland, Australia. It is possible play solo, with a friend in split screen or with split screen against the computer, and if music or engine sounds are played in the race.
A key choice in the game is selecting between Arcade and Simulation mode - in the later, the car can take a lot less damage before giving up, fine-tuning must be done by the driver and rules are enforced, under the risk of a disqualification.

Graphically, the game is less sterile than most driving games of the platform (and in 16-bit consoles, generally), thanks to the inclusion of several polygonal buildings scattered around the circuits.","Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","miximages/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png","marquees/snes/Newman Haas IndyCar featuring Nigel Mansell (NA).png"
"snes_nfl-football","NFL Football","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-01","1993","1990s","Konami","Konami","Sports / Football (American)","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","It's very simple... the NFL is football and this is the only officially licensed NFL game for the NES... the closest thing to ""real"" professional football that you can play without a helmet and shoulder pads. You can select any of the American and National Conference teams, and if you're good enough, you can take them all the way to the Super Bowl! Team rosters are broken down into positions and individually rated according to skills, with position upgrade packages, player ability handicapping, actual NFL Offensive and Defensive Playbook alignments with audibles and even variable wind direction and strength. You not only coach from the sidelines, you also control all the movements of the players on the field. Run, pass, catch, kick, punt, jump, tackle, block and dive... Refs even call penalties. No matter what kind of fan you are, whether you know the rulebook inside-out or just watch an occasional game on TV... this is the one football game you've got to have!","NFL Football (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","miximages/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NFL Football (NA).png","marquees/snes/NFL Football (NA).png"
"snes_nfl-quarterback-club-96","NFL Quarterback Club 96","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Iguana Entertainment","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Football (American)","Sports","1-5","1","5","60","3","This game is the next-gen update of NFL Quarterback Club.

Every team from the 1996 NFL season is shown here, with real player numbers and stats. 32 offensive plays and 16 defensive plays are available as you play.  Options include setting quarter lengths, from one to 15 minutes, and selecting your mode of play: Preseason, Playoffs, or a full Season, in which you play 18 games towards the goal of the Super Bowl.","NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NFL Quarterback Club '96 (NA).png"
"snes_nfl-quarterback-club","NFL Quarterback Club","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-04-01","1995","1990s","Iguana Entertainment","LJN","Sports / Football (American)","Sports","1-5","1","5","60","3","One of the earliest in the series which features all 28 NFL teams including their respective nicknames and logos and many of the top players of the day including John Elway, Joe Montana, Brett Farve and Troy Aikman, just to name a few.

There are a variety of game modes to choose from including NFL Play which includes preseason, season and playoff modes along with the Simulation mode that allows players to relive past moments of NFL history and lets the player recreate these moments or change history all together.

Also included is the Quarterback Challenge which would become a staple in the series.  In the QB Challenge, the player chooses from one of nineteen different NFL quarterbacks to compete in a series of challenges that will test their accuracy, speed, power and knowledge.  Players can also create their own custom quarterback and throw them into the competition.

Players can also customize their options including quarter lengths, weather conditions, rules, etc. The game also contains a battery backup to save games and created characters.","NFL Quarterback Club (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","miximages/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png","marquees/snes/NFL Quarterback Club (NA).png"
"snes_nhl-hockey-94","NHL Hockey '94","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-10-02","1993","1990s","Electronic Arts","EA Sports","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-5","1","5","90","4.5","After establishing themselves in the virtual rinks, EA combined both NHL and NHLPA licenses in NHL '94, allowing them to feature all 26 real teams (including both expansion teams) and players of the new NHL season for the first time in the series.

Aside from the licenses, the gameplay for this edition sees for the first time the inclusion of the devastating one-timer shot, but to keep forwards (literally) in check, defenders also feature harder body checks, with new animations. For an additional challenge, players can also choose to control the goalkeeper manually. A popular feature in previous editions, fighting is completely removed from the game. Game modes include exhibition games (or ""regular season"" in-game), playoffs (best of one or best of seven) and an all new shoot-out mode. The main options remain basically the same couple since the establishment of the series - period length (5, 10 or 20 minutes), line changes (off, manual or the new automatic mode) and penalties. (off, on, on but no offsides). 

While there are some minor differences between platforms (Genesis  features record keeping for users and the MegaCD version has more stat screens, CD quality audio backdrops and the usual highlight clips here and there), only the PC version released at the same time features complete season action.","NHL '94 (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NHL '94 (NA).png"
"snes_nhl-95","NHL 95","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-08","1994","1990s","High Score Productions","Electronic Arts","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","NHL '95 was EA Sports' third licensed ice hockey game, promising the fastest action in the series to date. The action is viewed top-down, and can be played with or without realistic penalties. There is a full season mode, allowing you to guide a team through all 84 matches, with the option of injuries potentially putting players out of a sequence of matches. You have full control over trading and creating players for your squad. New in-game moves include dummy shots and lie-down blocks.","NHL '95 (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NHL '95 (NA).png"
"snes_nhl-96","NHL 96","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-10-26","1995","1990s","High Score Productions","EA Sports","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","NHL 96 was the first EA Sports game to feature Virtual Stadium technology, which resulted in a 3D feel and multiple camera angles. The NHL license ensures that real teams and players of the era are included, with a full league and playoff system. Many game options can be toggled - the infamous fighting is option, as are the penalties and offsides. The control system makes it possible to execute one-touch passes, and several special trick moves to quickly advance the ball. As a bonus feature, it included 'Hockey Card' profiles of every player on CD, and interviews with the stars of the game.","NHL '96 (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NHL '96 (NA).png"
"snes_nhl-97","NHL 97","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-10-31","1996","1990s","Black Pearl Software","EA Sports","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","1997 edition of EA's NHL sport simulation series. Improvements in this edition come mostly in the graphical arena, with a refined version of the ""virtual stadium"" system that adds new camera angles (including the classical top-down one strangely missing from NHL 96), and polygonal players instead of the bitmap-based ones with motion-captured animations.

Other extras include multiplayer support for up to 8 players through network or Gravis's GrIP system, the option to create new players and trade them around, and the usual amount of EA sports licensed multimedia flair, including an interesting ""Goalie Mask Viewer"" which lets you observe the elaboratedly decorated helmets of 30 goalies.","NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/NHL '97 (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_nhl-98","NHL 98","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1997-12-01","1997","1990s","EA Sports","Electronic Arts","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","NHL 98 features much faster and refined gameplay (now using four action keys instead of two, removing the need to double-tap to perform some actions), along other new features such as ""in-the-fly"" tactics developed by Marc Crawford, then manager of the Colorado Avalanche and new commentary lines from Jim Hughson and Darryl Reaugh and a re-worked interface.
Graphics are improved from the previous version with the players being less angular, and thanks to 3Dfx support, with less pixels showing in the textures. The in-game presentation follows a TV-style script, zooming in players while displaying stats during clock stoppages.

As far as game modes go, in the year of the first Olympic Tournament with professional players, NHL 98 introduced the first real international competition, with 18 International teams to choose from. Other game modes include Season (option to pick between 25 or 82 games) and Playoff (best of 1, 3, 5 or 7 matches) and a Penalty Shootout mode.","NHL '98 (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","miximages/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png","marquees/snes/NHL '98 (NA).png"
"snes_super-hockey","Super Hockey","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-02","1993","1990s","Sculptured Software","Nintendo","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","This early SNES title is one of the first hockey titles to simulate 3 dimensions by using the SNES mode 7 SNES technology. It contains all 26 NHL teams at the time with varying strengths and weaknesses. It doesn't have the players association license, but all of the player numbers and abilities were true to their real-life counterparts. You can take your favorite team from the regular season all the way through the titular Stanley Cup. It even has a menu option in the North American version to change the between English and French languages.","NHL Stanley Cup (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","miximages/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","screenshots/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","backcovers/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png","marquees/snes/NHL Stanley Cup (NA).png"
"snes_nhlpa-hockey-93","NHLPA Hockey 93","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-31","1992","1990s","Park Place Productions","Electronic Arts","Sports / Hockey","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","The second part in Electronic Arts' hockey series. It features the NHLPA license (so it has real player names), but not the NHL license (so it doesn't have NHL team names).

Gameplay is altered only slightly over the predecessor. Regular season and Playoff modes are available. Goalies have improved AI, there are more foul play options and the game now features detailed statistics.","NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/NHLPA Hockey '93 (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_nice-de-shot","Nice De Shot","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-04-29","1994","1990s","Magical Company","ASK Kodansha","Sports / Golf","Sports","1-4","1","4","90","4.5","Nice de Shot is a golf game from Magical Company, released on the Super Famicom in 1994. The game supports up to two players who compete on eighteen different courses. A single-player option, in which the player goes through each course sequentially, is also available. Each course takes place in a different part of the world, and tends to include unusual features like ice, stalagmites and other unlikely obstacles.

The game uses a rudimentary polygonal landscape to convey the lay of the land to the player, though this increases the loading time in many parts of the game as the topography has to be loaded in every time it shifts, either after a shot or while adjusting the camera to aim the ball a different direction.","Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Nice de Shot (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
"snes_nichibutsu-arcade-classics","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-05-26","1995","1990s","Syscom","Nichibutsu","Compilation","Compilation","1","1","1","70","3.5","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics is a compilation for the Super Famicom and Sony PlayStation released in 1995. It features three of Nichibutsu's (or Nihon Bussan) Arcade games from the early 80s, adapted for consoles but otherwise left as-is. Contract developers Syscom were brought in to make the adaptations.

Crazy Climber: An action game where the protagonist scales a building. Best known for its complex controls that independently control each hand.

Frisky Tom: An action game where the protagonist has to maintain a plumbing system and protect it from rogue rats.

Moon Cresta: A single-screen shoot 'em up similar to Galaxian in which the player's ship can dock with larger ships between stages to become more powerful.","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics (JP).png"
"snes_nichibutsu-arcade-classics-2-heiankyou-alien","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-15","1995","1990s","Syscom","Nichibutsu","Unknown","Unknown","1-2","1","2","80","4","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2: Heiankyo Alien is a 1995 16-bit remaster of the 1979 computer game Heiankyo Alien. It also includes a recreation of the original 1979 game configured to work with the SNES controller. The game remains the same: the player, as a Heian-era policeman, must dig pits to catch carnivorous aliens and then bury them to destroy them. It takes three steps to dig a pit big enough, and aliens will quickly fill holes that aren't large enough. If an alien catches the policeman, he will be eaten.

The enhanced version adds wooden blocks that can be chopped down, but this takes as long as digging pits and puts the player in peril during the time it takes to remove them. The game also includes a two player versus mode that gives each player half the screen. When an alien is removed from one player's side, it appears on the other player's side, so players must quickly remove aliens so they don't get overwhelmed by the sheer numbers.","Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nichibutsu Arcade Classics 2 - Heiankyou Alien (JP).png"
"snes_nickelodeon-guts","Nickelodeon GUTS","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Viacom New Media","Viacom New Media","Sports","Sports","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Nickelodeon GUTS is based off of the Nickelodeon TV show in the late 90's that is basically American Gladiators for kids.  The events in the game are based off of the actual game show events with the main goal being to score as many points as possible while trying to beat the clock.

In Elastic Sports, the player is strapped onto a long bungee cord and is bounced around.  There are three events: Slam Dunk is a basketball game, Attack is lacrosse and Spiral where the objective is to throw a football through a hanging tire.

Action Sports is an obstacle course which someone would find at a boot camp and is divided into two events:  Basic Training involves poles, swinging ropes and punching bags and Tornado Run which adds water jets and moving platforms.  Players can also pick up bonus items that will add time or replenish health and a chance at the bonus round.

The final event is the Aggro Crag which is a giant indoor mountain the player must climb while avoiding various obstacles such as confetti cannons, moving platforms and smoke vents.  The goal of this event is to navigate the mountain and activate five different sensors located on the mountain before being able to activate the last one at the very top of the mountain.

There is also an option for two friends to compete either head-to-head or alternating depending upon the event.  Elastic lets the players compete against each other on a split-screen while Action Sports and the Aggro Crag has to be played through alternating play.","Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","miximages/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png","marquees/snes/Nickelodeon GUTS (NA).png"
"snes_nigel-mansell-s-world-championship-racing","Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-16","1993","1990s","GameTek","Gremlin Graphics Software","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","90","4.5","Timing smiled on Gremlin with this licensed Formula 1 simulation, as Nigel's long quest for a championship had finally come good. The game was pitched somewhere in between a full-blown simulation like F1 GP and action games like Continental Circus. Only 12 cars were in each race, which could last from between 3 and 20 laps.

Before each race you can customize wing downforce, gear ratio, tyre selection and fuel load choices – pitstops are a standard feature of the longer races. The 16-bit versions include a unique 'Improve With Mansell' mode, in which a digitized version of Nigel's head appears in the top corner of the screen, offering instructions and praise.","Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","miximages/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png","marquees/snes/Nigel Mansell's World Championship Racing (NA).png"
