"snes_ninjawarriors-the-new-generation","Ninjawarriors : The New Generation","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-02-02","1994","1990s","Natsume","Titus","Beat'em Up","Beat 'em up","1","1","1","80","4","In “Ninja Warriors,” you fight as one of three cybernetic ninja warriors — Ninja, Kunoichi, or Kamaitachi — against the tyrant Benglar, who controls the land with his brainwashed forces. Each character has unique abilities, but all can attack, jump, grab enemies, block attacks, and unleash a special screen-wide attack that charges during combat. Your mission is to battle through side-scrolling levels, overcome obstacles, and eliminate Benglar to liberate the land.","Ninjawarriors (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ninjawarriors (NA).png"
"snes_nintama-rantarou","Nintama Rantarou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-28","1995","1990s","Culture Brain","Culture Brain","Unknown","Unknown","1","1","1","90","4.5","Nintama Rantarou (""Ninja Boy Rantarou"") is a platformer and the first of five games on the system to be based on the 1993 anime Nintama Rantarou, which was adapted from the manga Rakudai Ninja Rantarou, and features the lighthearted adventures of a bespectacled boy and his two best friends as they try to graduate ninja school.

As Rantarou, Shinbei or Kirimaru, the player progresses through a series of stages using their ninja powers to keep out of trouble. The first part of the game ties into the school setting with classes that teach the player vital mechanics in the game, such as climbing walls and crouching under projectiles.","Nintama Rantarou (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nintama Rantarou (JP).png"
"snes_nishijin-pachinko-monogatari","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-23","1995","1990s","KSS","KSS","Casino","Board / Casual","1","1","1","40","2","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (""Nishijin Pachinko Story"") is a pachinko game and the first title of the Nishijin Pachinko franchise. It features both a freeplay mode, where the player can select a machine of their choice to practice on, and a story mode with its own protagonist and life simulation aspects.

The game is named for and features virtual versions of machines produced by the Nishijin company: a real-like pachinko machine manufacturer. It follows the likes of Heiwa Pachinko World and Kyouraku - Sanyo - Toyomaru Parlor! Parlor! in creating a game based around the actual pachinko machines of specific manufacturers with the intent being to prepare players for the real thing.

Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari was followed by two more sequels for the Super Famicom: Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 and Nishijin Pachinko 3, both released in 1996. After that, the series would move onto the Sony PlayStation as Nishijin Pachinko Tengoku.","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari (JP).png"
"snes_nishijin-pachinko-monogatari-2","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-06-28","1996","1990s","Soft Machine","KSS","Casino","Board / Casual","1","1","1","40","2","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (""Nishijin Pachinko Story 2"") is a pachinko game for the Super Famicom and the second of three games based on the machines created by the Nishijin pachinko manufacturer. Like its predecessor, it frames its pachinko gameplay with an adventure/life-sim interface that has the player relax in their apartment between trips to the pachinko parlors.

A secondary goal of the game is to teach players how to master the real-life versions of these machines, a common trait for pachinko games developed at the time. Other Super Famicom series, like Heiwa Pachinko World or Parlor! Parlor!, also features machines from other manufacturers.","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","miximages/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","screenshots/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","titlescreens/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","3dboxes/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","backcovers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","marquees/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 2 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png"
"snes_nishijin-pachinko-3","Nishijin Pachinko 3","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-12-20","1996","1990s","Soft Machine","KSS","Casino","Board / Casual","1","1","1","80","4","Nishijin Pachinko 3 is a pachinko game for the Super Famicom and the third game in KSS's Nishijin Pachinko series, which focuses on the machines produced by the Nishijin Company. After this game, the Nishijin Pachinko series would move to the Sony PlayStation with the new subtitle Tengoku (or ""paradise"").

Unlike the first two games, which used a life sim framing device and had Monogatari (""story"") in the title, the game simply presents two machines that the player can practice on. Since these are virtual approximations of real-like pachinko machines, the player can glean a strategy for winning big in the pachinko parlors with enough training.","Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","miximages/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","screenshots/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","titlescreens/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","3dboxes/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","backcovers/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png","marquees/snes/Nishijin Pachinko Monogatari 3 (English Translated by MrRichard999).png"
"snes_no-escape","No Escape","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-01","1994","1990s","Bits Studios","Sony Imagesoft","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","60","3","Based on the 1994 movie of the same name starring Ray Liotta, the player takes control of Liotta's character, Capt. J.T. Robbins, whose only objective of the game is to escape an island. After shooting his commanding officer, Robbins is imprisoned and eventually sent to the island Absolom as a war criminal, from where there is no escape possible. To do this still, the player must explore the island with side-scrolling platform gameplay for clues, finding items and combining them to make weapons and traps.  

Robbins can talk to some prisoners to help him in his quest, but he must also avoid violent prisoners, either by attacking them with punches or kicks or using traps and stealth to bypass them. The graphics are based off of rotoscopes of the actual actors from the movie.","No Escape (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/No Escape (NA).png","miximages/snes/No Escape (NA).png","screenshots/snes/No Escape (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/No Escape (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/No Escape (NA).png","backcovers/snes/No Escape (NA).png","marquees/snes/No Escape (NA).png"
"snes_nobunaga-s-ambition","Nobunaga's Ambition","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-01","1993","1990s","Koei","Koei","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1","1","1","80","4","In the 16th century, Japan's Sengoku period divided the nation into numerous feudal states, each ruled by a daimyo. Many dreamt of conquering the land, becoming the Shogun and ruling the entire country. One of those daimyos was the ambitious Nobunaga Oda, a merciless ruler and a renowned strategist. Players take on the role of Nobunaga Oda or one of many other daimyos to try to conquer Japan.

Nobunaga's Ambition is a turn-based strategy game which can be played in different modes.  Players begin by selecting either a 17 regions scenario or a larger 50 scenario. Then, the player must select a daimyo, each of which is rated attributes in several categories; Age, Health, Ambition, Luck, Charm and IQ. As the game progresses, these values change depending on events in the game. The age value represents a time limit of sorts, as an elderly daimyo becomes increasingly likely to die of old age or sickness.","Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","miximages/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png","marquees/snes/Nobunaga's Ambition (NA).png"
"snes_nolan-ryan-s-baseball","Nolan Ryan's Baseball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-02-01","1992","1990s","Romstar","Romstar","Sports / Baseball","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","Nolan Ryan's Baseball is a baseball video game based on the retired baseball player Nolan Ryan. The game has no licensing from Major League Baseball and the Major League Baseball Players Association; meaning that Nolan Ryan is the only non-fictional ballplayer in the entire game. All the other players have names that appear to be given names while Nolan Ryan uses his surname. This game was reviewed on the January 1992 (32nd) issue of Nintendo Power.","Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","miximages/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png","marquees/snes/Nolan Ryan's Baseball (NA).png"
"snes_nontan-to-issho-kurukuru-puzzle","Nontan To Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-11-25","1994","1990s","Game Freak","Victor Interactive Software","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Nontan to Isshi: KuruKuru Puzzle (""Nontan and Friends: Round-and-Round Puzzle"") is a puzzle game for the Super Famicom and Game Boy. It was released exclusively in Japan and is based on the Nontan books for younger children that follows the eponymous mischievous kitten and his animal friends on their adventures. The game is a twist on the standard block-stacking puzzle game.

Nontan to Isshi: KuruKuru Puzzle is one of the few games that Game Freak worked on before they created their breakout hit Pokémon.

The goal of Nontan to Isshi: KuruKuru Puzzle is to stack blocks so that two matching icons are connected to one another adjacently and vanish. The player cannot rotate the two-piece blocks that fall from the top: instead, they flip the blocks around to reveal two different blocks on the opposite side. Most of the flipped blocks are linked to their originals: a fish becomes a fish skeleton, an apple becomes an apple half, etc. Sometimes they're rare bee or character face blocks instead: both are rare and are therefore difficult to clear once one appears, but two face blocks will eliminate an entire row when connected and are thus worth the hassle.

The game has an endless single-player mode that only ends once the blocks reach the top of the screen. There is also a two-player mode.","Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","miximages/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png","marquees/snes/Nontan to Issho - Kurukuru Puzzle (JP).png"
