"snes_r-type-iii","R-Type III","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-08-02","1994","1990s","Jaleco","Irem","Shoot'em Up / Horizontal","Shooter","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","UNDER A NEW BYDO THREAT, EARTH RETALIATES It was believed on Earth that the deadly BYDO Empire had been destroyed--but now they've returned, as nasty as ever. Their ships and armies are more powerful than before, and Earth will surely be conquered unless the new R-90 ship can destroy them. Powered with engines that launch it into different dimensions, and armed with the Round, Shadow, and Cyclone Forces, R-90 has what it takes to wipe out the BYDO threat. Then, the BYDO mother ship must be found and destroyed to entirely erase the species from our galaxy.","R-Type III (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","miximages/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","screenshots/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","backcovers/snes/R-Type III (NA).png","marquees/snes/R-Type III (NA).png"
"snes_race-drivin","Race Drivin'","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-01","1992","1990s","Atari","THQ Software","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","30","1.5","Race Drivin' is a sequel to the arcade driving simulator Hard Drivin'. Game play is similar to the first game. The player must complete laps around the race track before a  timer expires. There are several checkpoints around the track which increase player's time as he passes. 

Race Drivin' has three courses, one of which is the track from Hard Drivin'. There is a new super-stunt track, and an autocross course. The player also may choose from three types of cars.","Race Drivin' (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","miximages/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png","marquees/snes/Race Drivin' (NA).png"
"snes_radical-psycho-machine-racing","Radical Psycho Machine Racing","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1991-11-01","1991","1990s","Silicon &amp; Synapse","Interplay","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","20","1","RPM Racing was preceded by Rareware's R.C. Pro-Am in 1988 and Electronic Art's Racing Destruction Set in 1985, with the latter being the larger influence. It is a precursor to Rock 'n Roll Racing, which was done by the same developer.

Combat, obstacles, and traction on the non-flat tracks play a significant role. There are 3 vehicle types to choose from that perform differently on pavement, dirt, ice, flat or bumpy tracks. Cars can be armed with oil, nitro, and mines. Additionally, there are 3 levels of tires, engines, shocks, and armor.

There are full race season, single race, and custom track modes. In 1 player games, the 2nd player is replaced with one of 8 AI opponents who are each associated with their own vehicle.

In season mode, each track has an entrance fee and cash prize for placing 1st; often lesser cash prizes for lesser placements are also awarded. For an additional fee, racers can opt to move to a higher racing class (levels) where fees and prizes are bigger. Each class has its own set of tracks that the player may choose from. No track is mandatory or unavailable within a class. The player also has the option of going back to a lower class for free, which may become necessary if funds are eaten up be fees without winning prizes. However, going up always incurs a fee, even if the player has been there before.

Custom track designing is not intuitive but fairly easy to learn. Players can set the track's laps, gravity, and starting line type. Each track section can be pavement, dirt, or ice. Track sections are laid out like puzzle pieces on a 2D non-isometric grid (Pipe-Mania style). Sections include horizontal and vertical straights, four standard 90° turns, eight T-sections for diverging and converging multiple paths in any direction, and a criss-cross. Either a horizontal or vertical special straight section is used as the starting grid. The starting grid can be oriented forward, reversed, face-off with player 1 forward, or face-off with player 1 reversed. 7 points are used to adjust the 6 slopes in straight sections to 5 different heights. Slopes can be flat (0:1), inclined (1:1), or steep (2:1). Non-straight sections can only be adjusted for height for the entire section at once. An isometric view of individual sections is provided to help to orient the section's design to the adjacent sections. If the edge of a section does not match the height of the adjacent section(s), the player is warned immediately but may proceed without correcting the error immediately. But such error must be eliminated before the track can be saved. Also required for a track to be saved is no dead ends, and a starting grid section. The track verification is surprisingly liberal, even allowing diverging and converging paths that are against the flow of traffic (like turning around and going the wrong way up a freeway merging lane). The AI opponents are generally not able to discern the fastest or shortest route and can even be diverted into a path with no natural exit, or with very clever path designs, valid lap paths can be entirely different for player 1, player 2, and AI players; which can lead to interesting traffic patterns.","Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","miximages/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png","marquees/snes/Radical Psycho Machine Racing (NA).png"
"snes_radical-rex","Radical Rex","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-10-01","1994","1990s","Beam Software","Activision","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","60","3","Radical Rex is a fire-breathing Tyrannosaurus on a skateboard who has to save the dinosaur race under the spell of an evil creature. The game consists of 10 levels set in the prehistoric age. Radical Rex can use a skateboard or glide down hills, jump, kick and breath fire. Tougher enemies need multiple hits and when burnt Rex needs to kick them once more to destroy them or use his scream (hits everything on the screen). While playing Rex collects eggs, health, power-ups for his fire breath and scream refills. Checkpoints are shown as torches that need to be charged with fire. After each level, there is a Bomberman-like sequence to earn an extra continue. Enemies include flies, turtles, toads and all kinds of dinosaurs.","Radical Rex (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","miximages/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png","marquees/snes/Radical Rex (NA).png"
"snes_raiden-trad","Raiden Trad","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-04-02","1992","1990s","Seibu Kaihatsu","Electro Brain","Shoot'em Up / Vertical","Shooter","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","In the year 2090, Earth is invaded by extra-terrestrials. The World Alliance of Nations has developed a supersonic attack fighter, Raiden, to defend the Earth. A pilot must take control of the experimental fighter to destroy the alien warships that just happen to look like odd variations of typical military tanks and planes with gadgets attached to them. 

Raiden (called Raiden Trad on the Genesis and SNES) is an over-head vertical-scrolling shooter, based on an arcade game of the same title. It features two forms of weapon upgrades and two types of missiles (normal or homing). You start the game with several bombs which you can use to destroy most enemies on the screen to get yourself out of a jam. Each level ends with a large boss or bosses.","Raiden Trad (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","miximages/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png","marquees/snes/Raiden Trad (NA).png"
"snes_network-q-rally","Network Q Rally","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-04-05","1995","1990s","JVC","Victor Entertainment","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","","","This car rally game was canceled in full development and never went out. The rom available on the net is however perfectly playable and seems to offer a game almost complete even if we feel that it lacks a lot of things that are usually found in this kind of softs (settings of the car, choice between automatic gearbox and manual ... etc ...). In the state, the game still allows us to play a championship on 4 different tracks.","Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","miximages/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","screenshots/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","titlescreens/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","3dboxes/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","backcovers/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png","marquees/snes/Rally - The Final Round of the World Rally Championship (Prototype).png"
"snes_rampart","Rampart","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-08-01","1992","1990s","Bitmasters","Electronic Arts","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Tetris meets shoot-'em-up, Rampart was originally a one or two-player arcade game combining strategy and artillery action. Build your castle from Tetris-style pieces, place your cannons, bombard the enemy, try to repair, do it all over again. Later arcade revisions incorporated 3-player,  and the PC conversion does as well, making it a faithful, entertaining classic for multiple players on the same PC.","Rampart (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Rampart (NA).png","miximages/snes/Rampart (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Rampart (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Rampart (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Rampart (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Rampart (NA).png","marquees/snes/Rampart (NA).png"
"snes_ranma-1-2-akanekodan-teki-hihou","Ranma 1/2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-10-22","1993","1990s","Atelier Double","Toho Company","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","The game is based on the animÃ© series Ranma 1/2. It tells the story of the young martial artist Ranma Saotome. Traveling with his father Genma across China, Ranma discovered a cursed spring. When he and his father fell into that spring, they were both transformed: Ranma into a little girl, Genma into a panda bear. Hot water restored them to their original appearance, but whenever they are splashed with cold water, they turn into their ""cursed"" forms. 

The story of the game begins as Ranma and Genma are having breakfast by their friends. Suddenly, several cat-like creatures burst in and kidnap Genma. Ranma finds out that the cats belonged to Akaneko (Crimson Cat) Gang, who kidnaps strong fighters for some reason. Soon Ranma discovers that Kamusaru, the leader of the Crimson Cat Gang, is looking for an ancient treasure that will allow him to turn into a Nekomaou (Cat Demon King), and take over the world. Ranma and his friends will do anything to rescue Genma and to stop Kamusaru's evil plans!

The game features many characters from the animÃ©, including Ranma's fiancee Akane, the fighter Ryouga, the amazon warrior Shampoo who is in love with Ranma, and others. The game is built like a traditional Japanese RPG: your party fights random enemies in turn-based combat viewed from a third-person, Final Fantasy-like perspective. In battles, you can attack physically, or use various offensive and defensive techniques, which consume ""ki"" (an equivalent of magic points). Not only Ranma and Genma, but also the other characters in your parties can take a different shape. You carry around a bucket of cold water, and every time you use it on one of your characters, he/she is transformed into a ""cursed"" form. This is sometimes required to advance the story of the game (for example, Ranma can enter a certain location only as a girl).","Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","miximages/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","screenshots/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","titlescreens/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","3dboxes/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","backcovers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png","marquees/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Akanekodan Teki Hihou (English Translated by Dynamic Designs With Anime Names).png"
"snes_ranma-1-2-chougi-ranbu-hen","Ranma 1/2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-04-28","1994","1990s","Nippon Computer Systems (NCS)","Toho Company","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A 2D one-on-one fighting game based on the Ranma 1/2 manga series dealing with the adventures of a male martial artist cursed to transform into a female version of himself when splashed with water.

Ranma 1/2 : Chougi Ranbu Hen pits 12 characters from the series (including the male and female versions of Ranma) against each other in best 2-out-of-3 rounds matches, with the goal being to defeat all the opponents and thus claim a magical lucky cat that will grant the champion whatever he wishes.

Gameplay involves controlling your character in a 2D scrolling arena, in which you attempt to defeat your opponent by using your basic attacks and special moves, all the while avoiding your opponent's strikes. You can also charge up your character until he reaches ""super"" mode, which allows you to unleash super moves for extra damage, but performing any basic attack negates the super mode.

Features the standard single player and Vs modes along with a Tag Team Vs mode in which two players select a pair of characters and can exchange them in mid-fight for strategic advantage.","Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","miximages/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","screenshots/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","titlescreens/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","3dboxes/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","backcovers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png","marquees/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chougi Ranbu Hen (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1 With Framerate Hack Patch by Upsilandre, Rev 1.4).png"
"snes_ranma-1-2-chounai-gekitou-hen","Ranma 1/2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-03-27","1992","1990s","Masaya Games","Masaya Games","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","A one-on-one 2D fighting game based on the popular Ranma 1/2 manga series set around the misadventures of a martial artist named Ranma cursed with being transformed into a female version of himself when doused with water, with the game focusing on the one-on-one duels between the various characters.

Gameplay is similar to other one-on-one beat 'em ups, in which you control your characters movements and attacks in a scrolling 2D plane attempting to defeat your opponent with special moves (triggered by specific controller motions) and avoid his attacks.
The single player story mode allows you to choose the male and female versions of Ranma and then takes you through 7 best 2-out-of-3 matches against a collection of characters from the series, while a 2-player Vs mode allows you to freely select any of the characters without restrictions.","Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","miximages/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png","marquees/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Chounai Gekitou Hen (JP).png"
"snes_ranma-1-2","Ranma 1/2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-04-07","1992","1990s","Atelier Double","Ocean","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","60","3","Ranma 1/2 translates the familiar characters of the manga into direct confrontations where fast-paced combat and rivalry take center stage. In side-scrolling arenas, you control one of the fighters and try to defeat your opponent using a mix of basic attacks, special moves, and situational super techniques. Timing, movement, and reading the right moments to strike are crucial, while the protagonist's transformations and the exaggerated setting add a humorous edge – an action-driven showdown that fans of the series will instantly recognize.","Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Hard Battle (NA).png"
"snes_ranma-1-2-ougi-jaanken","Ranma 1/2 - Ougi Jaanken","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-07-21","1995","1990s","Rumic Soft","Rumic Soft","Puzzle","Puzzle","2","1","2","80","4","The game involves falling blocks of hands displaying the rock, paper, and scissors signs, which fall three at a time. In a similar manner to Tetris, the player can rotate the blocks to stack them, using pieces that defeat other pieces (e.g. paper over rock) to eliminate stacks. Doing so adds garbage pieces onto the opponent's screen to make it more difficult for them to eliminate their stacks of blocks. The objective is to fill up the opponent's screen up with blocks so that additional ones can no longer fit. Each character has two special attacks which can be used when large combinations are completed. Once in each round each player can also use a bucket of water that can transform a cursed character into their alternate form, which weakens their attacks until the effect wears off. This does not affect Akane or the Gambling King.","Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","miximages/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png","marquees/snes/Ranma 1-2 - Ougi Jaanken (JP).png"
