"snes_rocko-s-modern-life-spunky-s-dangerous-day","Rocko's Modern Life : Spunky's Dangerous Day","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-04-02","1994","1990s","Viacom New Media","Viacom New Media","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","50","2.5","Rocko's Modern Life is a side-scrolling adventure where the player takes control of the Nickelodeon character, Rocko, as they travel through three different stages (Rock-O-Whey Beach, Hill-O-Stench garbage dump, and Laundryland) trying to protect his beloved dog Spunky from the various dangers throughout the stages.

As Rocko travels through the stage, he has to perform various tasks to keep spunky out of danger such as tossing him above onto safer platforms, moving objects to break his fall or fight off various enemies that want to harm him.

There is no time limit and starts out with lives, with a health bar for Spunky while the player's character, Rocko, can't be hurt.  The story is told through comic-style panels and features the classic characters such as Hefer, The Bigheads and Earl the Experimental Dog.","Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","miximages/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png","marquees/snes/Rocko's Modern Life - Spunky's Dangerous Day (NA).png"
"snes_rocky-rodent","Rocky Rodent","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-09-01","1993","1990s","Irem","Irem","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","Mafia member Don Garcia has kidnapped Melody, the daughter of the owner of the Rose Restaurant. Rocky Rodent agrees to rescue her in exchange for an all-you-can-eat meal.

In order to find Melody, Rocky Rodent has to complete a number of fast-paced, side-scrolling platformer levels. While he can also defeat enemies by jumping on them, his most important weapons are four different hairstyles: His first hairstyle allows him to skewer and throw his enemies, destroy porous blocks as well as cling to and jump up certain ledges. A mohawk can be thrown at enemies like a boomerang or thrown into certain walls, from where it serves as a trampoline. Braids can be used as a grappling hook and a corkscrew-like haircut lets him bounce on his head to jump higher than normal. When Rocky is attacked by an enemy, he looses his current haircut. If he is attacked while not wearing any special haircut, he looses a live.","Rocky Rodent (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","miximages/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png","marquees/snes/Rocky Rodent (NA).png"
"snes_roger-clemens-mvp-baseball","Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-09-01","1992","1990s","Sculptured Software","LJN","Sports / Baseball","Sports","1-2","1","2","60","3","Roger Clemens is known by some as the greatest pitcher in baseball history. In  Roger Clemens MPV Baseball players play a standard a 9 innings baseball game in Exhibition mode and a full regular season can be played including the World Championship Playoffs in the Regular Season mode. While playing in Regular Season mode the player can see their chosen team’s progress by using the in game calendar showing all the games.

Players select a fictional team from the following groups:

1. National Division West: San Francisco Quakes, Los Angeles Apaches, Cincinnati Big Cats, San Diego Suns, Houston Mustangs, and Atlanta Condors.

2. National Division East: Chicago Swords, Montreal Lumberjacks, New York Buffalos, St. Louis Commandos, Pittsburgh Panthers, and Philadelphia Generals.

3. American Division West: Oakland Bashers, Seattle Pioneers, Minnesota Hounds, California Waves, Texas Cowboys, Chicago Red Birds, and Kansas City Kings.

4. American Division East: Toronto Bears, Boston Hornets, New York Rebels, Detroit Wheels, Milwaukee Marshals, Baltimore Eagles, and Cleveland Tomahawks.

Each team has a set of players the make up the team each with their own skill level and statistics which, like the teams, is fictional other then Clemens himself who is on the Boston Hornets team. 

When controlling the pitcher the player can control the speed and spin of the pitch as well as the pitchers position on the plate. The pitcher can also stop the opponent stealing a base which can be seen via windows showing the positions of all the baserunners. When controlling the batter the player has to time the hit to successfully hit the ball so it will not be a missed hit or a foul, or has the option to not hit in case of a ‘ball’.  The player can also steal a base while batting. After the ball is hit the game goes into fielding mode where the camera shifts to a view that is lower than the normal 45 degree angle (the fielder gets smaller as he moves away from the camera). The player then controls the closest fielder to get or catch the ball and then throw it to the selected base. There are many different cameras angles that the game selects depending on the location of the ball. The camera can be on the left, center, or right side of the field in ether infield or outfield position and all cameras can pan to follow the ball. 

The player can use a password to save their progress in Regular Season mode. An exhibition match can be played in two player mode with the use of two controllers.","Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_romance-of-the-three-kingdoms-ii","Romance of the Three Kingdoms II","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-05-01","1992","1990s","Koei","Koei","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1-4","1","4","60","3","The sequel to Romance of the Three Kingdoms. The game concept is still the same, though they did improve a lot of features that one may consider 'disturbing' from RTK I (e.g. Generals dying easily due to disease, etc.). RTK can be said to be much more 'stable' where players no longer have to worry that much about time length in the game, as the game take a slower time rate to allow the players to build up their forces without much interference. The RTK II gameplay and interface is closely similar to Nobunaga's Ambition II.","Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","miximages/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png","marquees/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (NA).png"
"snes_romance-of-the-three-kingdoms-iii-dragon-of-destiny","Romance of the Three Kingdoms III : Dragon of Destiny","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-11-08","1992","1990s","Koei","Koei","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1","1","1","80","4","Romance of the Three Kingdoms III: Dragon of Destiny is another strategy game by Koei. The concept is the same here as in Koei's other wargames: conquer all you can!

In this case the historical setting is ancient China. The country is under the control of one Emperor, but all of the faction leaders want to be the emperor themselves. You begin with one territory and you must conquer more to win the game.","Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","miximages/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png","marquees/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms III - Dragon of Destiny (NA).png"
"snes_romance-of-the-three-kingdoms-iv-wall-of-fire","Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV : Wall of Fire","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Koei","Koei","Strategy","Strategy / Sim","1","1","1","70","3.5","The Three Kingdoms era of Chinese history is the setting for this strategic war game. Select one of 38 leaders and guide your people to power by military or diplomatic means in an isometrically viewed world. Military tactics such as well-placed taunts and deliberate enemy confusion are on offer. Weaponry includes catapults and automatic-firing crossbows.","Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","miximages/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png","marquees/snes/Romance of the Three Kingdoms IV - Wall of Fire (NA).png"
"snes_romancing-saga","Romancing Saga","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-28","1992","1990s","Square","Square","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","80","4","Once upon a time, three evil gods fought against the holy gods, and the holy God-Father chose a human warrior to help them. Finally, after a prolonged, terrible war, the leader of the three evil gods - Death, the eldest of them - surrendered. The youngest evil god, Schirach, became a human and has lost her powers. The middle one, Saruin, was sealed away in the dark world. Now, after thousand years have passed, and the seal of Saruin has weakened, it is time for you, the hero of the new generation, to defeat him.","Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","miximages/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","screenshots/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","titlescreens/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","3dboxes/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","backcovers/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png","marquees/snes/Romancing Sa-Ga (English Translated by DDSTranslation, Rev 1.2).png"
"snes_rudra-no-hihou","Rudra No Hihou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-04-05","1996","1990s","Square","Squaresoft","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","The Earth is ruled by heavenly powers, who destroy it and create it again every 4000 years, to end the live of powerful races and to let new ones appear. Many years ago, four great races dominated the Earth: the wise Danans, the beautiful Mermaids, the proud Reptiles, and the strong Giants. But those races had to give place to a newly born race: the humans. During the era of the humans, the planet's air has become filled with smog and the water dirty with pollutants. The cult of the Rudra have appeared and begun fortelling the end of the world. As the 4000 year deadline approaches, three characters each embark on their separate yet intertwined quests.","Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png","miximages/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png","screenshots/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png","titlescreens/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).jpg","3dboxes/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png","backcovers/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png","marquees/snes/Rudra no Hihou (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1b).png"
"snes_run-saber","Run Saber","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-09-02","1993","1990s","Horisoft","Atlus","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","In the year 2998, the brilliant Dr. Bruford is experimenting with genetic structures and radiation. He hopes to create new functions in nature that will remove the pollution from the Earth before it is too late. Unfortunately Dr. Bruford's experiments go wrong and genetic monsters are unleashed upon the world. The only thing that can stop these creatures are Dr. Bruford's previous achievement: Genetically enhanced warriors known as the Run Sabers. Two of the Run Sabers, Sheena and Allen are ready to activate.

Run Saber is a side-scrolling platform game similar in gameplay to Strider. Players assume the role of one or both of the Run Saber characters. Choosing either Sheena with her Ice powers or Allen with his Thunder powers, players must defeat mutant monsters. The Run Sabers can move side to side, jump in the air, attack with a sword, climb along walls or roofs, slide across the ground and drop-kick. Additionally, each character has a special move, which damages everything on the screen with either Ice or Lightning, and which can be activated by collecting orb power-ups. Other power-ups include small health, large health, extra life and a larger sword. Players progress through 5 different levels, often encountering powerful boss and miniboss mutant creatures.","Run Saber (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","miximages/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Run Saber (NA).png","marquees/snes/Run Saber (NA).png"
"snes_ryuuki-heidan-danzarb","Ryuuki Heidan Danzarb","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-04-23","1993","1990s","Pandora Box","Yutaka","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","Danzarb is a quirky sci-fi mecha RPG. It's segmented into chapters or episodes, each having a minigame or puzzle for you to solve. In one, for example, you're tasked with locating a bunch of ID card segments then you have to do a little sliding puzzle to put them together. In another, you collect clues to a time bomb defuse sequence from enemy soldiers, and you have to piece them together in a logic puzzle to decode the sequence.","Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Ryuuki Heidan - Danzarb (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_ryuuko-no-ken-2","Ryuuko no Ken 2","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-21","1994","1990s","Saurus","Saurus","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","In this sequel to the original Art of Fighting, and prequel to the original Fatal Fury, we learn that Mr. Karate, the final boss of the original game, behind the mask, is really Takuma Sakazaki, Ryo and Yuri's father, that Takuma was being used by Geese Howard for his own evil intentions, and that Mr. Big was the one who had originally kidnapped Yuri.

Many of the problems that existed in the first game have been fixed in this installment (it is easier to execute special moves, you no longer have only Ryo or Robert to choose from in 1 player mode, etc.).

The entire cast from the first Art of Fighting is here (with the exceptions of Todo and Mr. Karate), along with a few new characters: Takuma Sakazaki, Ryo and Yuri's father; Yuri Sakazaki, Takuma's daughter and Ryo's sister; Eiji Kisaragi, a sworn enemy of the Sakazakis; Temjin, a Mongolian trucker; and a younger version of Geese Howard, whom you face as the last boss if you beat the game without losing a round.","Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","miximages/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png","marquees/snes/Ryuuko no Ken 2 (JP).png"
"snes_saikyou-takada-nobuhiko","Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-12-27","1995","1990s","Dual","Hudson Soft","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Saikyou: Takada Nobuhiko (""The Strongest: Nobuhiko Takada"") is an MMA fighting game from Dual and Hudson Soft exclusive to the Super Famicom. It features the name and likeness of MMA fighter and shoot-style wrestling pioneer Nobuhiko Takada and the organization he founded, UWF (Union of Wrestling Force) International. The other wrestlers and fighters are talent signed onto the UWF but have had their names altered to avoid licensing issues, similar to how Fire Pro Wrestling handles its roster: ""Geiger"", for instance, is actually Big Van Vader.
The game features multiple modes including: Sparring, for practice; Story, for a series of fights against opponents; Vs. Single for one-off single matches; and Vs. Double for one off tag team matches.","Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","miximages/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png","marquees/snes/Saikyou - Takada Nobuhiko (JP).png"
"snes_same-game","Same Game","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-03-01","1996","1990s","Hudson Soft","Hudson Soft","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","60","3","A puzzle game based on the ""samegame"" model, where players remove grouped amount of icons with the intent to clear the entire board with no remainders. They can only remove icons if they are grouped together in groups of two and more, and only count as grouped if the icons are touching horizontally or vertically. The game features a number of Hudson characters, each of which is used as the basis for the puzzle icons. Depending on the difficulty, the player can expect to match icons based on Hudson's Momotaro Densetsu/Dentetsu series, Bomberman series, Bonk series, and the Milon games Milon's Secret Castle and the then-upcoming DoReMi Fantasy: Milon's DokiDoki Adventure.","Same Game (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Same Game (JP).png","miximages/snes/Same Game (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Same Game (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Same Game (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Same Game (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Same Game (JP).png","marquees/snes/Same Game (JP).png"
