"snes_sanrio-shanghai","Sanrio Shanghai","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-08-31","1994","1990s","SAS Sakata","Character Soft","Mahjong","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","50","2.5","Sanrio Shanghai is a mahjong solitaire licensed game that uses the world of Sanrio characters, such as Hello Kitty, KeroKeroKeroppi and others, for the various tiles that the player must remove. The game features a few modes: a single-player, and two competitive two-player modes. One of these multiplayer modes involves alternating turns, while the other has each player solve as much of the grid as possible within a time limit before switching over.","Sanrio Shanghai (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","miximages/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png","marquees/snes/Sanrio Shanghai (JP).png"
"snes_sanrio-world-smash-ball","Sanrio World Smash Ball!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-16","1993","1990s","Tomcat System","Character Soft","Action / Breakout games","Action","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Sanrio World Smash Ball! is a competitive multiplayer sport game similar to table tennis that uses four playable characters from Sanrio's wide catalog of cute mascots. The most famous Sanrio character, Hello Kitty, acts as referee for each contest. Each arena is subtly different, with multiple configurations of breakable blocks, impassable squares, walls and other obstacles that the two players can use to their advantage to win the match.","Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Sanrio World Smash Ball! (English Translated by Suicidal Translations With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
"snes_saturday-night-slam-masters","Saturday Night Slam Masters","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-09-01","1994","1990s","Capcom","Capcom","Sports","Sports","1-4","1","4","90","4.5","Slam Masters is an arcade conversion of Capcom's wrestling game of the same name.

Ten wrestlers with different fighting styles and individual special attacks are available. They are controlled with three buttons; one for jumping, one for attacking and one for pinning. When close to your opponents, you can grab them to perform various moves ranging from ordinary attacks to body slams to throws. Since the action takes place in a ring, it is also possible to climb the turnbuckle or use the ropes to your advantage. You can also leave the ring for a limited amount of time to fight with different weapons that can be found outside the ring.

Apart from a standard Single Match mode, the SNES version also features a Battle Royal mode for up to four players. This mode is not available in the Genesis version, which features a Death Match mode that can not be found in the SNES version. A Death Match is a no rules match in which weapons can be used used and the rope is replaced with barbed wire.","Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","miximages/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png","marquees/snes/Saturday Night Slam Masters (NA).png"
"snes_scooby-doo-mystery","Scooby-Doo Mystery","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-11-01","1995","1990s","Sunsoft","Acclaim Entertainment","Adventure","Adventure","1-4","1","4","60","3","This version is different from it's Genesis counterpart.  While the gameplay is similar (exploring levels to collect clues and solve the mystery), there are four different episodes for this version.  While visiting the Drabwell Ranch, ghost's interrupt the festivities and the gang must find out who is behind the hauntings and why.  Another adventure takes the gang to Deadman's Cove where a ghostly pirate has been scaring off tourists and it is up to Scooby and the Gang to bring it to a halt. The other two mysteries take place at a fun fair and a haunted mansion.

Players can use Scooby's unique sniffing ability to find clues. Scooby also consume Scooby Snacks to reduce the fright meter.  Additionally there are a series of mini-games that player can participate in such as ""Wac-A-Monster"" or ""Make a Scooby Sandwich"".  The graphics are faithfully recreated to resemble the long-running Hanna Barbara series.","Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","miximages/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png","marquees/snes/Scooby-Doo Mystery (NA).png"
"snes_sd-f-1-grand-prix","Sd F-1 Grand Prix","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-10-27","1995","1990s","Video System","Video System","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-2","1","2","80","4","SD F-1 Grand Prix is a comedic spin-off of Video Systems's F-1 Grand Prix series. Rather than featuring real teams and drivers from Formula 1, it features ten anthropomorphic characters riding very small versions of the various Formula 1 open-wheel vehicles. It still features the licenses of FOCA and Fuji Television, two organizations that control the Formula 1 licensing rights in Japan.

The game is loosely inspired by Super Mario Kart and its imitators. The game depicts the course from behind the player's vehicle, with the second half of the screen taken up by either the second human player (if applicable) or an overhead map of the course and the progress of all the competitors. Uncommon for a cart racer, SD F-1 Grand Prix allows the player to earn money based on how they ranked and use that money to upgrade their vehicle between races.","SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","miximages/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","screenshots/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","backcovers/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png","marquees/snes/SD F-1 Grand Prix (JP).png"
"snes_sd-gundam-power-formation-puzzle","Sd Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-01-26","1996","1990s","Tom Create","Bandai","Puzzle","Puzzle","1","1","1","70","3.5","SD Gundam Power Formation Puzzle is a licensed puzzle game based on the SD Gundam setting, which in turn is a spin-off from the Gundam universe. It is exclusive to the Super Famicom. It features a story mode, a time trial mode and a versus mode for multiplayer.
The puzzle game involves stacking colored blocks in rows across the bottom of the screen. When four blocks of the same color are adjacent to each other, though not necessarily in a straight horizontal or vertical line, they are destroyed and disappear. Placing four differently-colored blocks in a row will also cause them disappear.","SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","miximages/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","screenshots/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","backcovers/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png","marquees/snes/SD Gundam - Power Formation Puzzle (JP).png"
