"snes_squirrel","Squirrel","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1999-04-05","1999","1990s","DVS Electronic Co","Unknown","Unknown","Unknown","1","1","1","","","Squirrel is a port of Squirrel King for Super Nintendo. This version includes all the levels from the original game, although it completely removes the bosses, except for the final boss, which is entirely new. The game uses the same engine base as Bonkers for the same console, and shares its music and sound effects. Some enemy sprites and the title screen are modified from graphics taken from Trials of Mana. The game over screen is based on that of Super Mario Bros. 3.","Squirrel (TW).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Squirrel (TW).png","miximages/snes/Squirrel (TW).png","screenshots/snes/Squirrel (TW).jpg","titlescreens/snes/Squirrel (TW).jpg","3dboxes/snes/Squirrel (TW).png","backcovers/snes/Squirrel (TW).png","marquees/snes/Squirrel (TW).png"
"snes_st-andrews-eikou-to-rekishi-no-old-course","St Andrews - Eikou To Rekishi No Old Course","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-15","1995","1990s","Epoch","Epoch","Sports","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","St. Andrews: Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (""St. Andrews: Old Course of History and Glory"") is a golf game based on the eponymous prestigious golf course St. Andrews Links in Scotland. St. Andrews is frequently the home of The British Open (or simply The Open): one of the four major tournaments of professional golf.The game features a tournament mode, a single-player stroke play mode and a two-player match play. The player can also practice with the training mode. The game depicts each of its courses with a slight angle, and has a harsh learning curve as the player must hit the right trajectory of the swing as well as the power level.","St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","miximages/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","screenshots/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","backcovers/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png","marquees/snes/St. Andrews - Eikou to Rekishi no Old Course (JP).png"
"snes_starwing","Starwing","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-03","1993","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","80","4","This is the first SNES title using the Super FX chip; you are flying ace Fox McCloud, who with his trusted wingmen and his powerful Arwing starfighter, must rid the Lylat System of the evil that surrounds it. Wave after wave of enemies will fly towards you, and with the advice of your wingmen, you must guide your way through each level and take down as many as you can.","Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","miximages/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","screenshots/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","titlescreens/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","3dboxes/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","backcovers/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png","marquees/snes/Star Fox (NA, Rev 2).png"
"snes_starwing-competition","Starwing : Competition","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-05-01","1993","1990s","Argonaut Software","Nintendo","Shoot'em Up","Shooter","1","1","1","90","4.5","Star Fox Competition Weekend Edition is a rare version of Star Fox (StarWing in Europe) that was sold as a promotional item in certain shops. In Europe &amp; North America it was used for competitions (local and nationwide) to see who could get the most points. Prizes included Nintendo T-Shirts, promotional items, or even holiday destinations.

This version has a different game system.
Instead of seeing the map screen, you will immediately get stuck in. You will see that there is a time limit, and a score.
As far as can be seen, you can't get past Stage 3, which was specially made for the promotion.

