"snes_super-loopz","Super Loopz","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-04","1994","1990s","Graffiti","Imagineer","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Super Loopz is a revamped version of Loopz. Feature enhanced graphics and new musics, but the same gameplay: sort of Tetris encounter Pipe Dream. Tubes of random shapes drop on to a board, and by rotating and placing them you have to make shapes that join up in loops. Once you have placed a piece you can't remove it, except by using a special items which destroy every part of an incomplete loop it touches; every tenth loop takes you to a new level.","Super Loopz (JP).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","miximages/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png","marquees/snes/Super Loopz (JP).png"
"snes_super-mad-champ","Super Mad Champ","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-04","1995","1990s","Givro","Tsukuda Original","Racing, Driving","Racing","1","1","1","80","4","Super Mad Champ is a motorcycle racing game for the Super Famicom. Unlike many others for the system, Super Mad Champ has a lot of combat: the player fights both on and off their motorcycle, often having to take out opponents on the ground than in the race. When the player crashes, they have to fight another opponent on-foot before they can escape. The player can earn and tune up new bikes by completing races, and the game features eighteen tracks (across three Grand Prix cups) in total.","Super Mad Champ (JP).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","miximages/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png","marquees/snes/Super Mad Champ (JP).png"
"snes_super-mario-all-stars","Super Mario All-Stars","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-16","1993","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","80","4","Super Mario All-Stars is a compilation on Super Nintendo of all the platform games featuring Mario released for Nintendo. It includes : Super Mario Bros. 1, 2 and 3 as well as Super Mario Bros.: The Lost Levels, which was never released in Europe before. More than a simple compilation, the game brings improved graphics and the possibility to save your progress. Nostalgic fans of the first Nintendo console will be pleased.","Super Mario All-Stars (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Mario All-Stars (NA).png"
"snes_super-mario-kart","Super Mario Kart","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-21","1993","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Racing TPV","Racing","1-2","1","2","80","4","A collection of Super Mario characters, including Mario, Luigi, Yoshi, Peach, Bowser and Donkey Kong Jr., are out to win the Super Mario Kart championship. Using Mode 7 scaling, the first true kart game on the SNES has you collecting coins to boost your speed while using shells and bananas to stop others on the track. 

There are 4 cups in all to race for, Mushroom Cup, Flower Cup, Star Cup and Special Cup, each with a selection of tracks to race on (based in Bowser's Castle, Super Mario World and more). Plus, a 2 player battle mode is on offer, where the first to pop the three balloons wins.","Super Mario Kart (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Mario Kart (NA).png"
"snes_super-mario-rpg-legend-of-the-seven-stars","Super Mario RPG : Legend of the Seven Stars","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-05-13","1996","1990s","Square","Nintendo","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","90","4.5","Super Mario RPG is a console RPG starring none other than Nintendo's popular character Mario. This game starts off like almost every other Mario game, with Bowser kidnapping Princess Toadstool. However, when Mario goes to Bowser's Keep to save her, a giant sword made by the evil Smithy crashes into the castle and sends Mario, the Princess, and Bowser flying to separate parts of the world.

The game plays much like a traditional RPG: Mario and the members of his party gain experience points by defeating enemies. These experience points can increase the team's maximum number of hit points and flower points (magic points). The battles are also turn-based, but this time there are timed attacks and defenses. Timed attacks allow you to inflict more harm than normal to an enemy. Timed defenses, on the other hand, allow an enemy to inflict less harm than normal. You are only allowed to have three out of five team members on your party at a time. Mario is always in your party, regardless. Your team members can also purchase more weapons and abilities, along with better armor.","Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Mario RPG - Legend of the Seven Stars (NA).png"
"snes_super-mario-world","Super Mario World","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-04-11","1992","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform / Run &amp; Jump","Platformer","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","Once again, the evil Bowser has captured Princess Peach (while on a holiday on Yoshi's Island), and it's up to Mario to save her. To aid him in his quest, Mario must use the flying ability of the feather to get him to places hard to reach. But what's even more important is his new friend, the dinosaur Yoshi, who Mario can ride through each level and eat the enemies.

Over 90 levels are on offer, with plenty of Bowser's evil koopas and new characters around each level. Apart from saving Peach, you can also save some of Yoshi's friends, other dinosaurs that each have a special attack. Each level also contains a set of extra items to collect, including dinosaur coins, and break the tape at the end of each level to collect stars, that allow you to open up the bonus games.","Super Mario World (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Mario World (NA).png"
"snes_super-mario-world-2-yoshi-s-island","Super Mario World 2 : Yoshi's Island","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-10-06","1995","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","100","5","The events of Yoshi's Island take place in the past. Mario was one of two twin brothers whom the evil Magikoopa Kamek tried to kidnap. Kamek kidnapped one of the brothers, but the second, Mario, fell down on Yoshi's island, where Yoshis live. The Yoshis decided to carry baby Mario and to bring him to his parents. But Kamek has sent troops and hordes of monsters to stop the Yoshis and to kidnap Mario.

In the game, players control the differently colored Yoshis most of the time. Mario is a helpless baby unless a star that makes him invincible is collected. As a Yoshi, players can swallow enemies to make eggs; these eggs are used for attacking other enemies and activating switches. Yoshi can do a flutter jump to clear large gaps that a regular jump can't. Players can also pound the ground for attacks and also flipping certain switches. Additionally, Yoshis can transform into vehicles, such as a helicopter, submarine, or a train. If Mario is dismounted from a Yoshi, a countdown will start, and Yoshi must catch the baby before Kamek's servants kidnap it. When Mario is invincible, he doesn't ride on Yoshi and is immune to attack. While invincible, Mario can run up certain walls.","Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Mario World 2 - Yoshi's Island (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_super-metroid","Super Metroid","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-07-28","1994","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","Join forces with the power of Samus for a Super Metroid clash!
Take on the legion of space pirates and take on a new Metroid force as you progress through the underworld of the planet Zebes! It's up to you and Samus to find the larval Metroid before evil forces misuse its energy.
Every vicious corner, every hidden passageway on the planet Zebes holds menacing creatures ready for battle... including the horrible Ridley and the giant lizard, Kraid. Use your lethal somersault to make enemies bite the dust. Use an electric beam through the gaps. No one will stop you or Samus. But beware! It seems that Mother Brain is back...","Super Metroid (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Metroid (NA).png"
"snes_super-morph","Super Morph","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","Millenium Interactive","Sony Electronic Publishing","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","Young Morris Rolph has been trapped in limbo due to a malfunction on Dr Krankenpot's new teleport machine (how come characters with German-sounding names in computer games and films are always evil or stupid? Even Darth Vader sounds German.). Now he's been split into his 4 component parts, represented by liquid, gas, a hard ball and a bouncy ball, and he must get through 24 levels (36 on the special A1200 version - mirroring the game's plot-line of improved technology causing more problems for Morris) to retrieve the parts of the machine so it can be rebuilt to bring him back to life.

The gameplay is vaguely influenced by Lemmings, as the levels are populated by lots of points which can't be crossed in all forms - walls, fires, gaps, thornbushes and suchlike. You get a set number of transformations on each level, although extra ones can be picked up at times, and there are heaters and freezers dotted around which cause transformations in some states. All this is done against a time limit. Like Lemmings 2, you can take on the next level in each of the worlds at any time, so you have to be stuck 4 times before you can't progress.","Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","miximages/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","screenshots/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","backcovers/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png","marquees/snes/Super Morph (NTSC Conversion).png"
"snes_super-naxat-open-golf-de-shoubu-da-dorabocchan","Super Naxat Open - Golf De Shoubu Da Dorabocchan","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-18","1994","1990s","Kuusou Kagaku","Naxat Soft","Sports / Golf","Sports","1","1","1","70","3.5","Super Naxat Open is the spiritual successor to Naxat Open, a golf game that Naxat published and lent their name to. The game uses the standard top-down perspective for golf games from this era.

It is unusual for the inclusion of Naxat's Dorabocchan (""Little Dorabo"") character, who had appeared in two games at this point: 1990's Makai Prince Dorabocchan for the PC Engine and 1993's Chou Makai Taisen: Dorabocchan for SNES, best known as The Twisted Tales of Spike McFang in the US. Dorabocchan/Spike's inherent magical talent allows him to beneficially manipulate his game, which appears in the form of magical cards he can play before taking a swing. The player can also choose to play as five other characters from the franchise, as well as a choice of six caddies.","Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","miximages/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","screenshots/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","backcovers/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png","marquees/snes/Super Naxat Open - Golf de Shoubu da! Dorabocchan (English Translated by MrRichard999, Rev 0.99 With FastROM Hack by Kandowontu).png"
"snes_super-nazo-puyo-rulue-no-roux","Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue No Roux","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-05-26","1995","1990s","Compile","Banpresto","Puzzle / Fall","Puzzle","1","1","1","70","3.5","A spin-off of the successful action puzzle series Puyo Puyo with a twist. Whereas the main Puyo Puyo games are aimed at defeating AI or human controlled opponents in puzzle duels (where each player has his own play area in which color-coded ""puyos"" fall and must clear them out faster than his opponent). Nazo Puyo instead challenges the player with different single-player scenarios in which a specific objective must be completed (such as clearing only the red Puyos, or performing a specific sequence of combos, etc.).

The game uses the mold of a top-down RPG in which you control the young magician Arle as she explores towns and dungeons in her search for adventure. Each quest involves finding an NPC who will trigger one of the aforementioned scenarios and rewards Arle with experience points and unique items that increase her stats.","Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","miximages/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png","marquees/snes/Super Nazo Puyo - Rulue no Roux (English Translated by Momochi, Rev 1.1).png"
"snes_super-ninja-boy","Super Ninja Boy","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-04-02","1993","1990s","Culture Brain","Culture Brain","Role Playing Game","RPG","1-2","1","2","90","4.5","One day a spaceship came down from the heavens and into the skies of Chinaland. It began broadcasting an image of peace. Soon Emperor Chin was holding a peace conference with the space ship's leader, Rub-A-Doc. Rub-A-Doc visited with some of the other places in the world before departing to the heavens and leaving the world. All seemed peaceful however there seems to be trouble starting up, strange monsters have been seen throughout Chinaland. Could the increase in monsters be somehow related to the recent visit from Rub-A-Doc? The ninja masters of Mount Edin, Jack and Ryu are sent to investigate...

Super Ninja Boy is an action RPG for one or two players. In a two player game, each player controls either Jack or Ryu. In a one player game, only Jack appears.A second player may join at any time by visiting a convenience store.

As mentioned, this game is an RPG and contains familiar RPG elements such as equipping differing kinds of weapons, equipment and gaining levels. The Ninja Boys are treated as a single entity and so these statistics are shared between Jack and Ryu with equal values. Battles with the enemy, either through random encounters or visible on the map is where the action starts. The characters enter a side-scrolling 'battle mode' where they may jump, fight, use their special techniques, items and spells... as well as activate various 'bonuses' on the battlefield by breaking open the bonus boxes. A battle is over and experience points awarded when a pre-determined number of enemies are defeated or the Ninja Boys are defeated.

When bosses are encountered, the gameplay changes to a turn-based system. Each turn, commands are given to Jack &amp; Ryu, which include usage of special items and skills accuired during the game, who then perform actions based on those commands. The enemy's turn takes place the same way. The battle ends when one side of the conflict runs out of hitpoints.","Super Ninja Boy (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Ninja Boy (NA).png"
"snes_super-ninja-kun","Super Ninja-kun","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-08-05","1994","1990s","Jaleco","Jaleco","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Super Ninja-Kun is a 1994 Japan-only side-scrolling action game developed and published by Jaleco for the SNES. It is a graphically updated version of UPL's Ninja-kun Arcade games from the 1980s, which Jaleco had previously adapted for consoles (as well as creating their own spin-offs, named Ninja Jajamaru-kun).

As well as featuring 16-bit graphics the game now also features a story mode, bosses, an upgradable health gauge (the Arcade game only allowed Ninja-kun to take a single hit) and multiple weapon types. Ninja-kun can also create a powerful one-off magic blast by collecting the souls of dead enemies as they float off the screen. The game also includes a two-player cooperative mode.

Super Ninja-kun is not to be confused with Super Ninja Boy, another ninja-themed action game for the SNES.","Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","miximages/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","screenshots/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","backcovers/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png","marquees/snes/Super Ninja-kun (English Translated by Diogo Ribeiro).png"
