"snes_super-solitaire","Super Solitaire","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-01-02","1994","1990s","Beam Software","Extreme Entertainment","Playing cards","Board / Casual","1","1","1","60","3","Super Solitaire is a SNES version of the classic solitaire card game with 12 varieties of play (Klondike, Free Cell, Golf, Cruel, Pyramid, Stonewall, Doesn't Matter, Aces Up, Florentine, Poker, Canfield and Scorpion). It features classic artwork for backgrounds along with optional cartoony graphics. Very soft music is included on the track. The game also sports options where you can choose to skip to the next card when stuck or get a hint on what to do.","Super Solitaire (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Solitaire (NA).png"
"snes_super-soukoban","Super Soukoban","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-29","1993","1990s","Pack In Video","Pack In Video","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Super Sokoban (or Soukoban) is a puzzle game in which the player must push crates around a maze to their designated storage area, ensuring they do not accidentally push a crate into a position where it cannot be recovered. Thinking Rabbit created the original game in 1982 on home computers, and Super Sokoban is the first Super Famicom game to follow the same blueprint.

The intro establishes that the eponymous Sokoban, or warehouse worker, must perform enough box-pushing puzzles to earn enough cash for a flashy new car so that he might finally impress the girl of his dreams.

An added wrinkle is that each stage has a fixed move limit, and so the player must not only push the boxes in the right order but do so with maximum efficiency. The game was not released outside of Japan.","Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","miximages/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","screenshots/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","backcovers/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png","marquees/snes/Super Soukoban (English Translated by MrRichard999 and Krom, Rev 0.5).png"
"snes_super-star-wars","Super Star Wars","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-04-02","1993","1990s","Sculptured Software","JVC","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","The story of this game is based on Episode IV: A New Hope, the first of the original Star Wars movie trilogy. The player initially controls the movie's main hero, Luke Skywalker, but two more controllable characters join later: the careless captain Han Solo and his Wookie side-kick, Chewbacca. The game generally follows the movie plot, allowing the player to experience some of the most important sequences, such as the search for Obi-Wan Kenobi, rescuing princess Leia, and the final confrontation against Death Star and its TIE Fighters, led by the mysterious Darth Vader.

Most of the game levels follow traditional platform gameplay style. As Luke, Han, or Chewbacca, the player makes his way through the linear levels, fighting enemies, jumping over obstacles, and collecting power-ups. The power-ups include standard health-restoring items, upgrades for the blaster gun , as well as special items that can be used for massive destruction of the enemies.","Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Star Wars (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_super-star-wars-return-of-the-jedi","Super Star Wars : Return of the Jedi","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-30","1995","1990s","Sculptured Software","JVC","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","You select your character from the movie (Luke Skywalker, Han Solo, Princess Leia, or Chewbacca) before each level, as each one has different abilities, and go try to beat levels based on the movie's scenes brandishing lightsabers, blasters or crossbows, etc. depending on your choice of character.

Gameplay consists of standard platform action spiced up with some slightly different (gameplay-wise) sequences. Race to Jabba the Hutt's palace in a speeder, fight your way through Jabba the Hutt's minions before killing Jabba himself, control speeder bikes and the Millennium Falcon, and much more.","Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Star Wars - Return of the Jedi (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_super-star-wars-the-empire-strikes-back","Super Star Wars : The Empire Strikes Back","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-02-24","1994","1990s","Sculptured Software","JVC","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","80","4","Super Star Wars: The Empire Strikes Back takes you on an action-packed journey through iconic moments – from the icy battles on Hoth to the mysterious training with Yoda and the dramatic confrontation with Darth Vader. Many scenes are expanded or reimagined, adding new locations and enemies while introducing surprising twists. You switch between Luke, Han and Chewbacca, each playing differently with unique special abilities – from destructive spins to Luke's lightsaber defense and unlockable Force powers. Alongside classic platforming sections, vehicle stages in the snowspeeder, Millennium Falcon and X-Wing deliver cinematic variety, while hidden collectibles, upgrades and new powers constantly encourage you to explore every stage thoroughly.","Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Star Wars - The Empire Strikes Back (NA, Rev 1).png"
"snes_super-street-fighter-ii","Super Street Fighter II","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-09-01","1994","1990s","Capcom","Capcom","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","This is the sequel to the super hit Street Fighter II Turbo and Street Fighter II Championship games for the SNES and Genesis. This port of the arcade game featured all 4 new characters and stages making a total of 16 playable. The game was packed into massive 32Meg and 40Meg cartridges for the SNES and Genesis. Featured many multiplayer modes in addition to the single player mode. There was the returning elimination group battle where you and a group of people played until one person was the champ. The Point Battle where the person with the most points wins. Newer modes included Tournament where you had an 8 man double elimination tournament. Finally the Challenge mode, where you tried to get the most points on a CPU opponent or beat them real fast trying to surpass records.","Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Street Fighter II (NA, Rev 1).png"
