"snes_super-tekkyuu-fight","Super Tekkyuu Fight!","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-09-15","1995","1990s","Metro 3D","Banpresto","Action / Labyrinth","Action","1-4","1","4","70","3.5","Super Tekkyuu Fight! (""Super Iron Ball Fight!"") is a maze action game featuring characters from the Compati Hero Series, which combines the worlds of Ultraman, SD Gundam, Kamen Rider and the original character Fighter Roar. Rather than relying on explosives, as is the case in the structurally similar Bomberman series, each character has a ball and chain which allows them to damage opponents and destroy blocks. They can acquire bonuses that increases the size of the ball, which causes it to do more damage, and the length of the chain, which increases its range.

The game features a single-player mode where the player takes one of the four protagonists through a series of maze stages that can be attempted in any order, each of which concludes with a boss fight. It also features a multiplayer mode where up to four players can compete for supremacy across a number of stages. Many enemies in the single-player mode use other weapons, like guns, missiles and flamethrowers.","Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).sfc","SFC","1.25","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","miximages/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png","marquees/snes/Super Tekkyuu Fight! (JP).png"
"snes_super-tennis","Super Tennis","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-06-04","1992","1990s","Tokyo Shoseki","Nintendo","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","The world circuit of tennis awaits you. Select from 20 male and female players, each with their own style of game play (strengths and weaknesses) and play singles, doubles either against or with a human opponent or a CPU controlled player(s) on all the major surfaces (clay, grass &amp; hardcourt).

The circuit mode is the meat of the game. Here, using a password save system, you play a selection of tournaments all around the world. The more games you play and win, the more points you receive. These points go towards your rank in the world tour rankings. There are 4 major tournaments (as in real life) and many minor tournaments to play.","Super Tennis (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","miximages/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png","marquees/snes/Super Tennis (NA).png"
"snes_super-tetris-2-bombliss","Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-12-18","1992","1990s","TOSE","Bullet Proof Software","Compilation","Compilation","1-2","1","2","80","4","Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss is one of the many versions of the famous soviet puzzle game, the first one available for the Super Nintendo, and an updated version of Tetris 2 + Bombliss.

As the title would suggest, the game features both standard Tetris as well as another similar game called Bombliss. Tetris offers three game modes: standard marathon (A-type), start with garbage (B-type), and rising garbage (C-type). There is also a two-player versus mode where clearing rows sends garbage to the other player.

Bombliss is slightly different from normal Tetris. Arranged on the board and in some dropped pieces are bomb squares. Completing a row does not clear the blocks in the row, but will trigger any bombs in the row which will destroy blocks. Different sizes of bombs will generate larger explosions. Bombliss is offered in standard marathon mode, a puzzle mode where a set board must be cleared using set piece drops, and a 2-player versus mode.","Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","miximages/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","screenshots/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","backcovers/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png","marquees/snes/Super Tetris 2 + Bombliss (JP, Rev 1).png"
"snes_super-tetris-3","Super Tetris 3","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-16","1994","1990s","TOSE","Bullet Proof Software","Puzzle / Fall","Puzzle","1-4","1","4","100","5","Super Tetris 3 is yet another version of the famous soviet puzzle game. However, despite what the title may suggest, contained within are actually four different versions of Tetris: Tetris Classic, Familiss, Sparkliss, and Magicaliss. Tetris Classic, of course, is the basic version of the game, which comes with an endless mode and a 25-line ""standard"" mode. Familiss, as the name might suggest, is a family mode for up to four players simultaneously. Sparkliss, meanwhile, is very similar to Bombliss from Super Tetris 2, only with a medieval fantasy theme and different explosion patterns. Sparkliss includes both a stage mode and a puzzle mode.","Super Tetris 3 (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","miximages/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png","marquees/snes/Super Tetris 3 (JP).png"
