"snes_wwf-royal-rumble","WWF Royal Rumble","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-01","1993","1990s","LJN","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Fighting","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Control one of twelve World Wrestling Federation stars in a ""no holds barred brawl"" -- in other words, do whatever moves you want, from choke holds, piledrivers, and even chair slams, until your opponent is knocked out. You can even go outside the ring if you like. Several modes of play are available, including one-on-one, tag team (two-on-two), and three-on-three tag team. You can play as the following WWF wresters: Bret Hart, Razor Ramon, Randy Savage, Shawn Michaels, The Undertaker, Tatanka, Mr. Perfect, Crush, Ric Flair, Yokozuna, Ted Dibiase, and Lex Luger.","WWF Royal Rumble (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","miximages/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","screenshots/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","backcovers/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png","marquees/snes/WWF Royal Rumble (NA).png"
"snes_wwf-super-wrestlemania","WWF Super WrestleMania","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-10-22","1992","1990s","LJN","Sculptured Software","Sports / Fighting","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","This was the first ever WWF licensed game for the SNES and Sega Genesis consoles. It featured one-on-one,  tag team, and Survivor Series matches. The two versions were not identical because they featured some different characters for each console. The Genesis had: Hulk Hogan, Papa Shango, Ultimate Warrior, Macho Man, British Bulldog, IRS, Ted Dibiase, Shawn Michaels. Meanwhile the SNES had: Macho Man, Undertaker, Jake The Snake, Hulk Hogan, Earthquake, The Road Warriors, Typhoon, Ted Dibiase, and Sid Justice.","WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","miximages/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","screenshots/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","backcovers/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png","marquees/snes/WWF Super WrestleMania (NA).png"
"snes_wwf-wrestlemania-the-arcade-game","WWF WrestleMania : The Arcade Game","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1996-01-25","1996","1990s","Sculptured Software","Acclaim Entertainment","Sports / Fighting","Sports","1-2","1","2","80","4","WWF Wrestlemania: The Arcade Game merges the personalities and action of the popular WWF TV show characters with a Mortal Kombat-style fighting game. This title features digitized graphics of 8 WWF superstars: Doink, Razor Ramon, Bret ""Hit Man"" Hart, Shawn Michaels, The Undertaker, Yokozuna, Bam Bam Bigelow, and Lex Luger.

Although it is a wrestling title, this game is structured a lot like a one-on-one fighter. It is very fast-paced, and features crazy special moves. Also, each match is divided into a series of rounds, much like a fighting game. Instead of each round ending in a KO, each round simply ends when you force the other wrestler(s) to fall by reducing their energy meter. Game modes include single player arcade mode, tournament mode, and vs mode for 1-on-1 multiplayer.","WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","miximages/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","screenshots/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","backcovers/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png","marquees/snes/WWF WrestleMania - The Arcade Game (NA).png"
"snes_x-zone","X Zone","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-06-24","1993","1990s","Kemco","Kemco","Lightgun Shooter","Shooter","1","1","1","50","2.5","Compond X's powerful bio-computer has begun to malfunction and ""X-Tra"", aka the external threat recognition assault system, is firing on anything that moves. In fact, the computer has accessed the nation's nuclear weapon countdown systems for a retaliatory strike. Since nuclear war might X-tinguish life on the planet, the player must enter the X-Termination zone of the compound and fight on through defenses to the central computer.

X-Zone is a first person light gun game. The player must shoot the robotic enemies on the screen and the projectiles that they launch at the player. Most levels are ended with a battle against a boss, which follows specific patterns and whose systems/weapons must be shot at and destroyed individually.","X Zone (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/X Zone (NA).png","miximages/snes/X Zone (NA).png","screenshots/snes/X Zone (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/X Zone (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/X Zone (NA).png","backcovers/snes/X Zone (NA).png","marquees/snes/X Zone (NA).png"
"snes_x-kaliber-2097","X-Kaliber 2097","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-01","1994","1990s","Athena","Activision","Platform","Platformer","1-2","1","2","60","3","The game takes place in the year 2097 A.D. in NEO N.Y. The city is run down and controlled by a warlord named Raptor. Raptor has unleashed an army of ruthless, mutating Morphs upon the city to enforce his brand of order. Only two special forces agents, Slash and Alix do not submit to Raptor's will. Raptor decides to hatch a plan to kidnap Alix, so Slash, and more importantly his magic X-Kaliber sword, will come to him in an attempt to try and rescue her.

The game consists of two modes. The first modes gameplay consists of side-scrolling action. The player can jump and swing a sword at enemies. The player can also deflect projectiles with the sword. The game also features one on one boss fights that are set up similar to a fighting game. The second mode is a two player duel that is set up like the one on one boss fights but is between two human controlled players.","X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","miximages/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","screenshots/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","backcovers/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png","marquees/snes/X-Kaliber 2097 (NA).png"
"snes_x-men-mutant-apocalypse","X-Men : Mutant Apocalypse","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-01-01","1995","1990s","Capcom","Capcom","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","Because of a rise of anti-mutant activity in Genosha, Professor Xavier uses Cerebro to scan the island nation and discovers Apocalypse's presence there. Genosha's government is apparently using Apocalypse to bring their mutant population under control. However, Xavier suspects Apocalypse to follow his own plans and sends five X-Men, Cyclops, Wolverine, Psylocke, Beast and Gambit, to investigate.

X-Men: Mutant Apocalypse combines the gameplay of a side-scrolling brawler like Final Fight with elements of a platformer, that is, players have to fight various enemies and survive the occasional jumping-puzzle. Most levels also feature mid- and end-bosses in form of robots, monsters or known Super-Villains.

Each character has unique abilities and special moves: Cyclops uses his Optic Blast to attack enemies from a distance, Wolverine can climb walls, Psylocke is very agile and the best jumper of the team, Beast can cling to the ceiling while Gambit throws explosive cards at his enemies. Special moves are quite similar to those found in Street Fighter II (Wolverine has a Dragon-Punch-style attack and Gambit's Double Kick is virtually identical to M. Bison's Scissors Kick), and require similar button combinations.

While most stages are unique for each character, some can be played with any of the X-Men. Progress is saved via password.","X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","miximages/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","screenshots/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","backcovers/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png","marquees/snes/X-Men - Mutant Apocalypse (NA).png"
"snes_x-men-vs-street-fighter","X-Men vs. Street Fighter","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1998-02-01","1998","1990s","Unknown","DVS Electronic","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","","","X-Men VS Street Fighter is an unlicensed one-on-one fighting game for the SNES programmed by an unknown company. Although it uses the name of the arcade title, it does not have a tag feature and has some characters that were not used in the original game (most notably Ice Man and Psylocke from X-Men: Children of the Atom.)","X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","miximages/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","screenshots/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","titlescreens/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","3dboxes/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","backcovers/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png","marquees/snes/X-Men Vs. Street Fighter (TW).png"
"snes_xak-the-art-of-visual-stage","Xak - The Art Of Visual Stage","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-02-26","1993","1990s","Microcabin","Sunsoft","Japanese RPG","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","You are a young boy named Latok, who lives in a village, training to become a good fighter. One day he meets a Pixie who tells him his father was a godly warrior who has fought the evil demon Badou two hundred fifty years ago, and has sealed his soul. But now the seal was broken, and Latok, as the last in the great family of warriors, has to prevent Badou's resurrection at all costs!

This is an action RPG, in which you fight enemies in real time. Defeated enemies give you experience points, and you level up as you gather them. You need to meet minimum level requirements in order to wield better weapons and to equip better armor.","Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","miximages/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","screenshots/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","backcovers/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png","marquees/snes/Xak - The Art of Visual Stage (English Translated by Dynamic Designs, Rev 1.1).png"
"snes_xardion","Xardion","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-04-01","1992","1990s","Jorudan","Asmik","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","50","2.5","Xardion tells the tale of 3 warring planets that are home to 3 distinct alien races. Their fighting is interrupted by the threat of invasion from a much more potent alien race. The 3 planets find that they are able to set aside their differences long enough to battle their shared menace.

Each planet sends a warrior to deal with the threat. There is a robot named Triton, a cat-like quadruped named Panthera, and a giant red bipedal insect alien name Alcedes. The single player of the game controls one of these characters at a time and can switch at any time. If the current character loses all of his health, the player is sent back to the start of the level and that one character receives a health refill.

Each character has special attributes that help the team. For example, Panthera's relatively small size allows him to squeeze into spots where the other 2 characters can not travel. The characters can gather a number of unique powerups and weapons to help them on their mission.","Xardion (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Xardion (NA).png","miximages/snes/Xardion (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Xardion (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Xardion (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Xardion (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Xardion (NA).png","marquees/snes/Xardion (NA).png"
"snes_yoshi-s-cookie","Yoshi's Cookie","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-01-01","1993","1990s","Bullet Proof Software","Bullet Proof Software","Puzzle","Puzzle","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Yoshi's Cookie is a tile-matching video game in which the player is given a playing field populated with cookies of several types, arranged in a rectangular grid. The main objective of each level is to clear the playing field of all the cookies. The player mixes and matches the cookies such that entire rows or columns consist only of cookies of the same type. The player controls a cursor on the grid that is used to rotate individual lines in a manner similar to a Rubik's Cube. When a single row or column contains all matching cookies, the row is cleared from the grid. The grid grows in size from cookies entering from the top and right sides of the playing field and a game over occurs when the grid overflows. A sixth cookie type, shaped like Yoshi's head, occasionally appears that acts as a wild card, used to help clear lines of any other cookie.","Yoshi's Cookie (NA).sfc","SFC","0.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","miximages/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png","marquees/snes/Yoshi's Cookie (NA).png"
"snes_yoshi-s-safari","Yoshi's Safari","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-09-01","1993","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Lightgun Shooter","Shooter","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","In Yoshi's Safari, Bowser, King of the Koopas, and his evil offspring have invaded Jewelry Land. The Koopalings have seized the Kingdom's precious jewels. It's up to Mario, armed with his trusty Super Scope, to ride Yoshi to track down Bowser and put a stop to his evil plans.

Unusual for the Mario games, Yoshi's Safari uses a first-person perspective. Gameplay consists of shooting enemies before they attack. Hidden areas and other secrets can be unlocked by shooting at large gates. There are bosses along the way that must be destroyed before players can move on to the next area. Game supports single or multi-player game modes.","Yoshi's Safari (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","miximages/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png","marquees/snes/Yoshi's Safari (NA).png"
"snes_youkai-buster-ruka-no-daibouken","Youkai Buster - Ruka No Daibouken","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-06-09","1995","1990s","Sting Entertainment","Taito","Platform","Platformer","1","1","1","70","3.5","Game developers Sting' were contracted by the publishing comglomerate Kadokawa Shoten for a tie-in game for their gaming magazine Marukatsu Super Famicom, which had recently just started pushing a new mascot named Ruka (with an accompanying mini manga in each issue) to try and help boost sales of the magazine. Rather than create a new game from scratch, Sting opted to re-brand the already existing ""The Jetsons: Invasion of the Planet Pirates"" game they had released the year before, which had not been released in Japan. The gameplay and level design between the two games is ultimately the same, though the story, sprites, and artwork were completely redone, as well as music.

Ruka-chan was a fiesty teenage-looking demon girl that was actually over 1000 years old. Her task was to rid the world of demons and monsters that were causing more trouble than she was. Ruka's boss Enma, the Buddhist deity charged with judging the souls of the dead - an Asian St. Peter, as it were - is represented as a giant red oni in a business suit and jokingly referred to by Ruka as ""Elvis"". One night his body was stolen by a handsome boy known only as, well, ""Body"" to be used for nefarious purposes along with his army of monsters. Left with only his head, Enma scolds and demands Ruka to get his body back or else he'll gladly take hers as a replacement.","Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","miximages/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png","marquees/snes/Youkai Buster - Ruka no Daibouken (JP).png"
"snes_young-merlin","Young Merlin","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-03-31","1994","1990s","Virgin","Westwood  Studios","Adventure","Adventure","1","1","1","80","4","After trying to rescue a drowning young woman, the the hero of Young Merlin is drawn into a fantasy world which is threatened by the evil Shadow King. Naturally, it is your job to save this world and also to rescue the aforementioned woman who has been kidnapped by the Shadow King. This is done in typical action adventure fashion: you have to fight enemies using spells that are gathered during the progress of the game as well as solve puzzles and use several objects. What sets Young Merlin apart from other action adventures is the fact that there is no text in the entire game, instead, icons are used to represent conversations, furthermore, the game uses passwords,instead of a battery to record the players progress.","Young Merlin (NA).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","miximages/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png","marquees/snes/Young Merlin (NA).png"
"snes_ys-iii-wanderers-from-ys","Ys III : Wanderers from Ys","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1992-01-02","1992","1990s","Nihon Falcom","Tonkin House","Action RPG","RPG","1","1","1","90","4.5","Adol's long time friend, Dogi wishes to return to his home town of Redmont after hearing about strange disturbances that are happening there.  Always looking for an adventure, Adol joins him and soon finds out the cause of this new evil.

Ys III breaks away from the ""bumping into enemies"" battle system of the first two games and allows Adol to control his sword in a variety of directions.  As well as changing the battle system, the game is now side scrolling instead of the previous games top-down view. Adol also now has the ability to jump.  Not new to the series is the use of magical rings which give Adol different powering up abilities such as healing and shielding.  The game also uses various key items found along the way to solve minor puzzles and progress the story.","Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).sfc","SFC","1","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","miximages/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","screenshots/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","titlescreens/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","3dboxes/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","backcovers/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png","marquees/snes/Ys III - Wanderers from Ys (NA).png"
"snes_ys-4-mask-of-the-sun","Ys 4 - Mask Of The Sun","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-11-19","1993","1990s","Nihon Falcom","Tonkin House","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","50","2.5","After having defeated the ultimate evil in his previous adventure, the great hero Adol stands on a sea shore, thinking about the fate of the once peaceful Utopian land of Ys, while the wind is blowing through his red hair. Suddenly, Adol notices a bottle, thrown onto the shore. It contains a letter, written in a foreign language, in which an unknown person asks Adol to come to his land and save it from a mysterious evil. Adol decides to accept the offer, and thus his adventure begins again...

Ys IV: Mask of the Sun is an action RPG with a combat system like the earlier Ys games. An enemy should be rammed from the sides in order to hurt him. If performed incorrectly, the player will get hurt himself. It's also possible to use magic and position oneself strategically in battle so that the enemy won't be able to inflict any damage.","Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).sfc","SFC","1.5","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","miximages/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","screenshots/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","titlescreens/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","3dboxes/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","backcovers/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png","marquees/snes/Ys IV - Mask of the Sun (English Translated by Aeon Genesis, Rev 2.1).png"
"snes_ys-v-kefin-the-lost-city-of-sand","Ys V: Kefin, The Lost City of Sand","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","2013-01-01","2013","2010s","Nihon Falcom","Nihon Falcom","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","70","3.5","Long, long ago, in the Kefin Desert, on the Continent of Afroka, a strong wind blew throughout the night... There is nothing but desert in Kefin area, but a legend tells a city was once was there -the city of Kefin. There is a rumor a legendary treasure is hidden somewhere amidst the ruins of the city. Who will dare to explore the dangerous region and to uncover the mysteries of the past? There is one who dares - Adol, the hero of the Ys saga. 

In this fifth installment of the series, the combat has been somewhat simplified to resemble a more classical action RPG style. Adol can fight monsters using melee and magical techniques, and defend himself by pressing the Y button at the right time. The ability to jump has been also added.","Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","miximages/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","screenshots/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","titlescreens/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","3dboxes/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","backcovers/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png","marquees/snes/Ys V - Ushinawareta Suna no Miyako Kefin (English Translated by Aeon Genesis).png"
"snes_yu-yu-hakusho","Yu Yu Hakusho","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-12-22","1993","1990s","Namco","Namco","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","YuYu Hakusho is a Super Famicom game based on the horror-themed manga and anime about a belligerent young man who dies unexpectedly from being hit by a car and is revived as a ""spirit detective"" by the rulers of the afterlife, and it becomes his job to investigate ghostly matters in the mortal world. The game is a series of set-piece battles where two opponents build up gauges using the D-pad and one of the face buttons: each combination leads to a separate attack and requires a different amount of build-up. The player can go through the story mode, which recounts events from the manga, or they can play a ""free"" mode against opponents of their choice.","Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","miximages/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png","marquees/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho (JP).png"
"snes_yu-yu-hakusho-tokubetsu-hen","Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-12-22","1994","1990s","Namco","Namco","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","YuYu Hakusho: Tokubetsu Hen (""YuYu Hakusho: Special Edition"") is a fighting game for the Super Famicom. Rather than controlling the character directly with the d-pad and buttons, the players charge attacks and unleash them at the right amount of power. Combat is depicted as a series of dramatic cutaways as each fighter launches their attacks and the other tries to defend or evade them.

YuYu Hakusho, an anime series about a teenage delinquent that is recruited by the underworld to fight demons and spirits that have escaped into the normal world, saw multiple adaptations for the Super Famicom and other consoles. Most are straightforward fighter games, though this and its predecessor YuYu Hakusho use this timing-based variant.","Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","miximages/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png","marquees/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho - Tokubetsu Hen (JP).png"
"snes_yu-yu-hakusho-2-kakutou-no-shou","Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou No Shou","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1994-06-10","1994","1990s","TOSE","Namco","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","YuYu Hakusho 2: Kakutou no Sho is the second of four Namco-developed Super Famicom adaptations of the YuYu Hakusho manga and anime, featuring the delinquent student Yusuke who becomes a spirit world investigator after he passes away. While its predecessor, YuYu Hakusho, featured a combat system that was turn-based and tactical, Kakutou no Sho is instead a standard example of the four-button fighter game model that became popular after the success of Capcom's Street Fighter II.","Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","miximages/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png","marquees/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho 2 - Kakutou no Shou (JP).png"
"snes_yu-yu-hakusho-final","Yu Yu Hakusho Final","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1995-03-24","1995","1990s","Namco","Namco","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","YuYu Hakusho Final: Makai Saikyou Retsuden (""Ghost Files Final: Strongest Demon Biographies"") is a fighting game for the Super Famicom and the fourth and final YuYu Hakusho game to be released for the system. As a fighting game, is has a lot in common with the second SFC YuYu Hakusho game - YuYu Hakusho 2: Kakutou no Sho - and the player can choose between thirteen characters from the anime/manga series, three of which are powered-up/demonic versions of other characters.

The chronology of the Story mode starts towards the end of the Black Chapter Saga, moving through the entirety of Saga of the Three Kings and concluding with a final boss fight with the villain of the 1994 movie, YuYu Hakusho the Movie: Poltergeist Report. The player can also compete in a Tournament mode or a two-player Vs. mode.","Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).sfc","SFC","3","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","miximages/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png","marquees/snes/Yu Yu Hakusho Final - Makai Saikyou Retsuden (JP).png"
"snes_zen-nippon-pro-wrestling","Zen Nippon Pro Wrestling","snes","Super Nintendo Entertainment System (SNES) / Super Famicom","SNES","Nintendo","4. kynslóð (16-bit)","1993-07-16","1993","1990s","Natsume","Masaya Games","Fighting","Fighting","1-2","1","2","80","4","Zen Nippon Pro Wrestling is a wrestling game based on the Zen-Nippon Pro Wrestling league and features the likeness of their most famous stars at the time.

Following the template laid by the classics of the genre, the game casts you as any of the 16 wrestling stars and then throws you to the ring to grapple the hell out of your opponent until one of you emerges victorious. You can attack your opponent with several moves (unique to each character) and upon ""locking-on"" by getting too close to the other wrestler you can execute grapples and throws.

The game uses 2D graphics from a heightened camera view and includes a plethora of game modes including single player Championship and Tournament modes (with single or Tag variants for both), 2 Player Vs., Team Battle, and a Training in which you can learn the basics of the game.","Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).sfc","SFC","2","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","miximages/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","screenshots/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","titlescreens/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","3dboxes/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","backcovers/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png","marquees/snes/Zen-Nihon Pro Wres (JP).png"
