"n64_zool-majuu-tsukai-densetsu","Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu","n64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo","5. kynslóð (64-bit)","1999-06-11","1999","1990s","Pandora Box","Imagineer","Role Playing Game","RPG","1","1","1","50","2.5","Zool: Majou Tsukai Densetsu (Translated ""Zool: Legend of the Monster Trainer"") is a role playing game for the Nintendo 64. It was released in 1999 in Japan only. This game is unrelated to the Amiga game Zool.  The gameplay is similar to Pokemon. It shares","Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).z64","Z64","12","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","false","covers/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","miximages/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","screenshots/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","titlescreens/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","3dboxes/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","backcovers/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png","marquees/n64/Zool - Majuu Tsukai Densetsu (JP).png"
"n64_007-the-world-is-not-enough","007 : The World is Not Enough","n64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo","5. kynslóð (64-bit)","2000-12-08","2000","2000s","Eurocom Developments","EA Games","Shooter","Shooter","1-4","1","4","80","4","The World Is Not Enough is a first-person shooter video game developed by Eurocom and based on the 1999 James Bond film of the same name. It was published by Electronic Arts and released for the Nintendo 64 on October 17, 2000, shortly before the release of its PlayStation counterpart. The game features a single-player campaign in which players assume the role of MI6 agent James Bond as he fights to stop a terrorist from triggering a nuclear meltdown in the waters of Istanbul. It includes a split-screen multiplayer mode where up to four players can compete in different types of deathmatch and objective-based games.","007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).z64","Z64","32","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","miximages/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","screenshots/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","titlescreens/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","3dboxes/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","backcovers/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png","marquees/n64/007 - The World is Not Enough (NA).png"
"n64_mario-kart-64","Mario Kart 64","n64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo","5. kynslóð (64-bit)","1997-06-24","1997","1990s","Nintendo","Nintendo","Racing, Driving","Racing","1-4","1","4","80","4","Mario Kart 64 races eight familiar faces, including Mario, Bowser and Yoshi, on four progressively tougher four-track circuits. Wide curves and gentle banks mark the Mushroom Cup tracks. Your commute gets notably tougher on the Flower Cup, though. The player will have to dodge semis rumbling on Toad's Turnpike and bounding boulders on fogbound Choco Mountain! Star Cup tracks range from the icy surfaces of Sherbet Land to the lava lakes of Bowser's Castle. The true test of karting competence, though, comes on the four tracks in Special Cup. Zip through the dark caves and green hillsides of Donkey Kong's Jungle Parkway, cling to the sheer cliffs of Yoshi Valley and hustle over haunted Banshee Boardwalk before tackling the longest track in the game: neon-lit Rainbow Road.","Mario Kart 64 (NA).z64","Z64","12","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","miximages/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","screenshots/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","titlescreens/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","3dboxes/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","backcovers/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png","marquees/n64/Mario Kart 64 (NA).png"
"n64_mario-tennis","Mario Tennis","n64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo 64","Nintendo","5. kynslóð (64-bit)","2000-11-03","2000","2000s","Camelot Software","Nintendo","Sports / Tennis","Sports","1-4","1","4","80","4","Choose your favourite Nintendo character and take to the world of tennis. Game types include ring shot (where you must shoot the ball through golden hoops that are suspended above the play area, gaining bonus points as you play), bomb tennis (simply put Tennis with Bombs) and Piranha Challenge (much like the tennis machines which chuck balls at you, but in this case they are replaced with Piranha plants). Of course, there is your normal tournament mode, with up to 4 players in on the action, and some crazy arenas, especially Bowser's Castle, which is suspended above a lava pit!","Mario Tennis (NA).z64","Z64","16","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","miximages/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","screenshots/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","titlescreens/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","3dboxes/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","backcovers/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png","marquees/n64/Mario Tennis (NA).png"
