"gba_fifa-football-2003","FIFA Football 2003","gba","Nintendo Game Boy Advance","Game Boy Advance","Nintendo","6. kynslóð (32-bit Handheld)","2002-11-19","2002","2000s","EA Sports","EA Sports","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","80","4","FIFA Football 2003, known as FIFA Soccer 2003 in North America, is an association football video game produced by Electronic Arts and released by EA Sports. It was released in 2002.

FIFA 2003 is the tenth game in the FIFA series and the seventh to be produced in 3D. A number of new features were added to improve upon the previous version. Club Championship Mode was introduced with the feature of playing against 17 of Europe's top clubs in their own stadiums and the fans singing their unique chants and songs. A TV-style broadcast package gives highlights at half-time and full-time, as well as comprehensive analysis. One of the most anticipated new features was EA Sport's ""Freestyle Control"" which allows the user to flick the ball on and lay it off to team mates. Other additions include greater likenesses of some of the more well-known players such as Thierry Henry and Ronaldinho, as well as realistic player responses.

The European cover features Roberto Carlos, Ryan Giggs, and Edgar Davids, representing Brazil, Manchester United and Juventus respectively. In the United States, Landon Donovan appeared in Roberto Carlos' place.","FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).gba","GBA","4","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","miximages/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","screenshots/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","titlescreens/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","3dboxes/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","backcovers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png","marquees/gba/FIFA Soccer 2003 (NA).png"
"gba_fifa-football-2004","FIFA Football 2004","gba","Nintendo Game Boy Advance","Game Boy Advance","Nintendo","6. kynslóð (32-bit Handheld)","2003-11-07","2003","2000s","Exient Entertainment","EA Sports","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","80","4","FIFA Soccer 2004 features eighteen leagues, with thirty-five national teams and over ten thousand players. Take them into a multi-season career, or individual tournaments or matches.nnnnLike EA's other 2004 games it has an in-depth career mode, where you must manage your budget and team. Promote players, demote players, and renegotiate contracts, all in an effort to get your team to the finals again and again.nnnnThe skills to master range from kicked passes and headers to lob passes and bicycle kicks. You can take control of players before the ball reaches them, which makes it easier to find space to take the ball. Like other 2004 titles, FIFA uses the right analog stick for it's Freestyle gameplay.","FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).gba","GBA","8","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","miximages/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","screenshots/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","titlescreens/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","3dboxes/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","backcovers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png","marquees/gba/FIFA Soccer 2004 (NA).png"
"gba_fifa-football-2005","FIFA Football 2005","gba","Nintendo Game Boy Advance","Game Boy Advance","Nintendo","6. kynslóð (32-bit Handheld)","2004-10-08","2004","2000s","EA Canada","EA Sports","Sports / Football (Soccer)","Sports","1-2","1","2","70","3.5","Released only a few months after Euro 2004, FIFA 2005 brings several features to the franchise, such as the return of a player creation tool, where a player can be created from scratch from customizing the face (while it's not possible to define the texture like in previous EA Sports games such as NHL 2000, the player can customize the facial features such as shape of the eyes or the cheeks, in a way similar to RPGs such as Neverwinter Nights), accessories and playing abilities. It's also possible to create all kinds of competitions. The Career mode was also extended, allowing now up to 15 seasons, and giving the player the chance to turn the tide of a simulated match anytime. This mode is a ""light"" managerial mode, where the player not only plays, but also signs new players and increase the clubs abilities in trainers, medical staff and other club staff).","FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).gba","GBA","8","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","true","covers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","miximages/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","screenshots/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","titlescreens/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","3dboxes/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","backcovers/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png","marquees/gba/FIFA Soccer 2005 (NA).png"
